- FROM THE JOHNSTONE BOOKS #johnstone #books

    - Impro 1979, Status chapter #book:impro #status
        - Nice and Nasty
            - Players: Whole class, working to the person beside them
            - Setup: None. Used before the word status is introduced, to dodge resistance.
            - Goal: Experience raising and lowering sensations without theory.
            - Rules
                - Say something nice to the person beside you.
                - Then say something nasty.
                - Notice the laughter and surprise when the effect lands wrong.
            - Coaching: Some people never really say anything nice and others never say anything really nasty, and they do not realise it. Experience first, discuss implications afterwards.
        - Hallo Milling with Eye Contact Rules
            - Players: Whole group
            - Setup: Open space
            - Goal: Feel dominance and submission through eye contact alone.
            - Rules
                - Mill about saying hallo to each other. The group feels awkward because nobody knows what status to play.
                - Assign half the group to hold every eye contact for a couple of seconds.
                - Assign the other half to make eye contact, break it, then immediately glance back for a moment.
                - Mill again. The group suddenly looks real: some dominant, some submissive.
            - Coaching: Holders report feeling powerful and visibly look it. Breakers-and-glancers feel feeble and look feeble. Students like doing this and are puzzled by the strength of the sensation.
        - The Er Experiment
            - Players: Teacher demonstrating, then class experimenting
            - Goal: Show how tiny vocal placement changes perceived status.
            - Rules
                - Speaker inserts a tentative short er at the start of each sentence: perceived helpless and weak, though listeners cannot say what changed.
                - Move the er into the middle of sentences: perceived a little stronger.
                - Make the er long and return it to the start: perceived more important, more confident.
                - Class tries it.
            - Variations: Videotape or mirror work for students who cannot hear themselves.
            - Coaching: Many people unconsciously resist the short er, substituting um or elongating the sound: they cling to self-importance. The short er invites interruption, the long er says do not interrupt me even though I have not thought what to say yet.
        - Still Head Speaking
            - Players: Any speaker, class observing
            - Setup: Mirror or videotape useful
            - Goal: Acquire the highest-value authority signal.
            - Rules
                - Speak while keeping the head absolutely still.
                - Notice how other high-status behaviours follow automatically: complete sentences, held eye contact, smooth movement, occupied space.
            - Variations: Practice in front of a mirror or on tape.
            - Coaching: Some find still-head speaking impossible, and some insist the head is still while jerking it about. Actors needing authority, tragic heroes, officers issuing commands, must learn it. You can waggle your head as the gravedigger, not as Hamlet.
        - Status Trick Conversations
            - Players: Pairs
            - Goal: Experience status changes through assigned physical tricks.
            - Rules
                - One moves very smoothly, the other jerkily.
                - One keeps putting his hands near his face while speaking, the other keeps hands away.
                - One holds toes pointing inward, the other sits back and spreads out.
            - Variations: Any body-behaviour contrast pair.
            - Coaching: Apparently unrelated things strongly influence each other: feet position changes sentence structure, hesitations, smiles, breathiness. If you speak with a still head you do many other high-status things automatically.
        - Minimal-Gap Status Scenes
            - Players: Pairs before the class
            - Goal: The founding exercise. Reproduce real-life moment-by-moment status adjustment.
            - Rules
                - Play scenes in which: both lower status, both raise status, one raises while the other lowers, or the status reverses during the scene.
                - Insist the gap be just a little above or below the partner's.
                - Grade transitions smoothly: if a photograph were taken every five seconds the prints should sort themselves into order by status shown.
            - Variations: Reversal scenes emphasised: easy in one jump, valuable graded delicately.
            - Coaching: Minimal gaps force you to really see your partner. Watching someone try to be high and fail is as delightful as watching success.
        - The Bystander
            - Players: 3
            - Setup: Restaurant or similar setting
            - Goal: Add texture to two-person status scenes.
            - Rules
                - Two players play a status scene, for instance quarrelling with the waiter.
                - The third is instructed to try not to get involved.
                - Play.
            - Coaching: The bystander's very subtle manoeuvrings are a delight to watch.
        - Status Sequence Runs
            - Players: Pairs through several scenes
            - Goal: Build confidence by chaining statuses across scenes.
            - Rules
                - Breakfast scene: husband plays low, wife high.
                - Office scene: husband plays high to a secretary who plays low.
                - Supper scene: both play low. Continue linking.
            - Variations: Any chain of contrasting configurations.
            - Coaching: Once the status becomes automatic, as in life, complex scenes can be improvised with no preparation. The exercises are crutches so instinctual systems can operate.
        - Entering the Wrong Room
            - Players: One student at a time
            - Setup: Real classroom door
            - Goal: Make a status transition physically real instead of acted.
            - Rules
                - Student leaves the room, then comes in through the real door acting entering the wrong room.
                - Coach rejects phoney solutions: entering head down, walking backwards, advancing two paces, act-seeing the audience, leaving phoney.
                - Ask for what we all do in life: the radar scans the new space for dangers.
                - Once real, set students to predetermine the direction of the status change.
            - Variations: Smiling with top teeth only helps a low entrance, turning your back on exit helps a high one. Videotape for difficult cases.
            - Coaching: You prepare a status for one situation and must alter it when confronted by the unexpected one.
        - The Governors Play
            - Players: 1, class watching
            - Setup: Invented at RADA to push students into emotional experience. Register and a pair of glasses as props.
            - Goal: Real emotion through status collapse, repeatable escalation.
            - Rules
                - Player enters late as a teacher carrying register and glasses, treats the class as his form: All right, quiet there, now then.
                - He puts the glasses on and sees not the class but a meeting of the school Governors.
                - Apologises, dropping in status frantically, struggles to the door.
                - The door sticks, wrestle for about ten seconds, huge status drop.
                - Exit, then optionally re-enter and replay: finding someone in the staff-room and chatting about tea, putting the glasses on again; visiting a psychiatrist; confessing to God.
            - Variations: Any profession and any wrong audience.
            - Coaching: Can turn people into crumbling wrecks in a short time, revelation for pretence-actors. Never push anyone into it, and the class must understand people are allowed to get upset without being called exhibitionistic.
        - Status in Gibberish
            - Players: Pairs or groups
            - Setup: After basic status exercises work
            - Goal: Prove that what is said matters less than the status played.
            - Rules: Repeat any status exercise in gibberish, for instance one high meeting one low and reversing status in an imaginary language.
            - Variations: All status exercises take gibberish well.
            - Coaching: Audiences laugh amazingly though nobody knows what is being said. The status reversal alone enthrals.
        - Every Status on a Fragment
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Short learned fragment: A is late, B has been waiting. A: Hallo. B: Hallo. A: Been waiting long? B: Ages.
            - Goal: Play any status on fixed words, separating text from transaction.
            - Rules
                - Both high: A strolls on with all the time in the world, Hallo as if not late at all; B holds eye contact,Hallo with emphasis.
                - Both low: A arrives running; B stands, bends head forward, low-status smile; breathless exchanges, anxiety, weak laugh on Ages.
                - Mixed configurations at will.
            - Variations: Any short fragment serves, office scenes such as Sir and Smith below.
            - Coaching: The implication of the words is that B lowers A, but any status can be played on them.
        - Sir and Smith
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Sir's office. Dialogue skeleton: Sir: Come in. Sit down Smith. You know why I sent for you? Smith: No, Sir. (newspaper pushed across desk) Smith: I was hoping you wouldn't see that. Sir: We can't employ anyone with a criminal record. Smith: Won't you reconsider? Sir: Good-bye, Smith. Smith: I never wanted your bloody job anyway.
            - Goal: Status against resistance, and status toward space.
            - Rules
                - Play the scene with Smith high: Won't you reconsider meets enormous resistance, gripping.
                - Play Sir's Good-bye, Smith low status.
                - Complication: Smith plays low status to the SPACE even when playing high to Sir, otherwise it looks like his office.
                - Sir plays high status to the space even when playing low to Smith, otherwise he looks like an intruder. Coach: Move about. Answer the telephone. Walk over to the window.
            - Variations: Any authority-interview skeleton.
            - Coaching: Status is played to anything, objects as well as people. A king may play low to a subject, but not to his palace.
        - Status to Objects
            - Players: 1
            - Setup: Bare stage with a bench
            - Goal: Solo status practice, environment as partner.
            - Rules
                - Actor waits onstage. Coach asks what status he is playing; he says he has not started.
                - Play low status to the bench: he glances around as if in a park he suspects is private.
                - Play low status to the pigeon: mimes feeding it, convincingly now.
                - More pigeons arrive, one lands on his arm, another shits on him; wipes the mess off surreptitiously.
            - Variations: Any environment, high or low to any object.
            - Coaching: He does not need another actor to play status scenes with, anything in the environment will do.
        - Non-Defence, Played Low
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Conflict scene: siblings sharing a flat, B enters asking about his letters, finds A opens them.
            - Goal: The best defence is none: total acceptance deflects aggression.
            - Rules
                - First run naturally: denials, escalation, near violence, stop the scene.
                - Restart: A admits everything while playing low status. B: Did you open my letter? A: Yes. B pauses: Yes? A: Yes. B: Well what did you do it for? A: I wanted to see what was inside.
                - B encouraged to be angry and close in; A keeps agreeing: You're right to be angry. I'm a shit. I always do it.
                - If shaken, A cries, nods, says You're right, you're right.
                - End: Well, don't open them again. A: I poke my nose into everything.
            - Variations: Any interrogation frame.
            - Coaching: The more A accepts B's dominance the more powerfully B is deflected, like the wolf exposing neck and underbelly. Players leap about with joy afterwards, controlling the attacker like a puppet.
        - Non-Defence, Played High
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Same letters scene, taught after low version is mastered
            - Goal: Maintain high status while making no verbal defence.
            - Rules
                - Same dialogue but calmly, sipping coffee as if no attack were being made: Yes... I open your letters. Dismissive.
                - Catch concealed low-status leakage: arm climbing to the back of the chair as if to flee, foot starting to tap.
                - Weight the situation heavily in A's favour: set it in A's house, B a guest who arrived late the night before, tasked with asking for the daughter's hand in marriage. Sample: You must be John... Cynthia tells me you want to marry her... Oh, by the way, a letter came for you this morning. It's been opened. I open everyone's letters... It's from your mother. Some of it I thought most unsuitable. You'll see I crossed some paragraphs out.
            - Variations: Any host-guest asymmetry.
            - Coaching: At first hardly anyone can carry it off, they pretend high but are crumbling. Reinforce the position until pure verbal non-defence sustains it.
        - Insult Lists and Shop Scenes
            - Players: Whole class divided in two halves, then pairs
            - Setup: Each half writes a list of insulting names: fool, slut, pig, arsehole, jerk, meatface, dumbhead, flatfoot, pigeyes, bugeyes, buckteeth, cowflop, monkeyface, swine, rathead. Only half the class knows who suggested each, and each suggestion carries the group's stamp of approval.
            - Goal: Liberate the class so status work gets easier. The insult is the verbal custard pie.
            - Rules
                - Set aside the lists. First play boringly literal shop scenes: Can I help you? I'd like a pair of shoes. Would these do? Another colour. Coach insists they buy something and sell something, no attempts to be interesting.
                - Hand out the lists: add ONE insult to the end of each sentence. Can I help you, fool? Yes, bugeyes! Delightful but boring.
                - Explain we want to see someone INSULTED: repeat every insult in disbelief and outrage. Why, I... Flat-footed pig yourself! Flat-footed pig! You call me a flat-footed pig, you... you... Arsehole! Buy something! I want a hat, buckteeth! Buckteeth! Try this for size, jerk!
            - Variations: Rotate the lists so terrible things get said to everybody; later give one actor a list and let the other invent his own; set the scenes among people, master-servant teams meeting; gibberish version repeating the last sound of each insult sentence.
            - Coaching: Insults must remain ornamentation, never become the scene itself. Bound people relax into physical freedom, gestures flow. Deeply releasing: status lowering so drastic and so pleasurable that ordinary status scenes hold fewer terrors.
        - Group Insult Approach
            - Players: Two groups
            - Setup: Groups at opposite sides of the room
            - Goal: Enormous energy release, collective permission.
            - Rules
                - Groups slowly approach each other, individuals running forward to hurl insults then dragged back by friends.
                - Target is the opposing GROUP, never individual members.
                - Teacher ensures every insult is received.
                - Usually ends with both groups face to face screaming, everyone having a very good time.
            - Variations: Repeat in mime. Makes a good aside exercise: whoever is insulted repeats the word to the audience.
            - Coaching: Useful even in schools with censored lists, or entirely in gibberish.
        - Space Streaming Demo
            - Players: 2 volunteers, class watching
            - Setup: Face to face about a foot apart
            - Goal: Make territorial space tangible.
            - Rules
                - Volunteers stand facing, close. Note the strong desire to change body position.
                - If they hold still, love or hate feelings arise as space streams into each other.
                - They will modify positions until space flows relatively unhindered, or step back to weaken the force.
            - Variations: Kneeling, bowing, prostrating demonstrated as ritualised low-status ways of shutting space off. Sergeant-major humiliation: recruit to attention, scream from an inch away.
            - Coaching: High-status players let their space flow into others, low-status players avoid letting it flow out.
        - Reading Posture Direction
            - Players: 1 demonstrator, class guessing
            - Setup: Neutral symmetrical seat on a bench
            - Goal: Show that trivial adjustments redirect all the space.
            - Rules
                - Sit neutrally, then cross left leg over right: space visibly flows to the right, the leg an aerofoil.
                - Rest the right arm along the bench back: space flows more strongly that way.
                - Turn the head right: practically all the space flows right. Anyone sitting neutrally in the beam reads lower status.
                - Cross left wrist over right: direction follows the top hand.
            - Variations: Any seated posture change.
            - Coaching: The class is amazed every time: the difference seems trivial yet the effect is strong.
        - Fear Crouch and Cherub Posture
            - Players: Demonstrated, then tried by all
            - Setup: Open space
            - Goal: Two opposite full-body status signatures.
            - Rules
                - Fear crouch: shoulders lift to protect the jugular, body curls forward to protect the underbelly.
                - Cherub posture: open all planes, head turns and tilts offering the neck, shoulders counter-turn to expose the chest, spine arches back and twists so pelvis opposes shoulders, exposing the underbelly. Sign of vulnerability and tenderness, powerful on onlookers.
            - Variations: Challenge drills: challenge a low-status player and watch the slide toward crouch; high-status players adopt cherub variants and, attacked, straighten but never crouch.
            - Coaching: High-status people often use versions of the cherub posture. Learners find both shapes startling to hold.
        - Street Approach Distances
            - Players: Pairs, working in public streets
            - Setup: Outdoor homework-style exercise
            - Goal: Calibrate real social distances against strangers.
            - Rules
                - Leaflet variant: hand out leaflets. You must establish you are giving leaflets and present one at the right moment, or passers-by ignore you or show alarm.
                - Recognition variant: one student recognises strangers, Hi! How are you? How's the family? Same old job then? The partner acts bored, Sorry? Come on, we'll be late, general impatience.
                - Debrief timing: nervous players mistime the initial approach and greet people sweeping past as if they did not exist.
            - Coaching: Great advantage: you cannot dismiss real people's reactions as untruthful.
        - Statue Study and Relocation
            - Players: 2 statues, rest study
            - Setup: Arrange two students in some spatial relationship
            - Goal: Expose unconscious mutual adjustment.
            - Rules
                - Others study the pair; many will not understand what holds the pose.
                - Lift the two statues, chairs and all, and place them on opposite sides of each other.
                - Compare: the space which seemed so natural now looks weird.
            - Variations: Freeze-tag style variants of the coach's invention.
            - Coaching: Everyone sees how carefully the pair adjusted their movements to fit each other.
        - Joining and Leaving Homework
            - Players: Solo observers
            - Setup: Cafes, bus stops, any real groups
            - Goal: Train perception of the kinetic adjustments around membership.
            - Rules
                - Watch groups of people.
                - Notice how everyone's attitude shifts when anyone joins or leaves.
                - Watch two people together, wait for one to leave, and watch the remaining person rearrange posture to express relationship to the people around him.
            - Coaching: Assigned as homework in the original teaching.
        - Master-Servant Foundation
            - Players: 2 or more
            - Setup: Any service setting
            - Goal: The defining rule of master-servant play.
            - Rules
                - Johnstone's law: both parties act as if ALL the space belonged to the master.
                - Servant's preferred position: the edge of the master's parabola of space. Crossing in front, shrink; behind the master, may be close and visibly higher; near a door so he can be dismissed instantly.
                - Mime dressing and undressing each other as master and servant, the clearest feedback on correct space.
                - Build scene structures: nice master with nasty servant, nasty master with nice servant, then the two households interrelating toward a duel, Commedia style, sometimes duelling piggyback.
            - Variations: Henpecked husbands and dominant wives, chauffeurs, any asymmetric pair.
            - Coaching: Trained servants can throttle the master while remaining visibly the servant. The audience delights in the contrast between status played between characters and status played to the space.
        - Keeping the Servant on the Hop
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Service scene
            - Goal: Teach acceptance plus deflection, the engine of compliant wit.
            - Rules
                - Master objects to everything the servant is, says or does.
                - Servant ACCEPTS the master's statement then deflects it. Why are you wearing that ridiculous uniform? It's my birthday, Sir. Correct.
                - Wrong answers reject the premise or blame the master: I'm not wearing a uniform, Sir, or You told me to, Sir. The master always boggles a moment at a correct reply, mind readjusting.
                - Coffee example: Where's the sugar? It's in, Sir. Correct: accepted that the master takes sugar and that none is visible. What about your diet, Sir? would be feeble.
            - Variations: Feeds the Making Faces master games in Impro for Storytellers.
            - Coaching: Recognition test: a correct reply makes the master boggle.
        - Servant in Trouble
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Service scene
            - Goal: Generate scene content from self-incrimination.
            - Rules
                - Servant volunteers disasters: Why are you wearing that uniform, Smith? I burned the other one, Sir. Where's the sugar? I've eaten the last lump, Sir.
                - Time-collapse variant: Good morning, Jenkins. I'm afraid it's not morning, Sir. I forgot to wake you. It's four o'clock in the afternoon. Don't you know what day it is? Your coronation, Sir.
                - Over-confession Jazzi, from Molire: Ah Perkins, I have a bone to pick with you! Not the rhubarb patch, Sir. ... the servant confesses goat, dog, poisoning, and the daughter, always one crime ahead of the accusation.
            - Variations: Any household or palace.
            - Coaching: Audiences laugh a lot at the over-confession pattern whatever the literary value.
        - Solo Master-Servant
            - Players: 1
            - Setup: Soft hat
            - Goal: Accelerate both roles in one player.
            - Rules
                - Wearing the hat he is the master.
                - Removes it and leaps into the position of the servant he was imagining.
                - The moment he cannot think what to say, change roles.
                - He may throttle himself, beat himself up, praise himself.
            - Coaching: Blocks far less than paired play, and the mind splits itself readily, as in Perls top-dog underdog work.
        - Mouth and Body Split
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: One actor speaks both parts, the other mouths the words supposed to be his
            - Goal: Sustain long master-servant scenes through shared control.
            - Rules
                - Voice actor runs both voices.
                - Mouthing actor starts passive; prod him to develop action: picking up a chair and threatening the master forces the master's next line.
                - Escalate: master beats the servant and supplies all screams and pleas himself.
            - Variations: Precursor of Lip-Sync in Impro for Storytellers.
            - Coaching: Sounds difficult, actually easier to sustain than normal two-voice scenes.
        - Numbered Pecking Orders
            - Players: 3 or more, numbered by rank
            - Setup: Numbers assigned instantly, any setting
            - Goal: Assemble hierarchies on the spur of the moment and cartoon real organisational life.
            - Rules
                - Orders and blame pass one way along the hierarchy, excuses and problems the other way.
                - Interact mainly with adjacent rank.
                - Balloon variant: give each actor a long balloon. If One hits Two, Two apologises and hits Three, down the line; the bottom rank ducks, cries, bites his lip or dies.
                - Faces: each person may make faces at anyone ABOVE him without getting caught; if One sees Three make a face at Two he informs Two.
            - Variations: Crowd scenes: numbering extras fixes the phoney spacing of most crowd work.
            - Coaching: Simple rules produce amazing permutations. Number One suppresses everything that irritates him; Number Four makes excuses, evades responsibility, invents problems to pass up the line.
        - Taking the Hat
            - Players: 4 numbered players
            - Setup: Soft trilby hats
            - Goal: Clown pecking-order chaos with total commitment.
            - Rules
                - Number One takes Number Two's hat and throws it at his feet. Two reacts with horror and embarrassment and shrieks for Number Three to pick it up and replace it. Two then takes Three's hat, and so on; Four must put his own hat on.
                - Then tell One he can take anybody's hat, Two anybody except One's, and so on.
                - Once learnt, let players weave about trying not to lose hats.
                - Hats MUST be thrown at the feet, never kicked or thrown away, which breaks the pattern.
                - Play scenes while continuing the insanity: burgling a house with people asleep upstairs, packing for holidays, meeting another pecking order also taking hats.
            - Coaching: Spectators collapse laughing. You cannot teach it unless you are yourself high and expressing great drive. Keep them high on the game and they use their bodies like excellent physical comedians, with no trace of self-consciousness.
        - Status Tower
            - Players: 2 or more entering in sequence
            - Setup: Bare stage
            - Goal: Naturalistic pecking-order building.
            - Rules
                - Someone begins with some low-status activity.
                - Each person entering plays a step higher.
                - Alternatively start at the top and add each person one step down.
            - Coaching: More naturalistic than the clown versions, good bridge to scripted work.
        - Maximum Gap: Finger-Snap Deaths
            - Players: Master plus successive servants, coach
            - Setup: Stage cleared for bodies. Master plays as high as possible, servants as low as possible.
            - Goal: Explore extreme status gaps and teach servants attention to the master.
            - Rules
                - The moment the master feels the slightest irritation he snaps his fingers: the servant commits suicide and a replacement enters.
                - Prod reluctant masters: the coach calls Kill him! until masters kill on their own impulse.
                - Ask the master to explain each death, stressing he need not be fair: waved arms, clumping, disrespect, misunderstanding requirements.
                - Then give each servant THREE lives, dying at the third snap. Watch them do exactly the same thing after a finger snap as before: Do something different, he's about to kill you again!
                - Once established, send in a third person who must placate the master and cope with the servants.
            - Variations: Direct precursor of the King Game in Impro for Storytellers, which formalises timing and competition.
            - Coaching: Servants survive seconds at first, minutes soon, and masters begin to feel pampered. Servants usually think the master harsh; audiences are amazed they survive so long.
        - Reversed-Expectation Status Scenes
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Roles implying fixed status, played inverted
            - Goal: Easy comic scenes from status-role mismatch.
            - Rules
                - Executioner playing as LOW as possible: too nervous to roll the last cigarette, apologises for the untidiness, asks the condemned for an autograph, accidentally shoots himself in the foot.
                - Ledge suicide playing HIGH: argues the rescuer into jumping off.
                - Bank scene: low-status robber, high-status cashier. Excuse me, Miss... Next cashier please, I'm going off duty... I've got a note. Here. Hand over the money? This is a stick-up! Not so loud... I shall have to refer this to Mr Carbuncle.
            - Variations: Any role pair with a built-in hierarchy.
            - Coaching: Very easy to create scenes this way. Maximum-gap transactions are the basis of absurd drama.

    - Impro 1979, Spontaneity chapter #book:impro #spontaneity
        - First Answer Drills
            - Players: Leader plus one student at a time, class watching
            - Goal: Expose and cure the rejection of first thoughts. "Teaching her to accept the first idea will make her seem far more inventive."
            - Rules: Fire quick prompts: "Say a word." "Tell me a colour." "Invent a name for a stone." "What's the opposite of 'starfish'?" After each answer, immediately ask "What was the first word you thought of?" and make the student say that one. His girl says "Pig" (first thought "Pea"), "Red" (first thought "Pink"), "Ground" (first thought "Pebble"). Another says "cabbage" while his lips formed an O shape: the real first answer was "Orange". On "What's the opposite of starfish?" the student gapes, Johnstone shouts "Answer, say it, I can see that he did think of it", out comes "Sunflower", and the student is amazed it was in him.
            - Variations: "Name some objects" as fast as possible. Repeating one word counts as original: "Pebble. Another pebble. A big pebble. A pebble with a hole in it."
            - Coaching: Ask immediately, first answers never reach long-term memory so later the student will deny substituting. Blocked on one word? Say it anyway. "Say whatever occurs to you. It doesn't have to be original."
        - Instant Emotion, Justify Afterwards ("Be Sad")
            - Players: Leader and one student
            - Goal: Show that behaviour comes first and the reason arrives automatically, so you need not "think up" motivation
            - Rules: "Just be sad. See what happens." Intensify: "Hold the face in a sad position, fight back the tears. Be unhappier. More. More. Now tell me why you're in this state?" Student: "My child has died." "Did you think that up?" "I just knew." Same with action: raise your arm, then supply why (strap in the Tube, waving, milking a giraffe, any reason is right).
            - Variations: Scene version: to "What are you doing here?" answer instantly without thinking, "I just came down to get the milk, Sir", "Oh Sir, don't put me in the refrigerator, Sir." Lost in a scene? Say "Oh my God! What's that?" and images jerk into the partner ("Mother!", "A secret staircase!").
            - Coaching: "Don't choose anything. Trust your mind. Take the first idea it gives you." Justify adjectives, don't avoid acting them.
        - Object Mime Drill (Shelf, Pocket, Box)
            - Players: One at a time before the class
            - Setup: Imaginary shelf, pocket, or box
            - Goal: Feel the difference between deciding first and letting the hand choose, since audiences delight in the second
            - Rules: First meeting: "mime taking a hat off", taking something off a shelf, out of a pocket. Leader deliberately doesn't watch, then explains the two modes: put the hand out and see what it closes on, or think first and then mime. Do both to sense the difference. Then run chains, changing the object each time. Filmed example: "Put your hand into an imaginary box. What do you take out?" "A cricket ball." "Take something else out." "Another cricket ball." "Unscrew it. What's inside?" "A medallion." "What's written on it?" "'Christmas 1948'." "Put both hands in." "A box." "What's written on it?" "'Export only.'" "Open it and take something out." "A pair of rubber corsets." "Far corners of the box." "Two lobsters." "Leave them. Take out a handful of something." "Dust." "Feel about in it." "A pearl." "Taste it." "Pear drops." "Take something off a shelf." "A shoe." "What size?" "Eleven." "Reach for something behind you." "A breast..."
            - Variations: Shelf, pocket, hat, box, behind you.
            - Coaching: Continually change the "set" (category) of the questions, that is what helps him fantasise. Run object after object until students stop bothering to think first. Watch for rejected first choices: a student mimes a book but wanted the tin of sardines, so make him take the sardines again. Expect the hand to pick up horse-turds, the audience prefers that to buckets and suitcases.
        - Yes Openings ("Your Name Smith?")
            - Players: Pairs
            - Goal: Feel naysaying versus yeasaying. "Those who say 'Yes' are rewarded by the adventures they have."
            - Rules: Play the interrogation twice. No version: "Your name Smith?" "No." "Are you Brown, then?" "Sorry." "Well, have you seen either of them?" "I'm afraid not." Total conflict, nothing develops. Yes version: "Your name Smith?" "Yes?" "You're the one who's been mucking about with my wife then?" "Very probably." "Take that, you swine." "Augh!"
            - Variations: High-status acceptance: "Is your name Smith?" "And what if it is?" "You've been making indecent suggestions to my wife." "I don't consider them indecent!"
            - Coaching: Low-status players tend to accept, high-status tend to block, but you can play high status and still yield. Even in a tremendous argument the actors are cooperating underneath.
        - Karno Inkwell Test
            - Players: Tester and candidate
            - Setup: Pen, empty inkwell
            - Goal: Detect who accepts offers (Fred Karno's hiring method for comics)
            - Rules: Poke the pen into the empty inkwell and pretend to flick ink at the candidate. If they mime being hit in the eye, hire them. If they look baffled and block, don't.
        - Working Someone
            - Players: Pairs, one driver and one assistant
            - Goal: Put all responsibility on one actor to calm anxious improvisers, since "his first skill lies in releasing his partner's imagination"
            - Rules: From Bill Gaskill: one actor owns the content and development, the partner merely assists and is constantly drawn in. Diver scene: "Have you got it?" "Here it is, Sir." "Well, unwrap it." "Here you are, Sir." "Well, help me put it on." "There, Sir. I think it's a good fit." "And the helmet." Ends with the assistant pumping air while the driver descends to a wreck, three tugs on the rope. Dentist scene: patient sits and opens his mouth, assistant catches on and mimes pumping the chair higher. "Having some trouble, Sir?" "Yes. It's one of these molars."
            - Variations: Any master and servant frame, interviewer and subject, customer and shopkeeper.
            - Coaching: The audience must never realise one actor is working the other, they should believe the dentist is running the scene. Don't think of getting the assistant to do things, think up ways of getting each other into trouble ("The regular dentist is on holiday, is he?" "You seem rather young." "Is it urgent?"). Anxious actors each try to carry the whole scene, assigning responsibility to one lets everyone work calmly.
        - "I Love You" Scenes
            - Players: Pairs
            - Goal: Produce interesting, unforced improvisation, because lovers almost always accept each other's ideas
            - Rules: Two students say "I love you" to each other and almost always accept each other's offers.
            - Coaching: Contrast with "start something" scenes where scared improvisers talk and block ("Nice day, isn't it." "Oh I don't think so."). Students don't realise when they block, stop the scene and explain, videotape replay makes it obvious. Transcribe the dialogue live and walk them through exactly what happened, then replay: the leg-amputation scene goes from dead ("You can't do that, Doctor." "Because I'm rather attached to it.") to alive once accepted ("It's the one you amputated last time, Doctor." "You mean you've got a pain in your wooden leg?"), and a collapsing chair becomes an offer ("My God! It's spreading to the furniture!").
        - Offer, Block, Accept (Programmed Variants)
            - Players: Pairs, teacher may referee and call categories
            - Setup: None, videotape useful
            - Goal: Give insight into the forces that shape scenes and why some partners feel impossible
            - Rules: Define terms: anything an actor does is an offer. A block wipes out the partner's premise or prevents action developing, antagonism is not blocking ("What if it is, you horrible little man!" and a servant's "No, Sir" both accept). Programmes to assign: (a) A offers and accepts, B offers and blocks: plumber scene, "You got a leak somewhere?" "Three feet of water in the basement." "Basement? You ain't got a basement." (b) Both block: "I'm here for my flying lesson." "In a bathing costume?" "I always wear a bathing costume." (c) A makes dull offers, B interesting ones: "Good morning!" "Great heavens! Frank! Did they let you out? Have you escaped?" "I hid in the laundry van... I see you've had the place redecorated." (d) Alternate offer and accept and the scene generates itself: yacht club, school reunion, "Pomeroy!" "Snodgrass!" "Butterfingers."
            - Variations: Request dull offers, interesting offers, "overaccept", "accept and block".
            - Coaching: Never think up an offer, assume one has already been made. Notice your partner is shorter: "Simpkins! Didn't I forbid you ever to be taller than me?" He sweats, fan yourself. He yawns: "Late, isn't it?" When the order to block comes from the teacher, morale doesn't collapse, which proves blocking is aggression. Accepted accidents become gifts, Stanislavsky berated an actor whose chair collapsed for breaking the fiction instead of continuing.
        - Two Places
            - Players: 2 or more
            - Setup: Bare stage
            - Goal: Comic scenes built on conflicting realities, exploiting blocking deliberately
            - Rules: One character plays, say, waiting at a bus stop while another claims the stage is his living-room. Both sustain their own place.
            - Coaching: From the Royal Court Writers' Group, circa 1959.
        - Presents
            - Players: Pairs, called A and B
            - Goal: Train overaccepting and generate warmth between players
            - Rules: A mimes giving a present, B receives it, B gives one back, and so on. Giver does not decide what the gift is: hold your hands out (about three feet apart signals something large) and let the receiver's hands close on whatever they choose. Receiver's job is to make the received thing as interesting as possible, to "overaccept": wind it up and let it walk about the floor, sit it on your arm and let it fly off after a small bird, put it on and turn into a gorilla.
            - Variations: Playing in gibberish helps.
            - Coaching: Concentrating on making your own gift interesting produces rivalry, concentrating on making the received gift interesting produces warmth. Expect strong resistance to being overwhelmed even by mimed gifts, coax the class over the hump, then "great joy and energy are released".
        - Blind Offers
            - Players: Pairs
            - Goal: Interact with zero intention to communicate, curing the sulks when partners "misunderstand"
            - Rules: A strikes a pose with no intention at all. B treats it as intentional and acts on it: photographs him. A says "Thank you." B stands on one leg, bends the other, A straddles the bent leg and "nails a horseshoe on it". B thanks him and lies on the ground, A mimes shovelling earth over him, B thanks him, and so on.
            - Variations: Once mastered, hold a completely different conversation while playing: "A touch of autumn in the air today, James," stretching a hand out. "Yes, it is a little brisk," peeling A's glove off. B lies on the floor. "Is the Mistress at home?" wiping his feet on B.
            - Coaching: Offer gestures that move away from the body, freeze in position until the partner reacts. Audiences laugh at every "Thank you" and watch fascinated, the effect looks telepathic.
        - It's Tuesday
            - Players: 2
            - Goal: Train overaccepting: an inconsequential remark must produce the maximum possible effect
            - Rules: A says something matter of fact: "It's Tuesday." B reacts at full intensity: "No... it can't be... It's the day predicted for my death by the old gypsy!" Turns white, clutches his throat, staggers into the audience, bangs his head, somersaults backwards and dies gasping "Feed the goldfish." A now overaccepts the goldfish remark: jealous grief, weeping on an audience member's knee, "Fifty years' supply of ants' eggs, and what did he leave to me, not a penny. I shall write to Mother." B recovers and overaccepts that: "Your mother! You mean Milly is still alive?" Plays passionate yearning until exhausted, then throws in a fresh ordinary remark ("Forgive me Jenkins, I got rather carried away") for A to detonate, perhaps a five-minute hate tirade.
            - Variations: Johnstone classifies it as a "make boring offers, and overaccept" game.
            - Coaching: "It doesn't matter how crummy the idea is, what matters is the intensity of the reaction." Three or four sentences can fill ten minutes and astonish an audience.
        - Yes, But..." and "Yes, And...
            - Players: Pairs
            - Goal: Demonstrate exactly how caution strangles spontaneity
            - Rules: A asks questions B can say Yes to, B replies "Yes, but..." and finishes however occurs. Bad way, think the reply up before speaking: "Excuse me, is that your dog?" "Yes, but I'm thinking of selling him." "Will you sell him to me?" "Yes, but he's expensive." Logical, unfunny, no one enjoys it. Good way, launch the "Yes, but..." enthusiastically the instant the question lands and complete it off the top of the head: "You took my money!" "Yes, but I've spent it." "You're a swine." "Yes, but everyone knows that."
            - Variations: Twin game "Yes, and..." is an accept-and-offer game.
            - Coaching: From Viola Spolin, Improvisation for the Theatre. Teach both ways deliberately, uptight people see precisely how cautious they normally are, and launching then improvising the completion is very funny.
        - Verse
            - Players: 2 or more plus leader, audience may feed lines
            - Goal: Force abandonment of conscious control, since improvised rhyme cannot be planned ahead
            - Rules: Leader opens in deliberately awful verse to lower the standard: "Tom and Else take your places, / A happy smile on your faces... We'll have Tom come in and propose / 'Cause Else's pregnant, I suppose..." Players improvise a scene in couplets, saying the line first and trusting luck for the rhyme. Stuck? Say "prompt" and the leader or audience shouts the next line. The verse commands the action and the teacher enforces it: "At last I've got you in my clutches: / I'll keep you here and take your crutches." Nobody mimes anything until Johnstone yells "Take the crutches!" The partner falls: "Oh, please Sir Jasper, let me go! / You must not treat a cripple so..." Back comes "I'll sacrifice you here in old Stonehenge," and again nothing happens until "Do it, it's what you said! Everyone wants to see her dead." Mid-couplet stall: "Lie down on yonder block and pray... prompt?" Audience: "I'll kill her at the break of day..."
            - Coaching: The worse the leader's model verse, the more encouraged the actors. If a player lands a genuinely witty couplet he dries up as his standard rises, so keep demanding he go wherever the verse drags him.
        - Say "Yes" Tape Game
            - Players: 1 actor plus a tape recorder
            - Setup: Prerecorded one-sided dialogue with pauses
            - Goal: Force total acceptance, the tape cannot adapt to you
            - Rules: Record a monologue with pauses for replies. In performance, an actor who has never heard the tape improvises against it. Sample tape: a voice from the footpath reveals it is an ant demanding to be picked up, announcing surrender ("We're fed up with being stepped on, bloody great things. Dictate your terms"), introducing Willy who climbs into your ear and blows paper bags against your eardrum, huge explosion, then "Get moving. Walk. Left, right, left, right..."
            - Coaching: You accept the tape or fail totally.
        - Gibberish Scenes and Impromptu Operas
            - Players: Groups
            - Goal: Prior freedom warm-ups before attempting verse
            - Rules: Only name-checked, no rules given: play whole scenes in gibberish, or improvise scenes as operas with sung dialogue.
            - Coaching: Students arrive turned off verse by school yet respecting it, these soften them up.

    - Impro 1979, Narrative Skills chapter #book:impro #narrative
        - Yes/No/Maybe Story Invention
            - Players: 2, questioner and answerer
            - Setup: None, party game
            - Goal: Trick "I can't think up a story" people into composing one effortlessly, convinced it isn't theirs
            - Rules: Answerer pretends to have thought of a story but will only say "Yes", "No", or "Maybe", following a secret arbitrary rule (Johnstone's: Yes if the question ends in a vowel, No if a consonant, Maybe if it ends in Y). Questioner interrogates and a story assembles itself: people? No. Animals? No. Buildings? Yes. Insects? Yes. "Do the insects take over the world?" "Yes." They reign alone, eat everything, grow too big to fit back into the people's buildings, and destroy the world. End by revealing the questioner invented the whole thing.
            - Variations: Party version: lure someone into authoring something outrageous (a midget dentist sexually assaulting Siamese twins), let them accuse you of perversion, then triumphantly reveal they made it up. Faubion Bowers wrote the game up in Playboy.
            - Coaching: Bend the rule to protect morale: say Yes after two Nos in a row, or abandon it entirely if the questioner wilts. Watch the questioner recapitulate and link unprompted ("Do they start out as harmless?" betrays intent) and declare "Is that the end?" when the links close.
        - Free Associate, Then Connect
            - Players: Pairs, Actor A and Actor B
            - Goal: Split storytelling into two trainable skills: generating disconnected material, and reincorporating it
            - Rules: A narrates for thirty seconds supplying deliberately unconnected material. B continues for thirty seconds connecting everything. Examples: cold night, howling wolves, concert pianist, old lady shovelling snow, becomes her long-lost pianist son, wolves closing on the hall, fur sprouting. Lighthouse with a dried sea, desert tap dripping since the beginning of time, jungle hermit, joined when the hermit turns the tap on, the seas refill, the lighthouse keeper keeps his picture. Cave of bicycle parts, smoke signals, a sonic boom, resolved as children finding Daddy has built a flying-machine and pedalling it round the valley all day.
            - Variations: Solo stage: leader says "Free-associate", lets them run, then says "Connect" or "Reincorporate". Bedtime co-telling with a child: ask questions, remember shelved elements and bring them back (Mr Elephant's leftover trunk-water squirts the woodcutter).
            - Coaching: If A tries to make it "easy" for B by joining things up himself it backfires, B has nothing to do ("She's joined it all up herself"). Writers get a lifelong tool: when stuck, look back at what you shelved instead of searching forwards.
        - Censor Overload Tricks
            - Players: Solo
            - Setup: Paper and pencil
            - Goal: Bypass the censor by distracting or overloading him
            - Rules: (a) Write a paragraph without premeditation while counting backwards aloud from a hundred. Johnstone's attempt began: "Extra. I fall through the first storey of the car park. The driver throughout the night thought the soft concrete slit his genitals thoughtfully. Nurse Grimshaw fell further..." He quit at sixty. (b) Draw with both hands at once, keeping attention equally divided rather than switching hand to hand, blank mind, draw as fast as possible, never deciding the subject.
            - Coaching: Feels like writing after concussion. Two-handed drawing regresses the mind to about age five, and curiously both hands draw with equal skill.
        - Lists
            - Players: Solo or group
            - Goal: Stop caring what comes out of your mind
            - Rules: "Name me a list of objects, but as quick as you can." Two modes: rational jumps, dog, cat, milk, saucer, spoon, fork, or the non-associative list produced at top speed: "Duck, rhomboid, platypus, elephant's egg, cactus, Johnnie Ray, clock face, East Acton..." and "Dead nun, postbox, cat-o-nine-tails, cement hopper, mouse-juice, Pope Urban the Eighth, a blob, giant opera singer, piece of lettuce, a kazoo, a vivisected clown, a lump of interstellar dust..."
            - Variations: Typed as fast as possible rather than spoken.
            - Coaching: Nonsense is manufactured by scrambling and takes time, so speed defeats censorship. Sequential lists feel thought up, nonsequential ones arrive by themselves, like hypnagogic images. Students must be coaxed, they feel bombarded by someone else's thoughts. Create an environment where no one is punished for what emerges.
        - Associating Images
            - Players: Pairs or circle
            - Setup: None. Developed at the studio from word-association games
            - Goal: Let sudden juxtaposed images trigger unconscious narrative material
            - Rules: One person throws out an image suddenly, the partner answers with whatever image it triggers: "A lobster..." "...With a flower in its claw." "A torn photograph..." "...An empty room." "Basket of eggs..." "...Cement mixer."
            - Coaching: Content reads itself afterwards (lobster with flower as a symbol of insensitivity) but was unconscious at the moment of pairing, leave it that way.
        - Characters
            - Players: Groups of three
            - Goal: Build characters far past what any one member would produce, by genuine agreement
            - Rules: Invent a name, then agree on details: "Betty Plum." "Big breasts." "Yes. A barmaid." "Lives in a room with blue curtains." "A stuffed toy dog on the dresser..." "Which she keeps her nightdress in." "Nylon." Continue until the group knows who she lives with, her taste in music, her secret ambition, the sorrow in her life. Second example: "George Honeywell, keeps bees, smokes a pipe, married, was married, in love with the daughter of the tobacconist, wears a soft cap, he's a voyeur, likes dogs..."
            - Coaching: Real agreement, never compromise. The instant anyone disagrees, wipe the character out and start another. Groups rapidly learn to develop characters further in ways that satisfy everyone.
        - Automatic Reading and Automatic Writing
            - Players: Leader and volunteer, solo once learned
            - Setup: Imaginary bookshelf, later blank paper
            - Goal: Prove "there is something inside them besides themselves"
            - Rules: Reading version, elicit a poem: "Mime taking a book from a shelf." "What colour is it?" "Blue." "Did you think up the colour or see it?" "See it." "Open the flyleaf, the publisher?" "Hodson." Book title, author (Alexander Pope), page number (thirty-nine), "Find me a line of verse." "So that we..." "Seeing it or inventing it?" "Seeing it." "Next word." "It's blurred." "I've given you a magnifying-glass..." Continue until verses emerge. Handle resistance: "It's in Russian" gets "There's something written in English in the margin", "too tiny to read" gets "I'm shrinking you down to the size of the book". Writing version: stare at a blank sheet, "see" a word, write it exactly where you saw it, keep going.
            - Variations: Story-questioning cousin: begin a tale aloud, "Suddenly he hears a sound and turns to see something moving in a doorway... What is the man wearing?" The answers flash in unbidden: "A suit." "Striped." "A white dog."
            - Coaching: Stop if the person grows frightened (Johnstone halted after two verses at a public lecture). Push past the point where you feel impelled to stop, filling exercise books, the gap between what you'd choose and what emerges is the lesson.
        - Guided Dreams
            - Players: One dreamer lying down, one guide, later two dreamers together
            - Setup: Quiet room, dreamer on the floor, eyes closed, relaxed
            - Goal: Experience effortless, choiceless imagination, things seem to happen rather than be invented
            - Rules: Guide suggests a scene and steers by questions: "You're on a beach." "Is it sandy or stony?" "Did you think that up?" "No, I just knew." Tell him he's lain there a long time, then suggest movement: along the shore, past a cave, look in, wade, climb the cliffs. Sample full journey: swimming a mountain lake, catching one particular fish, trading it to three hooded figures for a stick that vanishes an oak tree, a path upward, crying on the left, a naked woman ringed by little men, rescuing her, sleeping in leaves, she steals the wand, points it, everything goes grey and wintry, and in the mist "A huge oak tree, and three hooded figures leaping about and shouting." Story complete by brilliant reincorporation, everyone rolls about laughing.
            - Variations: Two people journey together attempting the same fantasy. Advanced students may be held or cuddled by other students during the journey.
            - Coaching: Always announce elapsed time or he refuses to get up. Watch breathing, coax near threatening areas (enter the cave, swim) but never push, steer out of bad trips. If they weep, cuddle. Abreaction protocol: it's good for you, you'll feel marvellous in half an hour, it happens to everybody. If alarm escalates, open their eyes, rock them. The point is effortless imagining, not catharsis. The guide should enter something like the same trance, and phrase suggestions as questions.
        - Experts
            - Players: 2, interviewer and expert, audience optional
            - Setup: Mock television interview
            - Goal: Practise instant unhedged invention under pressure (adapted from Vakhtangov, who had his Turandot wise men silently solve problems like "How do you make a fly the size of an elephant?")
            - Rules: Interviewer sets impossible problems, beginning sentences without knowing the ending: "We are fortunate enough to have Professor Trout... who has just returned from Africa where he has been teaching hippopotamuses to..." Anything completes it: "do handstands", "yodel". The expert has agreed to answer as part of the game and must jump in: "How exactly did you teach them?" "It was the carrot and the whip really." "But what techniques?" "Well, I demonstrated the stitches. Then I gave them sharpened telegraph poles and about a mile of barbed wire... Leather pole-holders strapped on to the forearms, or, er, in common parlance, feet... You get 'em on to rope, and then string, and finally they'll be doing crochet." "Any examples of their work?" "I'm wearing it. Every article of my clothing was knitted by the Kilimanjaro Hippo Co-operative."
            - Variations: Interviewer refers to charts imagined on the wall and demands explanations. Demonstrations: "I believe you've brought some of your soloists with you this evening," and the expert says Yes, calling in the yodelling mushrooms or miming them out of his pocket.
            - Coaching: Hedging and waffling ("the Government has assembled a large number of these hippopotamuses...") is the failure mode, the interviewer's job is to hold the expert to the problem until he stops evading. Audiences hedge in life and deeply respect a performer who answers anything. Play Verbal Chase first.
        - Verbal Chase
            - Players: 2
            - Goal: Train questioners to change the "set" every time, jerking spontaneous answers out of the victim
            - Rules: Rapid question and answer, each question jumping category unpredictably: "Imagine a box." (victim expects "What's in it?") "Who put you in there?" "My father." "What have you got in there with you?" "A toilet." "What's written on the outside of the box?" "Ladies!" Long version: "Where are you?" "Here!" "You're not. Where are you?" "In a box." "Who put you there?" "Mummy." "She's not really your mummy. Who is she?" "She's my aunt." "What's her secret plan?" "To kill me." "What with?" "A knife." "She sticks the knife where?" "In my stomach." "Cuts it open and takes out a handful of papery..." "On the boxes is written...?" "Help!" "Who wrote it?" "I did." "Who's in the box, crawling out?" "A spider." "A spider marked...?" "SIXES." "The spider does what?" "It eats me." "Inside the spider you meet?" "My father." "Holding?" "An elephant." "By the..." "Tail!"
            - Variations: Street version: "You're in a street. What street are you in?" "Main street." "What's the shop?" "A fishmonger's." "What does the fishmonger point at you?" "A pistol." "What comes out?" "Vinegar."
            - Coaching: Answering is easy, asking is hard. Speed matters, questions slightly too fast for normal thought. Ignore content entirely however embarrassing (implied homosexuality, sexual knife imagery), just keep jerking answers out. The more insane the questions, the better. Failure mode: the questioner does all the work with closed statements ("You meet a giant." "Yes."), rephrase open-ended: "A giant does what to you?" An expert questioner becomes a better improviser.
        - Word at a Time (Circle Story)
            - Players: Circle of any size, whittled to two survivors
            - Setup: Group in a circle, optionally to a beat, optionally a darkened room with curtains pulled over the huddle
            - Goal: Experience a story told by an outside force, learn to wipe your own ideas out of the mind
            - Rules: Tell a story round the circle, one word per person, as fast as possible. Anyone who blocks is thrown out until only two remain. Example fragment: "We... Are... Going... Away... To... Explore... A... Giant!" Tougher version: each speaker points to whoever must say the next word, so nobody can anticipate their turn.
            - Variations: To a beat. Eyes shut, group pressed together in the centre (at RADA they pulled curtains over the whole group and lay "like a huge pudding"), afterwards walk about and observe perceptual changes, colours brighter, spaces resized, focus sharper. Grand finale version: the entire group speaks simultaneously, "Start with 'We' and all speak at the same time," the majority swamps the minority who go wrong.
            - Coaching: Trying to control the future only ruins it: every word added plants a hoped-for next word you must abandon. The game works only when relaxed, attentive, and unbothered about being "obvious". Not recommended for German speakers, verbs locked at the end wreck it.
        - Word at a Time Letters
            - Players: Groups of four
            - Setup: One player writes, everyone relaxes
            - Goal: Surrender of control in written form
            - Rules: Compose a letter word by word round the group, the writer records everything including blocks. Johnstone's demonstration with a blocked Eng. Lit. graduate: "89", "The Elms", her protest "I can't think of anything else. I've got a block" yields the word "Block", then "Jan/March", "Dear Henry", "I hope Mrs Exeter has been behaving well. Mum hopes that you will take off your bra... Yours sincerely, Arthur. PS I hate you."
            - Variations: Late-night marathon version: three students wrote fourteen letters over a couple of hours with beers, armour peeling letter by letter.
            - Coaching: Letters pass through four stages if uncensored: (1) cautious or nonsensical with concealed sexual references, (2) obscene and psychotic, (3) full of religious feeling, (4) vulnerability and loneliness. Work with the same group day after day. With children, never insist on leading, Johnstone's disturbed nine-year-old supplied nearly every word while Johnstone merely connected.
        - Acted Word-at-a-Time Stories
            - Players: Pairs before an audience, optionally a participating group
            - Setup: Bare stage, optionally audience-supplied titles
            - Goal: Fully embodied improvised narrative in deep sympathy
            - Rules: Pairs stand with arms round each other, say "We" not "I", present tense, alternating single words while acting everything out. Forbid adjectives and "But", players stall with them ("We... met... a... big... huge... terrifying... angry... black... monster... but... we... escaped"). Once basic technique is mastered, forbid escape altogether: "Kill it or be killed, or make friends with it, outwit it." Remind them there isn't really a monster, so being torn apart costs nothing. If eaten: "Go on, don't stop the game." They fight their way out of the monster's interior, or continue in heaven, find God missing and take over the place, arm-wrestle him. Titles come from the audience, two combined into one: "Dracula and the Bald Lighthouse Keeper", "Rin-Tin-Tin and the Fall of the Roman Empire".
            - Variations: One partner eyes closed while the other steers him clear of furniture. Both eyes shut, the group stands round protecting them, and if the group is warm it starts feeding the story: wind noises, flapping coats for draughts, forest rustlings, dictated encounters with animals and monsters, flying the storytellers, burying them in heaps of bodies, becoming spiders crawling over their skins. Plus the all-speak-at-once group version above.
            - Coaching: Action-avoiders produce "we-go-to-the-market-where-we-buy-bread-and-now-we-walk-to-the-beach": start them inside a womb, on another planet, or hunted for murder. With eyes shut everything hallucinates real, touch becomes forest, and the storytellers know what trees and animals are there. The group effect releases extraordinary, almost sinister energy.
        - Ordering Game
            - Players: 2 or more, rest watch
            - Setup: Bare room
            - Goal: Get actors used to commanding and obeying, exposure to an "audience" with no success or failure to worry about
            - Rules: Actor A issues flat commands, B obeys instantly: "Sit down. Stand up. Go to the wall. Yawn. Say 'I'm tired.' Look around. Walk to the door..." No narrative intended.
            - Coaching: Early studio game, later adapted into Playwriting.
        - Playwriting
            - Players: 3, two actors plus one "playwright", teacher coaching
            - Setup: Chairs and props as needed
            - Goal: Teach narrative composition live, and investigate exactly why the would-be writer blocks
            - Rules: The playwright dictates every word and action: "Dennis, sit on the chair, and look ill. Betty, say 'Are you feeling well?' Dennis, say 'No, could you get me a glass of water.' Betty, get Dennis a glass of water. Drink it, Dennis..." If the playwright blocks he says "prompt" and anyone supplies the next line. Three rules: (1) interrupt a routine, (2) keep the action onstage, never divert to events happening elsewhere or at another time, (3) don't cancel the story. Cancelled version: illness introduced, cured by water, gone. Corrected version: "Dennis, find that the water goes right through you, and is splashing on the floor under the chair. Betty, put the glass under the chair to catch it when it runs through again. Dennis, examine yourself to try to work out what happened..." Deflection corrected: his characters peer through a hole in the floor chatting about what's underneath, teacher says get the action back, "He tells them to tear up the floorboards, and the 'block' dissolves." Routine-breaking example: washing-up scene stalls, "Break the routine," a plate is broken on purpose, quarrel follows, they reassemble the plate, find a piece missing, investigate, find the hole.
            - Variations: Offstage-diversion fix demonstrated live: boy tells girl it's four o'clock, they drift into gossip about absent others, restart, teacher shouts "Say it's time to begin," they still drift, "Get a bucket," and suddenly it's a forced-feeding scene with a funnel, her swelling up, inspired.
            - Coaching: "Make up a story" paralyses people, "describe a routine and then interrupt it" presents no problem. As a story proceeds it establishes new routines which must in turn be broken. Audiences stay interested only while action advances between the actors, otherwise theatre decays into literature.
        - Routine Interruption and Prearranged Surprise
            - Players: Pairs
            - Goal: Generate narrative automatically, without inventing plots
            - Rules: (a) Agree a routine, washing up, mountaineering, walking through a forest, taking goodies to Grandma, then interrupt it stupidly: mountaineers find a crashed plane, or get snowed up and start eating each other. (b) Each actor privately prearranges something the partner cannot anticipate, sprung mid-scene: a couple going to bed, the husband suddenly turns boot fetishist, or the wife starts laughing hysterically, or finds she's growing feathers.
            - Coaching: "It doesn't matter how stupidly you interrupt a routine, you will be automatically creating a narrative, and people will listen." Shakespeare's proof: the clown crawls under the sheet beside Caliban, the very last sensible move, therefore the best. Update stale fairy-tale interruptions: the princess should become a frog herself, the knight should kill the virgin and deflower the dragon. Cancelling is the great talent-wrecker: a revenge-row scene felt empty until rewritten so the girl injects her ex with a syringe at the height of the row and locks herself in the bathroom.

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 4 Spontaneity #book:storytellers #spontaneity
        - Progressive Desensitization Drill
            - Players: Whole class, teacher-led
            - Setup: None physical. Applied across a teaching sequence toward material a group avoids.
            - Goal: Coax students into dangerous areas, taboo, personal, frightening material, without panic or retreat.
            - Rules
                - Build a hierarchy from least to most frightening, Wolpe's feather in a far country, then a stuffed bird, then a live bird in a cage.
                - Present the most harmless item first while students stay relaxed and amused.
                - Ascend one small step at a time.
                - If anyone trembles or panics the step was too steep: back off to something less scary and proceed again.
                - Sequence games so each slightly approaches the avoided area, using laughter to keep tension discharged.
            - Variations: His wish to feed beginners while they were improvising, food as relaxation anchor.
            - Coaching: Reassure and protect while coaxing. Establish craziness as a mark of sanity so students join the club instead of snapping back into numbness.
        - Paradoxical Practice, Rehearse the Symptom
            - Players: Whole class, pairs, or individuals
            - Setup: Identify a specific unwanted habit: blocking, killing ideas, approval-seeking.
            - Goal: Bring the fault under conscious control by performing it deliberately.
            - Rules
                - Before teaching acceptance instruct: Kill every idea you're offered. Have them do it enthusiastically.
                - Debrief the feeling of killing an idea.
                - Return to normal playing. Students now recognize negativity when it occurs accidentally.
            - Variations: Works for any symptom: deliberate waffling, deliberate refusal, deliberate showing-off.
            - Coaching: Doing it wrong puts everyone in a good mood. Risks are non-existent: students just become more objective and better able to modify behaviour.
        - Tug-o'-War
            - Players: Whole class in pairs, teacher demonstration, optional one-versus-all finale
            - Setup: Open space, rope entirely mimed
            - Goal: Expose inappropriate competitiveness. On stage we swim or sink together, and losing good-naturedly puts everyone in a good mood.
            - Rules
                - Everyone finds a partner. Mime picking up a rope, have a mimed tug-o'-war.
                - Teacher stares out the window so nobody can scan for approval. Listen: straining, gasping.
                - Stop after about thirty seconds. Nobody has lost, and some ropes have gotten longer.
                - Teacher and a hefty student do a real tug-o'-war: about two seconds. Ask why the mimed ones lasted nearly a minute with no winner.
                - Replay the mime: Do whatever you think will please your partners. If they want to lose, pull them. If they want to win, agree to be pulled. Observe the happy smile on the winners' faces.
                - Finale: a smallish student tugs against the whole class. Who would an audience want to win? The underdog jerks the rope and everyone falls into a giggling heap.
            - Variations: Warning tale from Stuttgart: two students tried to win at losing by charging forward and smashing bald heads together.
            - Coaching: Shout Give in! Lose! Be what your partner wants you to be. Unless you are willing to be changed you might as well be working alone.
        - Giving Presents
            - Players: Pairs, may rotate through several partners
            - Setup: Open space, mimed objects
            - Goal: Train defining and delighting in received offers. Foundation of listening.
            - Rules
                - Pairs mime giving presents to each other.
                - Receiver must be delighted: Oh that's just what I wanted!
                - Keep exchanging. Note identity assertion: the tough student gives a skull, a gun, or a sharpened bicycle chain; the sensitive one gives sonnets or a flower. Round two, receiver defines: 1. New partners. 2. Don't define the present you give. Define the present you receive, and make it something that you really want! Sample: I've got this for you. Oh! It's the coat I saw in the shop window. But I have a surprise for you! A mechanical bird! Does it sing? Press the button. What is it? It's so heavy! Open it and see! An inflatable Parthenon! I was saving up for one!
            - Variations: Suitable even for three-year-olds.
            - Coaching: Defining the present you receive gives the illusion of revealing less of yourself, so defences drop and presents get more varied. Defining the present you receive is the secret of being a good listener.
        - Was the Work Good?
            - Players: Pairs, debrief after any partnered scene
            - Setup: Post-scene question period
            - Goal: Move attention from self-evaluation to the partner's experience.
            - Rules
                - After a paired scene ask: Was the work good?
                - Expect confusion, then clarify: Your work is good if your partner enjoyed working with you!
                - During play keep checking on your partner to make sure they are having a good time.
            - Variations: Never praise a player whose partner looked bad.
            - Coaching: If you're inept and yet everyone wants you as their partner, you'll soon be one of the very best.
        - Playing Tag
            - Players: Whole group
            - Setup: Large grassy area preferred
            - Goal: Restore childhood pleasure in risking capture, surface avoidance behaviour.
            - Rules
                - Simply play tag.
                - Watch for players sprinting for the horizon to avoid being chased: trying to win by refusing to play.
                - Teach that players who risk being caught get the most pleasure.
                - If a hunter staggers toward exhaustion, enter the game yourself and allow yourself to be tagged.
            - Variations: Tag in Pairs: partners link arms and play joined; match pairs evenly. Extreme slow-motion tag: compulsive winners betray themselves by speeding up; use lapses to discuss inappropriate thinking.
            - Coaching: Good-natured students never let a hunter stagger into exhaustion.
        - Blame Me
            - Players: Teacher and class, triggered whenever any student fails
            - Setup: Ongoing class, any exercise
            - Goal: Remove fear of humiliation so even shy students volunteer.
            - Rules
                - Wait until someone fails visibly.
                - Immediately take the blame aloud: It's all my fault. I gave you the wrong game. I should have rescued you sooner! I'm so stupid!
                - Remind them you are supposed to be the expert, so it follows logically that you are at fault.
                - Give students the internal script: Keith got me up here, and he wasn't any help. What an idiot!
                - Offer alternative targets: blame the situation, the oversized class, the air-conditioning noise. Never blame yourself.
                - Keep doing it until they believe you mean it.
            - Variations: Any plausible external scapegoat invented on the spot.
            - Coaching: Once students believe it, volunteers come forward knowing they will not be humiliated.
        - Drop Dead, Keith
            - Players: Teacher plus any player who keeps checking for approval mid-scene
            - Setup: Scene work in progress
            - Goal: Eliminate split-second eye contacts to the director for reassurance.
            - Rules
                - Detect quick glances at you mid-scene.
                - Shout: Say, Drop dead, Keith!
                - The player is too worried about your opinion to insult you, so the eye contacts cease.
            - Variations: Redirect attention instead: run exercises in pairs with fresh partners each round.
            - Coaching: Only attempt with strong existing rapport.
        - Volunteers, and Failure
            - Players: Teacher plus class, two volunteers per demonstration
            - Setup: Start of any demo exercise
            - Goal: Normalize volunteering and teach that errors are the mechanism of learning.
            - Rules
                - Call out: Two volunteers! You and you!
                - Interrogate the picked ones: What's wrong? You looked the picture of health before I dragged you out! Explain that looking sick is a pre-emptive sympathy ploy worth extra credit.
                - If nobody volunteers name the stall: They're waiting to see if I'm going to ask them to do something difficult. Advise: volunteer with a blank mind and see what happens.
                - Teach learning by falling off: stilts, bikes on grass, juggling facing a bed.
                - Tell the Gallway tennis story: the student calls where each ball lands until accuracy arrives once she stops flinching from her errors.
                - Cite Nicolaides: The sooner you make your first five thousand mistakes the sooner you will be able to correct them.
                - Push the shy: shy people should volunteer first, the longer you delay the greater the chance someone else does what you planned.
                - Frame hard games as advanced and predict failure in advance so students fail with honour.
            - Coaching: If you succeed brilliantly you'll depress the rest of us and we'll never get any volunteers.
        - When Students Fail
            - Players: Teacher plus the failing student
            - Setup: Immediately after visible struggle
            - Goal: Treat failure as survivable so tension drops instead of compounding.
            - Rules
                - Deadpan: You're not very good at this game. It'll take you twice as long to learn it! Failure framed as ordinary.
                - If failing continues: Excellent! We've found something that's really difficult for you!
                - Or run the interrogation: How many times have you played this game? Never! Are you supposed to be good at it? Not really. So why shouldn't you screw-up?
                - Announce precise mastery time while moving on: It'll take you twenty minutes to become proficient.
            - Coaching: The prediction is unverifiable since you move to another game, but it implies total confidence they will succeed.
        - Forcing Failure
            - Players: Technicians and Judges, players in earshot
            - Setup: Theatresports rehearsals and matches
            - Goal: Build boldness in support roles, normalize error.
            - Rules
                - Pestering speech to techies: No one's complaining about you! You're supposed to make several screw-ups per match. Theatresports is disposable theatre!
                - Techies assert themselves mid-show: door opens into a blast of organ music, stained-glass window fades in, love theme oozes in, moon appears, prop boat pushed on, lovers eloping across a lake that was never in their minds.
                - Abuse the Judges within players' earshot: Why so cautious? Get the scorecards up faster! Take risks!
            - Variations: Full unions of sound, light, action and scenography hijacking scenes into new stories.
            - Coaching: Timid technicians cannot create the magic. Abusing the Judges makes bad decisions hard for players to resent.
        - Be Average, Trying Demos
            - Players: Teacher plus one tense student, class watching
            - Setup: Ordinary room, a chair to look at
            - Goal: Prove that trying degrades automatic skill, license students to be average.
            - Rules
                - Ask: Are you trying your best? Challenge it: gymnasts tried with all their might and fell off the bars.
                - Perception demo: Look at the chair. Now TRY to look at the chair. Did it help?
                - Motor demo: Touch your nose. Now do it again but TRY to touch it.
                - Rigidity demo: Try to make your arm immovable, and it becomes easy for the coach to move because half its muscles assist.
                - Poll athletes: When was your fastest time? Were you trying hardest? Expect: funny you should ask, I really had no idea how fast I was going.
            - Variations: Plank image: anyone walks a plank, but over an abyss fear glues us to it. Treat the abyss as ordinary and cross in your average manner.
            - Coaching: We only try when we don't trust the forces within us.
        - Avoid the Letter S
            - Players: Two or more
            - Setup: Any conversation
            - Goal: Low-difficulty warm-up that makes players attend to their own words playfully.
            - Rules: The first player to use any word containing the letter s loses. Continue talking normally.
            - Variations: See Three-word and One-word Sentences in chapter 8.
            - Coaching: Used to warm up a lunch-time show team precisely because it is easy. His players later banned e, which caused long pauses and no trust.
        - Yes Instead of No, Parcel Drill
            - Players: Teacher plus one cautious beginner, or pairs
            - Setup: Any simple opening offer, typically involving a parcel
            - Goal: Break the reflex of rejection. Plain acceptance reads as talent.
            - Rules
                - Begin: Did you bring the parcel? Typical defensive reply: No. Flag it.
                - Retrain: whatever the impulse, answer yes. Did the parcel arrive? Yes, shall I open it? There seems to be something alive in it! The time-machine? It's out in the hall. They want it back by last Thursday.
                - Compare the two rounds aloud.
            - Variations: Generalize to any offer in any scene.
            - Coaching: It takes no more talent to accept the parcel than to reject it, yet the yes-sayer is instantly perceived as talented.
        - Disconnected Nouns Demo
            - Players: One speaker, rest observing
            - Goal: Demonstrate the social-self censorship that jams the imagination.
            - Rules
                - Shout disconnected nouns as loudly and quickly as possible: Cabbage! Match-box! Shoe-lace! Electron! Lithium!
                - Notice the stall around the fifth word when one feels too inexplicable.
                - Discuss the sensation.
            - Variations: Repeat with reassurance and laughter to extend the run.
            - Coaching: The jam proves the imagination is policed. Reframe: the imagination is a huge animal with a will of its own. Be interested in it, but accept no responsibility. You're not its keeper.
            - Notes: Three-word Sentences and One-word Sentences appear briefly in this chapter and fully under chapter 8 Being There, see those entries there.

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 7 Story Games #book:storytellers #narrative
        - Mega-Death
            - Players: Whole ensemble, unlimited
            - Setup: Empty stage
            - Goal: Get improvisers used to killing each other, played with elation
            - Rules
                - Enter and kill whoever is onstage within five to ten seconds or less.
                - Repeat until the stage is filled with bodies.
            - Coaching: Can be hysterically funny when played with elation
        - Word-At-A-Time
            - Players: 2 volunteers plus a leader (original demonstration form)
            - Setup: Volunteers sit beside the leader
            - Goal: Prevent anyone from thinking past the next word. Sentences must be grammatical and make sense
            - Rules
                - Leader opens: "Let's invent a story by adding a word each: Sally..."
                - Each player contributes one word in turn: "Was..." / "Going..." / "Mad..." (leader stirs things up here) / "Because..." / "Her..." / "Father..." / "Wanted..." / "To..." / "Put..." / "His..." / "Horse..." / "Into..." / "Her..." / "Stable..."
                - Continue until the story fizzles or completes
                - If you start "Henry..." planning "Henry was late for school", the next player may plan "Henry took a bath", the next "Henry took Betty to lunch", so by your turn Henry's lateness is gone forever
            - Variations: See Word-at-a-Time (in a Circle) below
            - Coaching: Some stories fizzle after one sentence, a few complete themselves, unusual early on
        - Word-At-A-Time (In A Circle)
            - Players: 6 to 8 in a circle
            - Setup: Circle formation
            - Goal: Fast, grammatical, meaningful word contributions
            - Rules
                - Group invents "stories" by adding a word each around the circle
                - Anyone who hesitates, is ungrammatical, or makes no sense is ejected
                - Ejectees become the Judges who say "Speed up!" and enforce the rules
            - Variations: 1. Group claps rhythmically, words synchronized to the beat. 2. Each player says a word then points to someone at random who must add the next word. 3. Letters, then stories, written by groups of preferably four students
            - Coaching: Judges police pace and correctness
        - Performance Word-AT-A-Time
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Players stay close together, acting out the story as they invent it
            - Goal: Physicalize a Word-at-a-Time story without degenerating into gossip
            - Rules
                - Players narrate and act simultaneously: "We are walking along the beach..."
                - Beginners are prompted with physical commands: "Walk forwards!" / "When we see a cave..." / "Go in!" / "We enter the cave and explore it..." / "Have flashlights!" / "Taking our flashlights we go along a dark passage..." / "Hear something." / "We hear someone singing..." / "See the person."
                - Once they have the hang of it, stop prompting and let them develop it alone
            - Variations: 1. If prompting stalls, ask players to add the word "suddenly". 2. One player plays with closed eyes. 3. Both close eyes while the class protects them: bird sounds in a meadow, tugging ankles for little people, flicked water drops plus wind hiss plus flapped coats for a storm
            - Coaching: Be protective. If the story takes over, players forget where they are, grab them before they sprint into brick walls. Excellent challenge, unlike most games. The Three Canadians of Loose Moose play it as street theatre. Closed eyes yield vivid inner reality
        - Avoiding The Wolf
            - Players: Any storytellers
            - Setup: Storytelling improvisation, solo or paired
            - Goal: Stop beginners dodging encounters with the "wolf", meaning anything frightening
            - Rules
                - Hero must interact with the wolf
                - Failure patterns: buying bread and going home ("the most unwolflike thing they could imagine"), meeting a gigantic walking suit of armour and fleeing ("We run out side and go home!")
                - Success pattern: "The monster paralysed us with its sting, and wrapped us in a cocoon of threads and began dragging us towards its lair..."
                - Teaching sequence: first ask students to meet a monster and escape from it
                - Then dare them to interact and either destroy it or be destroyed. If destroyed, continue in heaven or hell or inside the monster
            - Coaching: Tame it, ride it, make love to it, or be torn to pieces, but interact. The Senoi Indians, dream controllers, give the same advice. Apparent talent is willingness to accept the hero role
        - No Adjectives Or Buts
            - Players: Any Word-at-a-Time group
            - Setup: Applied once players are fluent
            - Goal: Stop wimping by delaying the noun
            - Rules
                - Spot the wimp pattern: "Cliff took Betty into his long, big, grey, enormous, vibrating, picturesque..."
                - Forbid adjectives, or forbid "buts"
                - Ban cliches such as finding treasure
            - Coaching: Only impose when fluency exists
        - What Comes Next?
            - Players: 1 hero onstage, class or audience suggests
            - Setup: Single player centre stage. Instruction: "Make no decisions for yourself. Just ask, 'What comes next?' and then do what we tell you. If the scene is boring, blame us."
            - Goal: Expose the struggle between positive and negative suggestion, train acceptance, reveal defensive storytelling habits
            - Rules
                - Hero asks "What comes next?" and performs each answer literally
                - Early suggestions fool the improviser: "The phone rings", "You jump up and down!", "You play the trombone", "You squeeze a zit"
                - Coach interrupts: "Stop sidetracking. Allow one action to lead to another!"
                - Analysis moment: "How did the scene begin?" / "The phone rang." / "Well, the knock at the door is a rejection of the phone." / "So what do we do?" / "Answer the phone!"
                - Name the blocks as they appear. "The phone's stuck to its cradle!" is the genius of being dull: original, block, wimp, bridging, instant trouble, cancels action, negative, gag. "You have a conversation" wimps because no responsibility is taken for what's said. "A man bursts in the door with a gun!" is sidetracking and being original
                - Force positivity: mother-in-law suggestion is meant negatively, so demand positive next steps: "She's coming to see you", "Make yourself respectable!", "You go to the cupboard", "Take out a rifle" (negative-looking but not story killing), "You load it"
                - Enforce promises: loading the rifle promises a shooting. Students bridge wildly: "You shoot yourself in the foot!" / "No he doesn't!" / "The bullets are the wrong size!" / "No they aren't!" Coach: "Ninety-nine per cent of the audience expect him to shoot his mother-in-law. So shoot her. Stop trying to be creative!"
                - Sidetracks get vetoed: salesman at the door, alarm clock rings. "Just shoot her!" / "Bang! What comes next?" / "She shoots back!" Everyone laughs, story advances without cancelling the mother-in-law
                - Negativity diagnosis, Lingen example: "You stand up" / "You break your leg" / "You try to stand up again and your other leg breaks" / "You break your arm", stopped before teeth snap off on a medicine box. Verdict: "The story can't work because it's in a loop, and there's no platform, and it's insanely negative."
            - Variations: 1. Two-person Version: men control a man, women control a woman, someone shouts for the wrong sex, funnier if warned against. Try sibling rivalries, parent-child scenes, strangers meeting scenes 2. Fifty purely positive suggestions in a row, feel the wave of negativity mounting 3. Alternate positive with negative: "Tom falls out of the window" / "a beautiful nurse takes him home to bandage him" / "her boyfriend is enraged" / "Tom is a better fighter" 4. Heroes add some of their own ideas to the audience's (not for beginners) 5. Hero also asks "What do I feel?" or "What's ahead of me?" or "What's that over there?" 6. A presenter questions the audience about the hero: "What emotion does he have?", "Why does she want revenge?", "What secret is he hiding from her?"
            - Coaching: Return to this game like body-builders to weights. Opening suggestions like stuck-to-the-chair, run-around-the-chair, stand-on-the-chair are refusals to move the hero and go unjustified. Without analysis of these defensive strategies the game teaches relatively little
        - Don't Spin Your Wheels
            - Players: Small groups (class divided)
            - Setup: Groups play What Comes Next unsupervised
            - Goal: Kill dead stories instantly instead of grinding on
            - Rules
                - Play What Comes Next in small groups
                - The moment the story wipes-out, scrap it and start another
                - "If anyone feels that a story has screwed-up, kill it and start another!"
                - Optionally follow with Word-at-a-Time to practise abandoning ideas
            - Coaching: "Never persist if there's no joy!" Compare to Theatresports players dragging scenes hunting an ending laugh. Organized boredom trains students to continue uninteresting activity. Watch self-run groups and restart joyless ones. Asked "Are you enjoying this?" players say "No we're not!" sounding astonished, as if they hadn't realized
        - Using A Committee
            - Players: Committee of 3 or 4 volunteers controlling, performers being controlled, remaining class as audience
            - Setup: Committee seated in front of the class, facing the acting area
            - Goal: Dramatize the opposition between audience and performer
            - Rules
                - Committee controls the players
                - Rest of the class applauds suggestions they like, says "Mmmmmm" non-committally to adequate ones, goes "Ugh" at suggestions that thwart us
                - Sample run: "There's a knock at the door" (Mmmmmm) / "There's no one there" (boo loudly) / "There's a parcel on the welcome mat" (Applause!) / "It's another welcome mat" (Booooooooo!)
                - Committees turn devious, reducing interaction to hazards: woman at crocodile cage, "You notice a coin on the end of its snout!", keeper enters (Booooooo!)
                - Contrast target, an interactive girl-and-crocodile chain: crocodile watches you, reminds you of your old dad, you throw it a sandwich, it thanks you, you look for a loud-speaker or ventriloquist, "Yes, it's me, Jennifer!", "You can talk?", "Yes, but only you can hear me!", be fascinated by its eyes, climb the railing, it asks you to lie down beside it, feel trusting, "What do you want, Dad?", "It's about the will, Jennifer."
            - Variations: See Keep Your Promises. Skilled committees dared to be committees of one
            - Coaching: Keep changing committees. Students are astonished at the mental reversal between audience seat and committee seat. Prompt committees when stuck, repeat that storytelling is difficult, build an atmosphere where failure isn't punishing
        - Keep Your Promises
            - Players: Committee plus spectators, as above
            - Setup: Interrupt a committee-controlled run after a few suggestions
            - Goal: Focus the committee on promises so it stays inside the circle of probability
            - Rules
                - Committee feeds suggestions, e.g.: "You wake up in a tent" / "You hear drums" / "You're on safari"
                - Break in and ask spectators their expectations: "An attack by lions", "nature-photography", "encounters with ivory-poachers", "a lost tribe ruled by She-Who-Must-Be-Obeyed"
                - Feed this back so the committee meets the audience's needs rather than flailing outside the circle of probability
            - Coaching: Speeds the process of committee and audience becoming one
        - The Performer Becomes The Committee
            - Players: 1, or 2 for the two-character variant
            - Setup: Put someone centre-stage and ask, "Why don't you make the suggestions yourself?"
            - Goal: Diminish self-censorship by outsourcing responsibility to an internalized voice
            - Rules
                - "Invent a bossy, parental, contemptuous voice. It has to be very officious and objectionable."
                - Ask yourself "What Comes Next?" and let the voice abuse and order you about
                - Obey it. Sample: "Go to the fridge." / "Open the door, stupid!" / "Take out the sliced bread." / "Make some toast, of course!"
                - The player may struggle against the voice, but the voice always wins: "Kiss him!" / "Do I have to?" / "Stop arguing and kiss him."
            - Variations: Sweet voices, loving voices, coaxing voices, uncertain voices, frightened voices. Scenes where two characters each give themselves instructions
            - Coaching: Seems psychotic, but wholehearted players report reduced self-censorship, the voice taking responsibility for what they do
        - Paradoxical What Comes Next?
            - Players: 1 hero plus suggesters
            - Setup: Play What Comes Next deliberately as badly as possible
            - Goal: Insight into how stories are destroyed
            - Rules
                - Feed the hero suggestions, each demonstrating one failure mode, labelled aloud: [INSTANT TROUBLE] "The house explodes." [CANCELLING] "The house assembles itself again." [CANCELLING] "Wake up - it was all a dream!" [BEING ORIGINAL] "Your head disappears." [CANCELLING] "You grow a new one!" [SIDETRACKING] "A woman rushes in the door!" [CANCELLING] "She falls out of the window." [BEING NEGATIVE] "She breaks her leg." [GAGGING] "She makes no bones about it!" [JOINING] "You break your leg."
            - Variations: Same three as What Comes Next variants: heroes adding own ideas, extra hero questions, presenter questioning the audience
            - Coaching: Use as diagnostic anatomy of cancellation, sidetrack, gag, and joining
        - Non-Sequential Lists
            - Players: Solo drill, or group
            - Setup: None, just list nouns fast
            - Goal: Break automatic chaining and threaten automatic self-censorship, warming up for Link the Items
            - Rules
                - List nouns as quickly as possible
                - Sequential chains feel natural: "Cat, Dog, Kennel, House, Table, Chair, Sofa..."
                - Disconnected objects feel unnatural: "House, Waterfall, Mandrake-root, Grinling Gibbons, Wellington Boot, Forest Fire, Grandfather Clock, One Parsec, Margarine, Shirley Temple"
                - Expect sweating and mental skid: "what has Margarine got to do with Shirley Temple?"
            - Variations: See performance version
            - Coaching: The inability to censor such lists makes the games feel enticingly risky
        - Performance Non-Sequential Lists
            - Players: 1 performer, a timekeeper (usually the Commentator), spectators counting
            - Setup: Challenge format between teams
            - Goal: Name maximum disconnected objects in 20 seconds
            - Rules
                - Team A challenges: "We challenge you to see how many disconnected objects a player can name in twenty seconds." Team B: "We accept!"
                - Spectators count each noun in unison: "Cabbage!" / "ONE!" / "The North Wall of the Eiger!" / "TWO!" / "A twenty-dollar bill!" / "THREE!" / "A sleeping princess!" / "FOUR!" / "A... a... a... a castle!" everyone boos the perceived princess-castle link
                - Timekeeper reports elapsed time, next player comes centre-stage
                - After six or seven items most players contort as if wrestling invisible opponents and give up
            - Variations: Improvisers from both teams play together, shouting words alternately, trying to avoid connections
            - Coaching: Ensure enough laughter to dispel anxiety. Really a warm-up for Link the Items
        - Link The Items
            - Players: 2 (or demonstrated solo in writing)
            - Setup: Play Non-sequential Lists first
            - Goal: Connect disconnected events into coherent narrative
            - Rules
                - One player states disconnected events, the second connects them
                - Example pair 1: "John woke up one morning to find that the sky was filled with black clouds. He looked at the bookshelf and saw that his Bible was missing." becomes "Remembering the prophecy that a black cloud would annihilate wrong-doers, John ran to get his New Testament, but every Bible in the city had turned to ash and the smoke was blotting out the sun."
                - Example pair 2: "Father Christmas was angry because the elves were on strike. A whale was seen with two heads." becomes "'No more toys for earth-children, until they do something about the pollution!' said the Chief Elf."
                - Three-item version: "Susan was having trouble finishing her homework. Charley was battering a slot machine with his fist. A band of bagpipers began marching along the avenue." Linked: Charley forgot his ear-plugs, battered the ear-plug machine that swallowed his money, and the bagpipers' savage sound triggers his werewolf transformation, snarling in a Scottish accent, wolf-hair pushing out
            - Coaching: Model the connection aloud yourself before expecting it of students
        - Verbal Chase
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: One asks non-sequential questions, the other answers and links them
            - Goal: Justify arbitrary leaps in dialogue. Leads naturally to the Expert Game (p. 123) and the Boris Game
            - Rules
                - Questioner fires unrelated questions, answerer must connect them: "Why did you open the umbrella in the living room?" / "To bring bad luck!" / "And the egg whisk?" / "Suicide weapon!"
                - Longer run: "Where were you at ten minutes after midnight?" / "In bed." / "With what fruit?" / "A ugli-fruit." / "And the bananas?" / "They were for the chimp?" / "What's its T-shirt say?" / "Africa for the Africans." / "And the space-ship?" / "He left it double-parked."
                - Common fault, questioner doing all the work: "Double-parked in front of the Mayor's office?" / "Yes!" / "And the pig was in the back seat?" / "That's right!" / "Eating the new tax regulations?" / "So it was!"
            - Coaching: Watch for the questioner carrying everything. Gateway game to Expert and Boris
        - The Boris Game
            - Players: 3 roles: interrogator, victim, Boris (an invisible eight-foot thug, sometimes another player voicing screams)
            - Setup: Introduce as "advanced", putting students on their mettle and absolving screw-ups. Training wheels phase: interrogator strikes failures with an airship balloon, 2 to 2.5 feet long, several inches in diameter, not floppy animal balloons
            - Goal: Packaging for Verbal Chase. Arbitrary leaps must be justified instantly, training free association and self-incrimination
            - Rules
                - Interrogator asks disconnected questions, prisoner must make sense of them: "Ms Ferguson, is it?" / "Look, I just came in here to use the toilet facilities." / "May I ask why you were washing that money?" / "I was counting the notes and some fell in the washbasin."
                - Answers designed to dodge get punished: balloon strike, both players yelling loudly: "Auuughh! All right! I'll tell! I'll tell! I was trying to wash the blue dye off!" / "And the scorpions?" / "Er... they... er... Auuughh!" / "I threw them into the ticket-seller's booth so that I could snatch the money!"
                - Yelling and writhing in pain makes it easier to spew ideas from the unconscious. Forcing incriminating answers produces bizarre material
                - Remove the balloon, add invisible Boris. Interrogator: "Bring him in, Boris!" Howls of terror, eight-foot Boris forces Gunnar onstage, thumps him into a chair
                - Interrogator begins courteously: "Perhaps you can tell us where you were on Thursday the 16th, sir?" Unsatisfactory answer triggers: "Help him, Boris!" Boris twists nose or arm or hurls victim at ceiling
                - Acceptable answers restrain Boris: "Well?" / "I was - I was at home!" / "With a dead horse?" / "It was a surprise for the wife!" Katrina restrains Boris, continues
                - Both players track exactly where imaginary Boris stands at all times. If they stare differently, interrogator introduces Igor, Boris's ill-natured twin
                - Interrogator rejects answers that sidetrack, cancel, or seem too gaggy, shaping a narrative
            - Variations: The Classic Boris formal rules below. Settings once mastered: bowling alleys, royal bedrooms, dungeons below Swiss finishing schools
            - Coaching: Victims incriminate themselves so Boris won't abuse them, which is why beginners volunteer as Heroes resisting torture, nothing happens till they confess. Boris must terrify enough that victims admit absolutely anything. Weak interrogators micro-manage: "Pull his nose, Boris!", "Rip off his leg!", better to blind-offer "Help him, Boris!" and let victims choose torments. A University of Waterloo Boris broke a collarbone, ambulance called, generally screams beat gymnastics. Boris is invisible so cruelty is self-inflicted, letting audiences laugh unreservedly
        - The Classic Boris
            - Players: Interrogator, victim, invisible Boris
            - Setup: As Boris Game, formal rules as first devised
            - Goal: Full-length interrogation narrative, story over acrobatics
            - Rules
                - Ask Boris to bring in the victim, or just shout "Next!" Victim hurls herself onstage or pretends to be frog-marched, as if propelled by the eight-foot Boris
                - Start politely. Unsatisfactory answers earn "Help him, Boris!" or "Jog her memory, Boris!" Victim howls in fake agony, reserving gymnastics until later in the scene
                - At unpredictable intervals, perhaps just once, interrogator says "Let's recapitulate," reviews the material, adds information, tries to make sense of the rigmarole
                - Present the victim with an object, a handkerchief, a shoe, even an audience member: "Perhaps this will jog your memory!" Victim expresses appalled and guilty recognition, weaves the object into the story
                - Force some resolution and/or call in the next victim
                - Sample flow: club scene, punishment-collar batteries dead, "Club? I came in here to use the telephone!" / "Help him, Boris!" / "All right! I'm lying! I'm... I'm a new member!" / "Weren't you black-balled?" / "No! Yes, I was! I was!" ... "So how did you get a letter of invitation?" / "I forged it!" (good answer, incriminating) ... Flexie-Boy Printing Set childhood, Sammy the Goldfish educated with books against the bowl, migraine, vet flushed him, substitute fish claimed miracle cure, taught to yodel, honour place in the billiard-hall tank, then the handbill: "Marvo the Magician... And his yodelling goldfish!" ... "just who is this Marvo?" / "Me! It's me! What use denying it?" ... launderette underwear kept in chest of drawers, worn as part of the act
            - Variations: None beyond setting changes
            - Coaching: If the interrogator enjoys the victim's flights of fancy they are allowed to continue. Emphasis must be on story or the game has little sustaining power. Very exhilarating, like skiing in total darkness
            - Escape Hatches (for Boris): 1. Implicate the interrogator: "I gave the money to you!" / "Me?" / "You think I didn't recognize you behind that moustache?" 2. Implicate Boris: "Boris tossed me up!" / "I'm sorry Boris, I had to tell him." 3. Attack Boris and hurl him about, another player grabbing the mike to supply screams. 4. The Androcles hatch: realize you once removed a thorn from Boris's foot
        - Keyboard Game
            - Players: 1 typist plus actors who enact the story (they may add ideas)
            - Setup: Typist sits upstage and to the side, angled toward the audience but with a clear view of the action, typing at an imaginary keyboard and speaking the story aloud. Split-attention game, like the Hat-Game (p. 156)
            - Goal: Generate a fully acted story through spoken narration, sharing attention between typing and staging
            - Rules
                - Speak the story as you type. At first the keyboard takes most of your attention, soon you only indicate it occasionally
                - Title: usual to ask the audience, but you may just type whatever occurs, deleting and retyping if disliked. Reject sabotage "originalities" like The Hind-leg of the Drunk Porcupine. Never accept a genre or title that fails to inspire
                - Actors first repeat or mouth the typist's dialogue, later add their own ideas, but never take over entirely
                - Structural rules: place the hero in a stable environment; establish a purpose and/or tragic flaw; optional scene change (home, work, movie); hero interacts involving suffering and chaos; achieve an ending by reincorporating shelved material, trashing the hero or creating new stability
                - Worked example, Harold's Adventure: type "Harold woke up one morning feeling particularly well. The sunlight was streaming into his room..." A player becomes Harold, hears birdsong, says "What a great day! I think I'll phone in sick!" Implicit promises: day off plus adventure, so he phones the boss feigning illness
                - Type a tragic flaw: "Harold's overriding fear was his dread of losing control." Mirror check, neat clothes, buttons sewn tightly, correct hair, sighs happily. Suspense planted
                - Weather was stressed, so outdoor promise: "Harold decided to visit the zoo." A frightened typist cancels (stove left on), bridges (misses the bus), or sidetracks (remembered appointment)
                - At the zoo: "Harold found himself all alone in..." pick any area, "...in the Ape House." Interaction: "Harold stared at Guy, and Guy stared back." An improviser leaps on as the gorilla. Moving to another cage would deny the promised interaction, so break the routine: "Harold noticed a gleam of metal in the gorilla's fur."
                - Actors may take over: zip-fastener realized, acrobat unzips from the suit, love, jobs as gorilla impersonators. Spectators want more chaos given Harold's flaw, so "delete" the zip-fastener: gorilla extends a finger, Harold touches it, finds himself inside the cage, covered in fur, watching his own body beat its chest outside the bars, inhabited by the gorilla's mind. Orderly world becomes disorderly, promise kept
                - Endings via reincorporation: the boss arrives at the zoo (Harold lied about illness), abuses the gorilla/Harold, gets thrown about. Or gorilla becomes lumberjack, Harold famous as the gorilla making paper aeroplanes. Or Harold wakes, all a dream, gorilla enters with breakfast tray. Or supply the missing purpose: Harold decides to rescue Guy and return him to Africa, stolen zoo-keeper uniform, tailored parcel, "Today was the day when he would return Guy the Gorilla to the wild!"
            - Escape Hatches (when inspiration fails): 1. Bring the character to you: "Suddenly Harold heard typing. There was a small door low in the wall. He opened it and saw another gorilla typing away..." Then type "Harold stepped through the door and..." Harold can complain, recognize you as an old friend, criticize the story, or read it aloud from the screen. Another player wakes up in the morning, lights fade, ersatz circularity achieved 2. Forest hero finds you typing behind a rock, in a hut, inside a hollow tree, or tiny under a leaf 3. Characterize yourself: "A drunken degenerate was drooling over a keyboard...", "She peered into the kennel and saw Fido typing away...", a bee in his shirt pocket typing on a miniature computer 4. Type "Something unexpected happened!" and let Harold solve it 5. Ask the audience "What comes next?" 6. Substitution sign: horizontal flat hand on vertical hand making a T shape, someone replaces you 7. Pretend to delete the last few sentences, audiences love characters deleting themselves 8. Hero reads a letter, diary, book, or newspaper aloud, creep silently away, hero becomes storyteller, or fade lights 9. Enter the scene as a character, Harold's boss perhaps, Harold rushes to the keyboard, keep alternating 10. Remember something shelved earlier and feed it back in
            - Variations: Typed by Captain Ahab, by the wolf that ate Red Riding Hood, by a vengeful God. Genre request: film noir, science fiction, Western, genres don't wreck keyboard stories but never accept one that doesn't inspire. "Dear Diary": diary writing, suicide-note composition, Captain Kirk updating the Enterprise log
            - Coaching: Fear of blanking turns the game into an ordeal, one improviser needed "about a year" before daring it, having seen it performed without learning its escape hatches

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 8 Being There #book:storytellers #presence
        - Three-word Sentences
            - Players: 2+ scene partners
            - Setup: Ordinary two-person scene, except every sentence must be exactly three words.
            - Goal: Stop beginners leaving their mouths on 'automatic' while their minds gallop into the future. Improve rapport, prevent gags, calm panicky improvisers.
            - Rules
                - Every sentence must be exactly three words, complete, no trailing off.
                - Adjust phrasing to fit: instead of 'Come in!' say 'Come in please,' or 'That you, honey?'
                - Keep playing the scene under this constraint.
            - Coaching: The imposed slowness can be read as emotional involvement, use it. Gags become almost impossible.
        - One-word Sentences
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Two players have greeted each other and get stuck. Trainer supplies activities: 'Open a cupboard!', 'Take something out of your pocket and show it to him.'
            - Goal: Force non-verbal physical solutions instead of verbal ones.
            - Rules
                - Each spoken line is restricted to one word.
                - Meaning must be carried by mimed objects and actions.
                - His example: Eric opens the cupboard, takes a tray, sets it on the table. 'Brandy?' 'Please!' He pours two brandies. 'Cheers!' Stuck again, Renee mimes taking something from her pocket and shows Eric. He takes it gingerly, sights along the barrel. 'Fired?' he asks, sniffing it. 'Recently,' says Renee. He places it on the table. 'Husband?' She wants to say 'I shot him between the eyes' but manages 'Dead,' taking the gun back.
            - Variations: Coached Word Length (below).
            - Coaching: Coach until players cotton on, most improvisers default to verbal solutions. The resistance to speech makes them seem emotional.
        - Coached Word Length
            - Players: 2+, trainer shouting numbers
            - Setup: Ordinary scene. Trainer shouts a number of words instead of an emotion.
            - Goal: Stretch sentences on cue. His verdict: audience laughs and applauds, but nothing happens, so not recommended.
            - Rules
                - Trainer shouts e.g. 'FOUR!' Player: 'Can I help you?'
                - Trainer shouts 'TEN!' Other player: 'Perhaps you can assist me with a small problem . . . sir.'
            - Coaching: Johnstone advises against it.
        - The Hat-Game
            - Players: 2 per round, Judges, optional Commentator
            - Setup: Both wear easily removable hats. Trilby or fedora best, keep a dent in the top. Worn fairly level, not jammed over ears, not shading eyes. No large floppy hats, no peaked caps (fingers near eyes). Play a scene.
            - Goal: Secretly seize your partner's hat. Win if you take it cleanly, lose if your attempt fails or your own hat gets taken.
            - Rules
                - Play a scene with the secret intention of taking the partner's hat.
                - Hats are to be taken, not batted into the air. Knocking a hat off doesn't count. Unless you end the grab with the hat in your hand, you lose.
                - Once a grab is attempted the round is over, Judges decide what counts as a grab. Aggressor loses unless the hat is taken cleanly, so you can win by luring a failed attempt.
                - Never mention the hat: no hat-shop encounters, no 'I had a hat like that once'.
                - Beyond arm's length: coach calls 'Risk the hat!' If the scene needs bare heads (barber mimes sheeting you), remove the hat, fan yourself with it, scratch your head, and don't replace it until your partner moves away. Unnecessary removal: Judges say 'Keep the hat in play!'
                - Never manoeuvre where the hat can be taken at will. 50 per cent chance is exciting, 100 per cent boring.
                - Take it when you can: enjoying the scene while ignoring an available hat is bridging and wastes the game.
                - Hold your ground: better to lose the hat than retreat. If you move, move forward, walk into your opponent, tempt an ill-advised grab.
                - Split consciousness: part of the mind plays the scene, another part watches. Planners die: 'There's something on your shoe!' or the migraine excuse keeps experienced players alert. Grandma asked 'What year were you born?' retreats inward to subtract 163, and her hat is wafted gently away.
                - Scoring: usual challenge is best out of three, one-on-one, each hat three points. One-off games: Judges point at the winner, five points.
            - Teaching stages: 1. Student takes a hat slowly from a non-resisting head. From the side or above, not the front ('you don't want your little finger stuck up a nostril'). Speed up once they've got the distance. 2. One tries to take, the wearer protects by grabbing first or dodging. 3. Both wear hats, each takes the other's the instant opportunity appears. Add dialogue, move about: play scenes. 4. Add roles: nuns, brain surgeons, cans of baked beans, mice, politicians at an apathy conference. 5. A winning taker continues round after round, optionally with a Commentator narrating ('That's Karen's fourth consecutive hat . . .').
            - Variations: Giving the Hat: bare-headed players each hold a hat and try to place it on the partner. Original is funnier because the loser's status drops visibly. Bum Tag: touch the other's behind first to win; sitting down or backing to a wall earns 'Keep the buttocks in play!' Good mass warm-up. Blindfold Hat-Games: colleagues steer the blindfolded players, audiences love it, potentially dangerous, wear goggles, never beginners; street version barefoot among mouse-traps (The Three Canadians).
            - Improvements to reject: Whistle rules deciding when the hat may be taken achieve nothing. The 'West Coast' ban on grabbing your own hat arose from one brief Vancouver demo and spread worldwide, killing the game: ducking, weaving and grabbing your own first is the humour. Requiring the grab to be justified within the scene trains planning and made one group quit as boring.
            - Coaching: 'Look after your partner! Be gentle! Think quick and light, not heavy and strong.' Anxiety makes students inept. Detach ego: 'What's it matter? It's not even your hat! Take risks! Win or lose, either way we'll be entertained!' Losers must not be indifferent: look astonished, confused, or laugh at yourself, we love to see one person 'lowered' by another. If onlookers don't laugh when hats are grabbed, something is wrong. Gaskill found the game made written scenes so witty he thought he'd 'found the secret of clowning'. A Zen monk was amazed to hear it called mindfulness training.
        - Gaskill's Samurai Game
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Two players face each other, motionless. Invented around 1960 by William Gaskill to discourage planning, inspired by the Seven Samurai duel.
            - Goal: Touch the opponent before being touched, with zero visible preparation. Train body-decision over planning.
            - Rules
                - Neither moves a muscle until one, giving no signal, reaches out and touches the other, and wins.
                - If the touched player leaps back so contact isn't made, the attacker loses.
                - Any defensive or aggressive movement that fails restarts the game.
                - Hat version: both wear hats a couple of feet apart, either wins by taking the other's hat. No dodging, no feints.
                - If you feel yourself tensing, say 'Pax,' step back, begin the round again.
            - Variations: The hat-taking version above.
            - Coaching: The torso clamps up before the arm moves, and that tension alerts the partner (like the boxer who watched for his opponent's 'decision' and punched first). Interrupt interior dialogue ('Shall I take it now? Not yet! How about now?') by shouting 'Step back! Relax! Calm down!' or suggest repeating 'I want nothing!'. Beginners get hypnotized, eyes flashing 'HAT! HAT! HAT!'. Explain the timing: a spontaneous grab takes a third of a second, faster than the wearer's eye registering, processing and activating muscles. Played right you feel calm, because the body decided.
        - Grandmother's Footsteps
            - Players: Class plus one 'grandmother'
            - Setup: Alan faces the wall, the class stands at a starting line. Childhood game, also called Red Light Green Light or Statues.
            - Goal: Advance without being caught moving. Warm-up for Making Faces.
            - Rules
                - Class creeps towards Alan.
                - He looks round suddenly: anyone he sees moving returns to the starting line.
                - Whoever gets close enough to touch him, or the wall, ends the game.
            - Coaching: Use as warm-up before Making Faces.
        - Making Faces, Party Version
            - Players: Whole group
            - Setup: Group spreads out pretending to be at a party: recognize old friends, exchange gossip, circulate, find refreshments.
            - Goal: Make faces at others unseen while catching others making faces.
            - Rules
                - Try to make faces at each other without being caught.
                - Point your fingers and say 'bang' to shoot anyone you catch making a face at you.
                - If shot, you leave the game.
            - Variations: Johnstone always wanted to play it on a lawn with water-pistols.
            - Coaching: Keep the party frame alive.
        - Making Faces in Threes (Master and Two Servants)
            - Players: 3 seated, plus coach
            - Setup: Three plain chairs facing the audience, edges touching (overlapping space helps the comedy). Middle player is the master, outer two the servants. Invented because master-servant pairs failed: masters refused to look away, believing that won.
            - Goal: Servants make secret faces at the master, who tries to catch and fire them. Trains 'seeing' instead of 'planning'.
            - Rules
                - Master picks anything to be boss of: 'A hospital, a shop, an atomic-power station, a bakery, a school for detectives.' His example: 'All right, I'm in charge of a zoo!' Interact with both zoo-keepers, don't send them away.
                - Master drags ideas from the servants instead of searching inward: start sentences with 'What about . . .' plus an animal, 'What about the wolves? What about the otters?' Or demand they know her mind and hit them with a two-foot airship balloon if they fail (the Keep the Servant on the Hop Game). Sample: 'Torben! What was I about to say?' 'You were about to ask about the snakes, madam!' 'Have they been stretched?' 'Extensively, madam.' 'And the elephants?' 'We've emptied them, madam!' 'Those boys climbing the fence?' 'Eaten, madam.'
                - Master disciplines: make slouching Torben sit up, pull servants about, beat with the balloon. Servants look respectful and obedient. Pamela's smile: 'It's the sheer pleasure of being near you, madam!'
                - Once masters drag ideas and discipline freely: 'Fire any servants you catch making faces at you!' Or pass secret notes to the servants, so the master only cottons on when the audience howls.
                - Caught servants protest innocence but leave if the master insists. Half a dozen volunteers rush to replace a fired servant.
                - Advanced: servants wave to spectators, letting them know what a fool their master is, while the master doesn't know the audience exists.
            - Servant rules: Face only when unobserved, freeze mid-face if caught out. Respect the master's space: resist the urge to lean towards the neck, no distortions you can't reverse instantly, keep hands away from the boss's head. Increase risk but fight desperately to survive, don't get caught on purpose and don't compete for the ugliest face. Elegant play: surviving on a hair's breadth for many minutes.
            - Master rules: Suspicion dawns gradually, find it almost incredible a servant would be so disrespectful. Turn your head mid-phrase rather than finishing sentences before looking. Look directly at one or the other, peripheral-vision watching means servants can't transgress. Share the scene with both servants.
            - Variations: See Fives, Pecking Orders, and Pairs below.
            - Coaching: 'Look at the other servant! Share the scene!' 'Does Klaus seem odd to you? His position. Why does his mouth look frozen?' Klaus, caught mid-face: 'I'm trying to look intelligent, madam.' 'Well, it's not working!' Even if you miss the face, enforce respectful behaviour. Most actors try to impress the audience with face-making skill and get caught at once. Stop before the laughter exhausts itself, let everyone try master.
        - Making Faces in Fives
            - Players: 5 per row, arranged U-S-M-S-U
            - Setup: Add a chair to each end of the threes row. Master in the middle, servants either side, under-servants at the ends.
            - Goal: Speed up training and involve more people, adding a second layer of face-making.
            - Rules
                - Servants make faces at the master as before.
                - Under-servants make faces at the servants.
                - Master fires servants, servants fire under-servants.
                - Masters may report under-servants' misbehaviour to the servants, but seldom fire them directly.
                - When a servant is fired, the under-servant moves up one place.
                - Replacements always start at the U positions.
            - Variations: Pecking Orders (below).
            - Coaching: Same as threes.
        - Pecking Orders
            - Players: 3 in a row, U-S-M
            - Setup: Remove a servant and an under-servant from one side of the fives layout, leaving a one-two-three pecking order.
            - Goal: Run a status ladder while everyone makes faces secretly at their superiors.
            - Rules
                - Play a scenario: rob a bank, or arrive at the beach for a picnic.
                - Making Faces continues underneath throughout.
            - Coaching: Members of 'clown' pecking orders should stay close together so we see them as one organism.
        - Making Faces in Master-Servant Pairs
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: Two-player scene, one master, one servant. Problem: masters won't look away, not wanting to be the straight man.
            - Goal: Keep the game alive despite the master's reluctance.
            - Rules
                - Coach forces the master to look away constantly: 'Look away to see the time! Go to the cupboard. Look in a drawer. Drop something and bend to pick it up. Throw a dart and walk past the servant to retrieve it. Turn away and address someone in the audience. Go to the phone . . .'
                - Minimum one look-away every ten seconds, though they'd prefer every ninety.
                - Play to total insanity: if the master kills the servant and looks away, the corpse still makes faces, and if they meet in hell, the game continues.
            - Variants: Avoid the symmetrical challenge where both make faces at each other. That's joining and gives minimum results: the point is one person mocking another.
            - Coaching: Drive the look-aways hard until looking away is habitual.
        - The Vampire
            - Players: 2
            - Setup: One player is a vampire forced into ordinary social contact. His example: a vampire desperately needs to rent a basement apartment from an oblivious landlord.
            - Goal: Teach 'having a resistance': full contortion only when unwatched, instant normality when observed.
            - Rules
                - When the partner glances away: become totally vampire, contort the face, writhe, hiss, show teeth.
                - The moment the partner glances back: return instantly to normal.
                - Start determined not to bite anyone, then let the resolution crumble.
                - Sample scene: landlord turns away, full vampire. Turns back: 'Were you hissing?' 'Was I?' 'I thought I heard something.' 'My . . . my asthma.' You reel away fighting the urge to bite, landlord turns to find you clutching the door-frame: 'Very solidly built, this house.'
                - Late-stage escalation: jump on the landlord, stagger away burning with embarrassment, weep: 'Oh, God, I'm sorry!' 'All right! I'll lie down and you can drive a stake through me! What else is there to do? Go on, see if I care!' Bite while apologizing profusely: 'I'm bleeding! You bit me! You're mad!' 'Yes, and now there are two of us!'
            - Coaching: Imagine you're a vegetarian for whom biting would be disgusting, or a teacher frantic to keep her job under a suspicious headmaster's interrogation. Not a frequent performance piece, once a year perhaps, mainly a class game for resistance.
        - Leave for the Same Reason
            - Players: 3, seated side by side in a straight line, which deliberately magnifies the problem. Training game, not for performance.
            - Setup: All three must leave for the same reason. Invented because his first students would never leave before a scene ended.
            - Goal: Teach cooperation, acceptance of offers, and freedom from hunting 'original ideas'.
            - Rules
                - Spoken round: one declares a reason, 'It's hot here!', and all three exit because of the heat. Again: 'There are too many ants here!' and all exit to escape ants.
                - Silent round: no dialogue. Each watches the others, copies any small movement, amplifies it together, and the shared reason for leaving emerges from the accumulated behaviour.
                - Take any idea: 'Ideas are an ocean to splash about in. Don't be selective.'
                - Repeat until it seems nothing special.
            - His examples: Failure state: one extends a hand (rain), one scratches (fleas), one applauds (show over), nobody left for the same reason. Successes: nose-wrinkling leads to investigating a delicious smell, blinking to bright light or pollution, sighing to throwing earth in a grave because the funeral's over, a mimed giant fish dragging all three offstage, hair-adjusting becomes hair falling out in shreds, foot-tapping grows into tap-dancing off, coughing means gas masks and poisoned air, feeding a bird becomes hundreds of attacking birds. Gibberish version, or dialogue unrelated to the exit: 'Great game last night!' palm testing for rain, raincoat, umbrella, 'That last goal was spectacular.'
            - Variations: Gibberish speech, or dialogue contradicting the physical exit.
            - Coaching: 'It's no use staring straight ahead, you'll have to see what the others are doing!' 'See what's happening, and do what the others do.' Side-coach ignored offers instantly: 'Maria wrinkled her nose, wrinkle yours. He's moving his neck, move your neck. Now groan. Leave in agony needing medical treatment!' Repetition allowed: 'No one said you have to be original. If you leave for the same reason every time, we'll admire your good nature.'
        - Dubbing: One Voice
            - Players: Two or more groups, any size, larger is easier. Each group is one character.
            - Setup: Groups stand along opposite walls, packed close, checking each other constantly. A spin-off from Word-at-a-Time games. At a Svenborg conference each character had 250 people and worked flawlessly.
            - Goal: Improvise whole dialogue in unison, proving that being obvious is enough.
            - Rules
                - Everyone in a group speaks simultaneously as one character.
                - Say the obvious: original contributions get drowned out.
                - If a line comes out unintelligible, the other group says 'Whaaaat?'
            - Teaching sequence: 1. Kneel to diminish anxiety: 'Can you say what I say, as I say it?' Drawl 'Gooood mooorning' with exaggerated lip movements. Correct echoing to true synchrony. 2. Offer 'It's a . . .' and follow them through 'veeery niiiiceee daaaaay'. You are now following them. 3. Hand the lead around, push the speed, then remove leaders entirely: conversational speed is reachable. 4. Men on one wall, women on the other. Women become one teenage girl, men one teenage boy. Simple checks: 'Toodaay iiisss Friiidaaay!' 'At the Looose Mooose Theeeatre!' 5. Scene: strangers meet, agree to coffee. 'Haallooo therrrre!' 'Hooow arrre yoouuuu?' 'I'mmm fiiiineee!' Tease with unpredictable questions: 'My name is Ssuusshharrlleen!' 'Whaaaaat?' 'Charrrrrlene!' 'Hi, Charrrleeeene!' 6. Family version: ten people as father, ten as mother, ten as the son or daughter who borrowed the Mercedes until midnight and straggles home at four. Prompts: 'It's your fault!', 'You're too soft with that boy!', 'He doesn't take after my side of the family!' Groups swirl and hurl abuse. Fuel with 'Notice that he's drunk!' 'Tell your parents you've decided to be an actor!' 'Walk over and hit him!'
            - Variations: One Voice Parties in Pairs: pairs link arms or shoulder up, each pair one character, at a party, or doctor-patient, parent-child, strangers meeting.
            - Coaching: 'Don't lead! Contribute half a syllable, then wait to see where the group wants the sentence to go. If nothing happens, make a tentative sound.' Loud dominant speakers turn cooperation into obedience, stop them. Afterwards point out that nothing clever was said, yet almost every line drew laughs: people love the obvious, and undefended players look beautiful. The audience is delighted when a sentence collapses into unintelligibility, they don't want flawless experts. Eye contact is a learning-stage instruction only: glance occasionally later, stare only when in trouble.
        - The Professor
            - Players: One tightly packed group as the professor, chairman, audience asking questions.
            - Setup: Introduce the group as a professor with a few moments to answer questions on any topic.
            - Goal: Crowd-unison comedy question and answer.
            - Rules
                - Chairman asks at most one or two questions, then hands over to the audience.
                - Audience fires questions, group answers in one voice.
                - 'Why is the sky blue?' 'Becaaause it's the colour of God's pyyyjaaaamas!'
            - Coaching: Never let the audience choose the subject: people speaking en masse have a mental age of about three, so 'Ichthyology' and 'quantum physics' wrecked games. 'Keep looking at each other! Don't lead. Don't say anything clever. Be obvious. Remember you're all in the same boat!' Keep it short, end while spectators are hungry for more, weak Judges let it dribble on.
        - The Audience Speak
            - Players: Entire audience as one voice, players prompting.
            - Setup: Introduced after the improvisers play a short One Voice Game, so the audience has seen the principle. Born in Gothenburg with 'reserved' Swedes.
            - Goal: The whole theatre improvises in unison.
            - Rules
                - Ask the audience 'Can you speak in one voice?' Expect 'Yyesssss weeee cannnn!' A 'Noooo weeee caaaan't!' is equally an admission that they can, and they'll laugh thirty seconds at their own cleverness.
                - Build simple scenes where the audience plays one role.
            - His examples: Blind date: players cluster and mime knocking on the fourth wall. 'Aaaanyboooody hoooome?' 'Whoooo's there?' 'It's yooour bliiinnd daaaattee!' 'Goooo awaaaayyyyy!' 'Weee've brought whiiskyy!' 'Cooommmmee innnn!' In Copenhagen the date brought a huge present: 'Do you know what it is?' 'It's aaaa biiiccyyyccclleee!' Other frames: naughty child confessing, 'Whhaaat haaweee yoouu doonnee thiiiis tiimmee?', audience as God interviewing new arrivals in heaven, players issuing the audience a speeding ticket.
            - Variations: Any audience-as-character frame.
            - Coaching: Needs critical mass: worked on the main stage, died in the smaller space, because intellectuals feel obliged to say clever things and a big crowd drowns them out.
        - Lip-Sync
            - Players: 2, master speaks, servant mimes. Discovered while teaching ventriloquism and letting the dummy walk about.
            - Setup Stage One: Master picks her own voice and invents a quite different voice for the servant. Nobody leads: if the servant's mouth opens with no voice ready, he yawns or gulps down a passing fly.
            - Goal: One player supplies all dialogue, the other supplies body, training shared control and listening.
            - Rules Stage One
                - Master calls the servant: 'Angus! Get in here!' Servant replies in, say, Scottish, gesticulating but forgetting to move his lips.
                - Master switches to her own voice: 'Angus! How many times do I have to tell you not to talk without moving your lips?' Back to the Angus voice: 'I didn't want you to see my teeth, madam!' 'Are you wearing my dentures? Return them at once!'
                - Beginners get prompted line by line so the servant knows what's coming ('Call your servant in.' 'James!' 'Say in his accent: You wanted to see me, sir?'), and soon add phrases of their own as the trainer drops out.
                - Desynchronized lips: master inserts an 'err' before the servant's sentences.
                - Demonstrate, then class plays in pairs, swapping partners.
            - Rules Stage Two: Servant makes physical blind offers, seized by the throat the master must gasp 'Pay me the money you owe me!' Clutching her feet earns 'Forgive me, sir! I can't get the dog out of the lawnmower!' Control is now shared, but restrain yourself, don't swamp the master.
            - Variations: Two-way Lip-Sync: each dubs another's voice. Please don't, it was invented as an ending gag, and he's watched five or six players spend ten unintelligible minutes on it. If challenged, keep it short and insist on one-on-one. Lip-Sync from Offstage: offstage voices on microphones dub onstage bodies, starting with weeping, laughing, sighing, screaming before adding words, actors justify with blind offers, excellent for funny voices, or run it with each voice standing beside its body as a 'shadow'. With Audience Volunteers: actors provide the voices, coach volunteers to overdo the lips, voices force activity ('I'll get you a juice from the refrigerator', 'Shall I lie on the bed so you can examine me, Doctor?'), and a Judge hauls discourteous voices onstage to apologize. Variant: two players onstage with one volunteer, one player doing all three voices.
            - Coaching: Servants underdo lip movement to hide errors, make them overdo it, audiences enjoy wholehearted mistakes. If voice always leads lips or lips always lead voice, correct it: control must be shared. Scenes develop more easily this way than when both players speak.
        - Invisibility
            - Players: 2 or more
            - Setup: One player is endowed with invisibility and behaves normally (the straight man), the others live in a world where invisible people do not exist. His setup: Deborah sits on the sofa reading, Tom enters.
            - Goal: React spontaneously and believably to the impossible instead of thinking intellectually.
            - Rules
                - Endow partner or partners with invisibility, act out the consequences.
                - There are no invisible people in this world, so the seer must rationalize: insane, drugged, haunted, or tricked. An invisible hand grasping your arm feels like a stiff arm with inexplicable white indentations. An invisible collision feels like slipping or a heart attack. Sitting on an invisible sleeper gives inexplicable warmth and softness, suspension in mid-air, and the sleeper wakes paralysed.
                - Track by ear and touch: stare at the point where the voice stopped.
                - Establish familiarity early so changes stay minimal: 'Is that you Deborah? Oh, no! You're invisible again.'
                - Fear drives action: 'Don't stay if you are afraid. Run out of the door!' 'But then the scene will be over.' 'It's your house! You live here! You'd come back eventually.'
            - Variations: Each Partner Endows the Other: neither can see the other. Coach them to blunder into each other safely, gasping and screaming. Tom grabs a chair against the terror, Deborah strikes it to see what holds it up. Rule additions: don't mime objects, don't wear hats or nobody knows if the hat is invisible or floating.
            - Coaching: Prime them first: remember being alone at night when something crashed in the next room, imagine a coffee mug floating up or a book reading itself. Side-coach errors the instant they occur: 'You can hear where the voice is coming from!' 'Don't look at Deborah. Look at the book!' Beginners scream at doors opening, inappropriate. Two mutually invisible players who never touch and never leave are refusing to be altered, make them sit on each other and flee. 'Scream when she screams! The sound was right beside you!' Intellectual reactions are always too slow. Provide real doors: openable, slammable, boltable.
        - The Ghost Game
            - Players: 3 or 4 skilled players
            - Setup: Characters enter a deserted interior, haunted house or old temple. His framing: travelling to a summer house, the car breaks down far from anywhere at dusk, you see a house in the trees, it seems derelict but the electricity is on, there's no phone, so you bed down for the night.
            - Goal: Escalating mutual invisibility until nobody can see anybody, ending in collective panic.
            - Rules
                - Anyone leaving the stage plays the Invisibility Game on re-entry: first entry everything visible, every subsequent post-exit entry invisible.
                - Leaving together doesn't exempt you: anyone who leaves re-enters unable to see the others.
                - When all players are back onstage, none of them can see any of the others.
                - Endings: everyone screaming and hiding under cushions, thrashing at imaginary spirits, huddling in corners, or back at the car, now a wreck, peering in at their own dead bodies.
            - His example flow: Peggy fetches the cases from the car, re-enters blind, drops them, flees shrieking. Ron chases, returns instantly, stares around: 'She ran round to the back of the house . . . Oily?' Oily, unseen: 'I can't get this case unlocked. It's got her medication.' Ron stares straight at the case without seeing Oily: 'Augh!' Oily touches him: 'Ron?' 'Someone touched me!'
            - Variations: Suggested cast: a cub reporter, a sceptical scientist, the chairman of a Society for Paranormal Research, and a medium.
            - Coaching: Expect to explain the rules several times and still field 'But can we see each other?' Someone will leave and come back pretending to be invisible. Recommended for very occasional use, skilled players only in public.

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 9 Filler Games #book:storytellers #warmup
        - The Die Game
            - Players: Four, five or six improvisers facing the audience, plus a conductor; the audience plays too.
            - Setup: A stage-facing-the-audience arrangement and an agreed story title.
            - Goal: Warming up the spectators.
            - Rules
                - The conductor explains that any player pointed at will begin or continue a story, and that if he/she hesitates, is ungrammatical, or makes some other error, the audience should shout 'Die', forcing the miscreant to 'commit suicide'; the last one left 'alive' wins.
                - Rehearse the audience in shouting 'Die' until they agree to be really loud (shouting makes them more responsive).
                - Begin the story, with the conductor trying to throw the players by pointing at them unexpectedly and lingering on any individual who seems about to crack up.
                - Players who are to 'die' will often ask for an object to die with, e.g. 'Can you name an implement you might find in a kitchen?', someone shouts 'Cheese grater!', and the player cheese-grates himself or herself to death (the audience laughs at the stupidity); it's best to find your own way of dying, and if you're stuck perhaps the sound imp will provide a rapidly approaching train or whatever.
            - Variations: Genre variant: each player is given a genre such as sci-fi, religion, romance, Western, and the story passes between genres; Johnstone's sample runs sci-fi ('As the spaceship approached Beta Four there was no sign of life from the colonists. Captain Skoog would be glad to be rid of the cargo of...'), religious ('...monks who had come to set up a branch of their order. His...'), romance ('...handsome profile assumed a masterful severity, as Communications Officer Ulla-Karin failed to detect any answering transmission...'), Western ('...from the Crossed Tentacle ranch far below where Tex had just wrestled one of the local Xangs into submission. The branding-iron was hissing into its body armour as it stared skywards. Following its gaze, Tex saw the bright star hovering over the foothills and...'), religious again ('...all over the prairie the rough soma-chewing Xang-boys knelt in awe, believing the atomic fire in the sky to be the sign they had been...'), then a romance player dithers ('Er... er...') and the audience yells 'DIE!' with relish. Second variant: no genres are agreed on beforehand and the conductor shouts out a genre as he points; Johnstone wouldn't recommend this.
            - Coaching: Any game that gets the audience to yell loudly is useful, but Johnstone would hate to see the Die Game in show after show; use it as a warm-up game, not as a 'closer'.
        - Emotional Goals
            - Players: Start with a whole group, then pairs; works best with two players, or two players and a 'passenger' (add more and it's difficult to follow).
            - Setup: No props needed.
            - Goal: Training gradual emotional transitions ('gradualism'), modulating from one state to another in tiny steps.
            - Rules
                - Players start with one emotion (or 'condition') and modulate to another, for example sad to happy, proud to ashamed, silly to intelligent.
                - Begin by asking a group to move from, say, 'interest' to 'panic'; perhaps they're so engrossed watching creatures trapped in a rock pool that they become marooned by the incoming tide.
                - Try various emotions, stressing the need to make gradual transitions.
                - Then have pairs play the game.
                - Then have each player choose his/her own emotions: one may be heading from terror to lust, while another goes from panic to calm.
            - Coaching: Asked to change from 'anger' to 'affection', most students start mildly peeved and take fifteen seconds to get totally enraged, yet they're supposed to be heading towards affection, not away from it; advise them to begin with whatever intensity they can muster and start diminishing it immediately. Encourage transitions by tiny steps: if you photographed them at fifteen-second intervals you'd want to be able to shuffle the prints and then lay them out in the order in which they were taken. This concept of 'gradualism' is easier to grasp if they've already played the Tempo Game (p. 207). Changing violently is dramatic, but changing slowly and continuously can also grip the attention (study Garbo, Vanessa Redgrave, Brando, and similar great actors). Footnote: Emotional Goals are described by Viola Spolin in Improvisation for the Theatre.
        - Endowments
            - Players: One improviser sent out of earshot, plus scene partners who endow him/her, with suggestions from the audience.
            - Setup: Nothing physical; get a 'profession and two physical qualities' from the audience.
            - Goal: Communication through indirect endowment; useful in the early days 'while we were still flailing about'.
            - Rules
                - Send an improviser out of earshot and get a 'profession and two physical qualities' from the audience, perhaps a 'teacher' who is 'blind' and has 'a huge nose'.
                - Fetch the improviser back and play a scene in which his/her partners endow him with these items, using gibberish; alternatively play the scene in English while giving the information indirectly.
                - Sample English dialogue: 'Come in. Careful! Keep your head straight! I suppose you find the door a bit narrow!' / 'It's always nice to visit you little people!' (instead of 'a huge nose' they've conveyed 'gigantism', and the spectators laugh at the misunderstanding); 'I'll drop a peanut. Perhaps you'd like to pick it up? No need to bend down!' / 'I'm an elephant!' (boos and laughter); 'I've got a big nose!' (applause); 'I'm afraid I forgot my homework.' / 'Do you want to copy mine?' (laughter at the error); 'I'm a teacher with big nose!' (applause); 'Careful! There's a desk to your right.' / 'I'm blind!'
            - Variations: Variant to avoid: some players become 'crippled' to communicate a limp, or get hiccups to convey that their partner has indigestion; do this and endowments aren't worth playing. It's reasonable to reach for something on the ground and say, 'You dropped your white stick,' but it's moronic to signal the need for your partner to become 'blind' by becoming 'blind' yourself. Chain Endowments (not a game to see more than once): several players are sent out of earshot while the endowments are being decided, then 'A' endows 'qualities' on to 'B', who conveys his understanding (misunderstanding) to 'C', and so on; the information becomes increasingly garbled and the audience laugh.
            - Coaching: Johnstone prefers the gibberish version. Endowments guarantee a laugh, but it's like shooting fish in a barrel.
        - Freeze Games
            - Players: Two or more players start an activity; others rush in on the freeze.
            - Setup: No props needed.
            - Goal: Claimed to develop the players' imaginations; in practice it teaches players to be original, and to sidetrack and cancel.
            - Rules
                - The players start an activity, such as directing traffic.
                - Someone shouts 'Freeze!' and runs into the scene to change the activity into some unrelated but visually similar one.
                - Example sequence: two people are pushing a boulder; a freeze changes them into three sailors tossing about in a storm; the next freeze changes them into four downhill skiers. People laugh because they like transformations.
            - Coaching: These visual puns are among the most popular of all improvisation games because they almost always get a laugh and there's no risk that anything might happen; Johnstone saw excellent examples in workshops at Chicago's Second City, but many less destructive games develop imagination too. They were never played in his classes at the Studio, and never by the Theatre Machine, but you might get challenged to one, and any game can be a straw to grab at when you're desperate. Why train students to kill stories? Morphing from 'playing the accordion' to 'stretching chest-expanders' to 'firing an arrow' to being 'nailed on a cross' gets admiration, but it's like warming yourself by burning the floorboards. We need coherent sequences like 'firing an arrow', and 'killing an animal', and 'discovering a collar around its neck that says "Property of the Emperor"'.
        - Guess the Phrase
            - Players: One guesser (Martin in the example), scene partners, and the audience supplying the phrase.
            - Setup: Stage space for a run of short scenes; nothing else needed.
            - Goal: It improves communication skills.
            - Rules
                - Send Martin out of earshot and get the audience to agree on a well-known phrase, song title, or whatever.
                - He returns and takes part in scenes that attempt to convey the phrase to him; his partners are not allowed to mention any of the keywords in the title.
                - For 'birds of a feather, flock together': a few players flap imaginary wings and start discussing migration; Martin becomes a bird and they start billing and cooing together; he guesses 'The early bird gets the worm?' and everyone laughs or groans.
                - A new scene begins instantly, Martin shown around a baronial hall by the owner: 'Shot and mounted most of these birds myself. I've got a complete set of Darwin's finches in the conservatory...'; Martin guesses 'You've feathered your own nest?'.
                - A duck-hunting scene begins: 'Look at that! They're packed so close that I can shoot them all in one burst!'; Martin says hopefully, 'The early worm gets the bird?'.
                - A new scene establishes him as the newest member of an ornithology club; the players hand him binoculars: 'Amazing! Look how the grebes are avoiding the loons!'; Martin says, 'Birds of a feather flock together,' and everyone cheers.
                - Typical phrases: Let sleeping dogs lie; Love thy neighbour; Don't bite off more than you can chew; Don't judge a book by its cover; The gods help those who help themselves. Typical song titles: 'Like a Virgin'; 'Que sera sera'.
            - Coaching: Obscure proverbs or song titles are useless, but a well-known title like 'Like a Virgin' can suggest scenes in the back seat of a car, or human sacrifices. Each wrong guess is followed instantly by a fresh scene attacking the phrase from another angle.
        - The No 'S' Game
            - Players: Scene players; the audience listens for violations.
            - Setup: Agree on a forbidden letter before the scene.
            - Goal: Forces the players to attend to what they're saying; can also convince them of the value of failure.
            - Rules
                - The players must avoid words that use an agreed letter.
                - Don't select letters that are rarely used, and don't let beginners select vowels.
                - Play the scene; some players are so used to leaving their mouths on automatic that they'll begin by saying 'Please, sir...' or 'Sit down, Susan...' and lose instantly, to the delight of the spectators.
                - If the players are making no errors, tell them to speed up, because there's no point in playing this game unless someone loses.
            - Variations: Variant: replace anyone who uses a word with 'S' in it and let the scene continue. Destructive variant: each player is asked to avoid a different letter, even though the spectators have enough trouble listening for just one forbidden letter; an excellent example of the way simple games can be ruined by adding complexity.
            - Coaching: Johnstone invented the game mid-lecture when extemporizing ways to interfere with verbalization. The spectators are longing for a player to screw up so that they can laugh and feel superior, hence the lesson in the value of failure.
        - A Scene without
            - Players: Scene players, with the audience supplying the condition.
            - Setup: No props needed.
            - Goal: A filler scene game driven by audience suggestion.
            - Rules
                - Ask the audience to complete the phrase 'a scene without...'; for example: 'a scene without scruples', or 'without air', or 'without guilt', or 'without a friend', or 'without a brain', or 'without hope', or 'without talent'.
                - Play the scene under the suggested deprivation.
            - Coaching: It's okay to ask for suggestions here, because even the most malevolent audience member finds it difficult to be destructive in this context.
        - Sideways Scenes
            - Players: Two or more performers; optional 'handlers' recruited from the audience for held positions.
            - Setup: The rear of the stage treated as the floor and the 'fourth wall' between actors and audience treated as the ceiling; a desk and two chairs set on their sides with their 'feet' against the back wall (or rear curtain); optionally a Velcro-rigged mug, a bed or sofa stood upright against the rear wall, a fly costume.
            - Goal: Illusion play with redirected gravity; it dates from the class in which Johnstone set out to find every possible way a scene could be played (backward scenes were invented at the same time).
            - Rules
                - Pretend that gravity is pulling the players away from the audience instead of towards the centre of the earth, as though the audience was looking down through the ceiling of an office.
                - The boss lies on the floor with the top of his head towards the audience and his feet against the rear wall; he struggles out of his coat and 'hangs' it on an imaginary hook on the floor some five feet away from the rear wall.
                - He levers himself towards the desk, 'rowing' himself along with his lower arm while moving his feet backward and forward along the wall, which gives the illusion that he's walking; he 'sits' at the desk pressing his bottom against its sideways seat, takes papers out of the sideways drawer, and 'rests' his feet on the sideways desk.
                - Any doors in the back wall are treated as trap-doors: Perkins emerges wriggling on to the stage as if climbing up a ladder, holds the boss's coffee mug sideways, and 'walks' along the back wall to place the mug on the vertical surface of the sideways desk; either it stays (rigged with Velcro) or the boss berates Perkins when it crashes to the 'wall' (i.e. on to the stage floor): 'How dare you throw my favourite mug at the wall just because I halved your salary!'
                - If the boss is shot, he becomes dead by standing in some crooked position against the rear wall (perhaps the police will tape around his outline).
                - Fly scene example: Dennis Cahill 'buzzed about' wearing a fly costume and 'landed' on the 'ceiling' (the fourth wall): 'It's that confounded fly again.' / 'I'll get the spray from the cupboard, sir.' They sprayed him and he 'fell' away from the audience and crash-landed on to his attacker in terminal agony.
            - Variations: Try a levitation scene in which a guru edges towards the audience as if floating to the ceiling; or a spiderman scene; or present an interview with an acrobat; or stand a bed upright against the rear wall and have the players stand against it and hold the bedding up in front of them; or a love scene on a sofa stood on its end with the lovers held in position, which took about ten 'handlers' who rushed out from the audience but was worth the effort.
            - Coaching: Excellent for very occasional use. Sideways scenes can be hysterically funny, but Johnstone has never seen other groups use them; dirty floors may be one reason. His company washes the stage before each performance, but he remembers a space in New York's Bowery district where anything that touched the floor was soiled by an oily black paste.
        - Yes-But
            - Players: Two players: Kurt asks questions, Gloria answers.
            - Setup: No props needed.
            - Goal: Demonstrates the difference between intellectual and intuitive thinking; trains unchosen answers.
            - Rules
                - Kurt asks questions that can receive a 'yes' answer, and Gloria responds with sentences that begin with 'Yes, but...': 'Are you closed?' / 'Yes, but do come in!'; 'Are you the animal doctor?' / 'Yes, but I don't do snakes!'; 'I'm afraid this is an emergency - that lump is the professor.' / 'Yes, but...'. And so on.
                - One common way to end the game is by having both players use 'Yes, but...' sentences: 'You'll have to go in after him!' / 'Yes, but then I might be digested!'; 'Yes, but I'll get a rope to tie around you.'; 'Yes, but I'm no good at knots.'; 'Yes, but I was a boy scout!' You can usually fade the lights somewhere about here; keep such scenes short.
                - As a teaching demonstration, Johnstone first answers students' 'yes questions' while 'staying safe', thinking up answers before opening his mouth: 'How old are you?' / 'I'm sorry, I have to be able to give a "yes" answer.'; 'Are you a hundred years old?' / 'Yes, but I'm quite fit for my age.'; 'Do you read much?' / 'Yes, but mostly about current events.'; 'Are you interested in politics?' / 'Yes, but I'm not sure who to vote for.'
                - He then admits he's been playing safe, offers to be braver, and this time shouts 'Yes! But!' vigorously and loudly with no idea what will follow: 'Are you a hundred years old?' / 'Yes! But I feel like ninety-eight!'; 'Did you vote in the election.' / 'Yes! But if it made any difference they wouldn't let me!'; 'Have you ever run for parliament?' / 'Yes! But I kept running.'
            - Variations: After Yes-But try the more positive Yes-And.
            - Coaching: Deciding what to say before you speak leads to planned answers, but shouting 'Yes! But...' with a blank mind drags out 'unchosen' answers which are almost always more entertaining. If you use the game in performance, to add variety (why else?), keep it short. Source: learned from a spectator at a Theatre Machine performance who had studied Viola Spolin's Improvisation for the Theatre, a book describing work in the Compass Players and Second City tradition.

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 10 Procedures, offers and focus #book:storytellers #scene
        - Blind Offers
            - Players: two improvisers (in the model scene, Sir and Perkins)
            - Setup: bare stage
            - Goal: trains making offers that leave definitions open for the partner, and accepting the partner's definitions, instead of controlling the scene
            - Rules
                - An offer is anything you say or do, and offers can be interesting, dull, controlling, or blind.
                - Make a blind offer by withholding the definition: presenting a mimed object while saying "You forgot this yesterday" is a blind offer, while "You left your hat here" is a controlling offer.
                - Model scene: "Good morning, Perkins!" "[Blind offer] We're ready for you now, sir." "[Blindish offer] And the frog?" "It's had the injection, sir." Sir snaps his fingers (a blind offer) and Perkins mimes dressing him in heavy protective clothing. Sir splutters and Perkins mimes opening the face-plate. "There's no air in here, Perkins." "I'm sorry, sir. I'll go behind the protective wall and start pumping." "Very well!" Sir clumps toward the imaginary frog.
                - Whatever your partner's blind offer implies, accept it: Perkins decided on the spur of the moment to be a lab assistant and Sir accepted him as such. There was no frog until Sir said "And the frog?", and he could have said any other noun.
                - Notice that a blind offer such as "We are ready for you now, sir" has infinite possibilities: Perkins could mime sealing a frog in a flask and Sir could say "We've got you now, Hillary, and we're going to throw you in the sea, and God help any poor soul who rescues you!", or Sir could say "Thank you, Perkins. Are the candles lit?" "Yes, sir, and the congregation has sung the first hymn." "You know, this is the first time I've ever anointed anyone!"
            - Variations: Johnstone floats a possible new game: "the first person to make a controlling offer loses"
            - Coaching: Never to know the next step but to have the courage to take it is an exhilarating way to improvise, but beginners and frightened improvisers prefer controlling offers ("I've a present for you! It's for your birthday! It's a bottle of whisky! It's your favourite!"), which alienate the partner into furious blocking ("It's not my birthday! And I never drink alcohol! Who are you, anyway? How dare you burst in here!"). The blind-offer scenes were so coherent that the Stage newspaper accused the company of presenting rehearsed scenes under the guise of improvisation.
        - Justify the Gesture
            - Players: pairs, with the whole class alternating roles
            - Setup: open space
            - Goal: trains allowing the mind to work by itself, accepting the idea that presents itself rather than scouring for originality, which makes working together effortless
            - Rules
                - Stage one (deliberately "stupid"): "Ann, raise your arm. Brian, look at her for one second. Brian, return her 'to neutral'. Ann, shower him with gratitude." Then Brian makes an abstract gesture, perhaps putting his hand on the top of his head, and Ann looks at him for a moment, returns him to neutral, and is thanked profusely. All students try the procedure, alternating between making an abstract gesture and having the partner cancel it.
                - Stage two: play as before, or justify the gesture. If a finger is being pointed you could pretend to manicure it, if someone covers their ears you could mime turning down the stereo. Don't force ideas, just be mildly interested as to whether an idea presents itself or not.
                - If no idea comes, return your partner to neutral, because you get thanked anyway.
                - Thank each other more, we need enthusiasm. Sample exchanges: Ann extends a hand and Brian kisses it. Brian raises his arm and Ann strap-hangs on a train. Ann extends her arms, Brian pretends to photograph her, and she becomes a model and thanks him.
            - Coaching: Take the spectators' attitude: they are under no pressure to be right, so their imaginations work automatically, and an extended palm reads as catching a ball, testing for rain, or wanting a palm read. If hesitation comes from wanting to be original ("I had an impulse to lift a small bird from her finger but remembered someone had done that earlier"), insist: accept the idea that presents itself, we've only got two arms and two legs, and besides, it's not the same bird. Treat ideas as gifts showered on you, like the mise-en-scene that replaces itself page after page as you read a novel, so creativity becomes attending rather than thinking. Master this and cooperation is automatic: you point, and your servant shoots the bird you had no idea you were pointing at.
        - He Said/She Said
            - Players: pairs (introduced by the coach with two volunteers while the group watches)
            - Setup: open space
            - Goal: trains controlling your partner through narration, and being controlled, without erecting walls against intrusion (includes the follow-up section "Defences")
            - Rules
                - Tell your partner what to do, and then add a line of dialogue.
                - Model exchange: "I'm home!" "He said, throwing his satchel on to the table. 'How was school?'" "She said, picking it up and looking inside it. 'Mum! That's mine!'" "He said, trying to pull it away from me but failing miserably. 'I just want to see what you have for your homework.'" "She said, emptying it out on to the table. 'I finished my homework at school, Mum.'" "He said, giving me a phoney smile and edging towards the door. 'I talked to your teacher today.'" "She said, grabbing me by the neck. 'Whatever for?'" "He said, turning pale and refusing to look at me. 'You haven't been to school for a month. You told your teacher we had moved to another city!'"
                - Introduce it when students are in a good mood, explaining that it's easy for small children but difficult for adults, which arouses curiosity and lessens self-punishment. Lead volunteers through it, feeding the dialogue: 'Say "Hallo! Max!"' Linda says it. Tell Max: 'Just say "She said ..." then "Embracing me"', assembling "She said, embracing me." Linda embraces him saying "Nice to see you," and you correct: "Not yet. First he has a line of dialogue, then you tell him what to do."
                - Defences: players soon find subtle ways to minimize interaction, controlling from a distance ("He said, going to the door," "She said, climbing on a chair and flapping her arms"). Point this out, because it's more fun to work closely together: "You swine!" "She said, throwing me to the floor and kneeling on my chest. 'Augh! You're hurting me!'" "He said, loving it. 'It's your turn to wash the dishes!'" "She said, dragging me to the sink ..."
                - Watch for the common defences of describing what the partner is already doing ("He said, breathlessly"), giving yourself the stage directions ("He said, cowering away from me as I kicked the door shut and produced my amazing weapon"), or expanding dialogue into whole paragraphs so the partner can't control you. Prevent paragraphing with the Three Word Sentence Game (see p. 155), and correct minimal control ("She said, sipping her tea") by shouting additions: "She said, sipping her tea, and realizing that it tasted strange," or "He said, looking bored and starting to undress."
            - Variations: Gibberish version: experts use gibberish dialogue ("Enyam regor?" "He said, sitting on the sofa, and taking my hand. 'Ocil lej?'") while continuing to give the instructions in English, and bizarrely find it less confusing. Shadows version: two players supply the dialogue while two other "shadows" stay very close and supply the stage directions, which encourages bolder scenes because players feel less alone. Audience volunteers version: the players give stage directions to a volunteer as well as to each other. Pimping version, preferred before raucous audiences: "He said, bursting into song," "He said, reading The Fall of the Roman Empire ... aloud," which gets laughs but turns the game into pure filler where no story emerges. Adjective version: supply adjectives instead of stage directions, e.g. "Sorry I'm late (SUSPICION)." "You're late rather a lot these days, what's going on? (PRIDE)." "I know where you've been. I followed you (CONTEMPT)."
            - Coaching: The game sounds coercive, but your partner can't really control you against your will ("There are no more patients, Doctor." "She said, unbuttoning her blouse. 'Nurse Kimble, stop it at once!'" "He said, buttoning it up for me ..."). It was suggested by actors at Tournus (Denmark), who named it "He Said, Removing His Trousers," and Johnstone's three-year-old learned it instantly because toddlers are used to being handled and eager for revenge. When confusion erupts, spell the rules out again and conclude: "So the confusion lies in us, not in the instructions. Don't get disheartened, it takes about forty minutes to master this game." Note that the comical is often identical to the horrific, as in the brain-stealing surgeon sequence played with three-word sentences.
        - Substitution Impro
            - Players: two starting players plus any number of substituting players (class or audience)
            - Setup: a stage with whatever the chosen scene needs
            - Goal: trains confident, unplanned playing, since the situation continues even though the actors keep changing, the exact opposite of the Freeze Game
            - Rules
                - Set up a master-servant scene or a boy-girl scene, and get the class to agree that the players will keep being substituted.
                - At first few substitutions occur, but the agreement lets you shout "replace them" and even push students physically into the scene.
                - Match the posture you inherit: if someone is on their knees, kneel as you replace them (a tap on the shoulder tells them to leave), if they are mid-sentence you should complete it, if their arms are outspread your arms should be outspread.
                - Sustain established voices through the scene: if one character speaks in a foreign accent and another in upper-class English, keep those dialects.
                - Expect visual overload at high frequency: players substituted every few seconds, a couple about to kiss changed so often the action goes into extreme slow motion, a shot character recast while falling, even the corpse morphing while the murderer tries to cover up the crime.
            - Variations: Battle of the Sexes: a dispute between a man and a woman, men substitute the man and women the woman, with the women cheering for the woman and the men for the man, which can generate so much enthusiasm that spectators run onstage (Johnstone recalls a "husband" saying cuttingly "Oh, it's you!", a woman rushing from the audience to replace the "wife" with "Did you think it was your mistress?", and a male spectator leaping up with "Married to you, who can afford a mistress?"). Don't let men substitute for women or vice versa outside this variant, it's a gag that stops the game being taken seriously. Quieter versions exist: start with someone sitting alone in the park who is approached by a stranger, perhaps they leave together and the scene continues in a cafe or someone's house. Also known as tag-team impro, satisfying the spectator's urge to leap onstage and say or do the thing the players failed to do.
            - Coaching: An excellent performance game, really more suitable for Free-Impro because it's less exhausting with more players, and it can occur spontaneously as a way of ending a scene. Johnstone introduced it because proud beginners wouldn't signal for help, and forced substitution made them wonderfully confident: why plan or worry about doing well when you know you're about to be replaced? Once substitutions reach a certain frequency everyone, including absolute beginners, is eager to leap in, a bit like starting a cold motor.
        - Moving Bodies
            - Players: pairs, one "puppet" and one "puppeteer" per pair
            - Setup: two chairs a few feet apart for the puppets, and real furniture: the scenes are not suitable for bare stages, and sofas offer more possibilities than chairs, so the Judges should say "Some furniture please!"
            - Goal: trains shared control of a scene, isolation of body parts, and justifying imposed movement
            - Rules
                - The characters invent their own dialogue, but their bodies are operated like puppets by other people (when Roddy Maude Roxby saw the original at Jerome Robbins' studio the puppeteers dictated the words too, but Johnstone insisted the control be shared).
                - Sit two puppets on chairs and start them as strangers in some public place, a park or a cruise ship. Warn the operators not to yank the puppets about or put them in painful positions.
                - Movements should be justified: if your puppeteer points your finger, say something like "What's that over there?" or "Look at this ladybird on my finger!" Conversely, if you say "Look at that!", you can hope your puppeteer points your finger for you.
                - Only the part of the body being moved should move, don't "help" your puppeteer, and shout reminders like "Just the arm," "Not the neck!" or "Only move the part that's being moved!", because puppets must isolate parts that habitually function as a unit.
                - A puppeteer who wants the puppet to tap its foot must keep on tapping it, the puppet won't take over the movement.
                - Errors should be justified: if your head has been left facing down say "Footprints!" or "I'm too ashamed to look you in the eye!", if you are toppling say "Quick! My heart medication!" or "I think we need a mural on that ceiling," and if your head faces somewhere inappropriate drop hints: "A lovely view." "Yes, I only wish I could see it."
            - Variations: With audience volunteers as puppeteers: creates enormous benevolence, ask them to be gentle, express hope that some sort of story emerges, remind them that elbows don't bend backward, fingers stay away from orifices, and that they should step back sometimes rather than move the puppets non-stop. If a puppet is reduced to a talking head it should say things like "I'll just stand up and walk over to the stereo," or "Shall we dance?"
            - Coaching: Puppeteers "forget" parts of the puppet, leaving an arm sticking out or walking it leg by leg with the head twisted sideways. Allow this in performance unless the puppet seems in pain, but don't let puppeteers be stupid deliberately, the accidents are funny enough. Beginner puppeteers forget that legs must be beneath us when we stand, and one pleasure is watching a small puppeteer handle a large puppet threatening to overbalance. The game is so entertaining that some players want it at the end of every performance, discourage this or the shows become predictable and the laughs too easy.
        - Explore the Possibilities
            - Players: a Moving Bodies troupe, potentially with audience helpers
            - Setup: as Moving Bodies, plus room to move through the auditorium
            - Goal: generating new versions of Moving Bodies
            - Rules
                - Start from real events: when a Moving Bodies scene turned into a flying lesson, six people ran out of the audience to help "fly" the student around the auditorium.
                - Invent further scenarios, for example a puppet tormented by questions of free will, or a puppet who is aware of the puppeteers and is diagnosed as insane.
            - Variants to avoid: Some cowardly impro groups cast audience volunteers as the puppets. This is not what the audience wants to see, and few volunteers have the skill to isolate the part of the body being moved.
        - The Arms
            - Players: pairs labelled A and B (A supplies the voice and body, B supplies the arms), later combining into larger configurations
            - Setup: open space for the party stage, chairs or a table for the teacher stage, audience seating facing the players for Two Sets of Arms
            - Goal: trains instant cooperation and shared control, and works as a sensitivity game allowing people to invade each other's space without feeling threatened (from Laurel and Hardy, now one of the most popular impro games in the world)
            - Rules
                - Party Version: "Find a partner. Call yourselves A and B. Bs stand behind As with your arms thrust under their arms. The As should put their hands behind the Bs' backs. Each pair is to become one person with A supplying the voice and B doing the gestures."
                - Coach the pair to break taboos in stages: can the Bs touch the As' hair while the As move their heads a little as if doing the touching themselves, could the Bs touch the As' faces, could they adjust the As' clothing (this evokes the most laughter, sweaters tugged about, necklines adjusted).
                - Set the scene: "Be at a party. Meet old friends! Find out where the drinks are! Shuffle about and introduce yourselves to strangers! Mingle!" Gaze out of the window so nobody feels scrutinized, and after twenty seconds or so make everyone change partners, since each change makes the students more alert.
                - Arms Teacher stage: select one pair of arms to address the group: "Be a teacher. But establish that the class is about to end so that the moment you feel the need to be 'clever' or 'original' you can just say, 'Class dismissed,' or 'For homework tonight I want you to ...' and we'll fade the lights." Encourage the arms to point at audience members while the body says things like "Tutti! Are you cheating?" or "Suzanne! Put your hands on the desk where I can see them!" or "Etienne? Did you throw that paper aeroplane?"
                - Next tell the pair to imagine a table in front of them, and B mimes picking up items that A describes: "The sex life of snails is a closed book to many people, but I have here a male snail, called Nat." The left hand displays Nat. "And I have here a female snail, called Betsy." The right hand mimes lifting Betsy. "Calm down, Nat! Stop it, Betsy! Just a moment, I'd better tie on the little blindfolds." And so on.
                - Two Sets of Arms stage: take two pairs of arms and ask them to face the audience, explaining that if they want to approach each other they'll have to move crab-wise because the illusion works best seen from the front. Ask for an interaction between strangers, a problem, a possible tilt, and a solution, for instance two strangers at a late-night bus stop discovering the last bus has gone, until one realizes the other was the school bully and can be bullied into sharing a taxi.
                - Loading the Pockets is a corrupt version: the pockets are crammed with objects which the arms produce one by one while the body comments, reducing the game to pure filler with no narrative.
            - Variations: In the tobacco days, have the arms light a cigarette for the body to smoke, excellent for gigs where the audience spoke no English. Shaving the face with foam and an empty razor might be fun.
            - Coaching: If players ignore their partners and start planning, encourage them to respond to what's actually happening. Common sense says the game would be easier if either A or B were in charge, but the control should be shared: if A says "I have here a stick of gelignite!" the arms should pick up a stick of gelignite, and if B's hand covers A's mouth, A should yawn. Some of the best moments in improvisation occur when no one is sure who's leading and who's following. Avoid Loading the Pockets, and especially the unpleasant spectacle of improvisers cramming their pockets before a scene: one object would be enough, but at the moment the improviser might move into the future (inspired by a rubber knife to commit suicide, or by a peanut to expound the problems of living with an elephant) the arms sidetrack by presenting a new object.
        - The Dwarf
            - Players: two performers, A above and B providing arms below
            - Setup: boots on A's hands, cloth covering A's chest so we just see the boots projecting underneath (build a special costume), a table or sofa for the dwarf to stand behind
            - Goal: two-person illusion performance and physical comedy
            - Rules
                - B's arms protrude under A's arms, A's hands are thrust into boots, and the cloth hides the join, producing the effect of a misshapen creature about three feet high standing just behind a table or sofa.
                - Let it "walk" about on the table or sofa-back, clamber up after "falling" over the edge, dance about, do the splits, lie on its side resting on one elbow, flap its arms to rise into the air, sit with a bump, and twist its foot three hundred and sixty degrees (or let someone else twist it).
            - Coaching: Originally a party trick from Johnstone's youth, used in improvisations and plays. Dwarves work well when they're cheeky, extrovert characters.
        - The Giant
            - Players: two performers, A the upper body and B the legs
            - Setup: a bench, cloth bags for A's legs, a costume covering both bodies
            - Goal: two-person illusion performance
            - Rules
                - B lies on the bench with bare legs dangling over the end, feet towards the audience. A faces front and stands astride B, A's legs hidden in cloth bags, the costume covering both bodies to give the effect of an eight-foot-high person who happens to be sitting down.
                - Sometimes the giant should lean one way while its legs point the other way, so it seems inherently unstable, and you can add instability by having the body lean back and wave its arms for balance while the feet lift into the air.
                - Give it business: it can force people to do exercises, touching its own toes by letting its feet swing up to meet its hands, it can bully its servants, it can be a terrible coward who gloats over the misfortunes of others.
            - Variations: Johnstone used two giants as the High Priests when he directed the Wakefield Cycle.
            - Coaching: Make sure the giant is always loud and exuberant.
        - Adjective
            - Players: pairs or groups
            - Goal: explores how a single applied quality colours a whole scene, provided it is justified
            - Rules
                - Apply an adjective to a scene, for example the slow-family, the fit-family, the evil-family, the suspicious-family, the religious-family, the silly-family, the right-wing-family.
                - Or set up a pecking order of bigots, or start a suspicious master-servant scene.
            - Coaching: Stanislavsky said "Never play adjectives," but it's okay if you justify them.
        - Wide Eyes
            - Players: individuals in scenes, then the whole group, then pairs
            - Setup: space for scenes, and a party setup for the en masse version
            - Goal: opens and relaxes the players, making them more open, vulnerable and uninhibited, diminishing self-consciousness and arousing protective feelings in the audience
            - Rules
                - Improvise a scene with your eyes as wide open as possible, keeping the lids raised even when you glance down.
                - Wide Eyes en Masse: set up a thirty-second "party" at which everyone has tight eyes, then discuss the sensations. Then hold a party in which all the guests have wide-open "innocent" eyes, noticing how in both cases self-consciousness diminishes because everyone concentrates on what they're doing rather than on your opinion of them. Then hold a party mixing narrow-eyed people with wide-eyed people.
                - Wide Eyes, Friends Meet: before you mention eyes, play a twenty-second scene in which two great friends meet after a long separation, then get them to repeat it with eyes wide open, and the difference may be astounding, with more enthusiasm, larger gestures, and less fear of the space. Tell them that if they glance "out front" occasionally they'll notice most of the onlookers also have wide eyes, it's infectious.
            - Variations: the mixed narrow-eyed and wide-eyed party
            - Coaching: We all had wide eyes in early childhood, and when we wanted something they became so enormous it was difficult to refuse us, but since then many of us close our lids defensively. Ask students to notice Harpo's eyes, or Goldie Hawn's, or Benny Hill's, or Nina Hagen's, and explain that tight eyes may be okay for baddies but don't encourage us to feel positive and outgoing. Warning: some professional actors open the eyes unnaturally wide, a "blob on a boiled egg" effect which is occasionally useful but is the equivalent of shouting all the time. The eye that is open, trusting and friendly has the pupil fully exposed with no white above or below it. Learn to open your eyes by relaxing them rather than pitting one set of muscles against another: try lying down, letting go, while trying to sense the exact weight of your eyeballs, which can be emotional.
        - Wide Mouth
            - Players: individual students, then in scenes
            - Goal: explores how mouth shape changes status, boldness, and fear of the audience
            - Rules
                - Have students project their voices very loudly while making their mouths narrower than usual, which releases laughter and makes them extremely bossy.
                - Then go for the rebound effect: widen the mouth slightly, perhaps parting the lips slightly, and look at the objects and people around you. You'll probably feel bolder, some people feel as if they could "eat the world," and fear of the audience lessens just as in the Wide Eye procedure. This is not grinning or smiling but a widening of about a quarter of an inch, and it's the intention to widen that creates the effect.
                - Play status through teeth: high-status people expose the bottom teeth (Jimmy Carter can do this even looking friendly and good natured) whereas low-status people expose the top teeth. Try biting the bottom lip, raising the upper lip and giving a stupid giggle, noticing how the back of the neck wants to shorten.
                - Expose both sets of teeth and breathe audibly: you may want to smash things (don't), the space to the sides becomes more important, elbows tend to move sideways and the body tends to rotate left and right a little. Use the effect for thugs or executioners, and note that an executioner's black hood hiding everything but the mouth and chin intensifies it.
            - Coaching: The man who auditioned dancers for the Crazy Horse nightclub in Paris said that when in doubt he opted for the ones with "big mouths," and many models and dynamic people seem to have extra teeth. Everyday mouths contracted so far that one wonders what they are refusing. The widened state releases positive behaviour, a sort of Mel Gibson in Lethal Weapon dogginess.
        - Tempo
            - Players: pairs, groups, or threesomes
            - Setup: open space
            - Goal: separates dramatic speed from physical speed and trains players to hold independent tempos
            - Rules
                - Establish that dramatic speed is related to the pace at which ideas are changing: a marathon runner might be dramatically slow, while a sentry standing at attention approached by a dog that cocks its leg might be dramatically fast, even if you only know it by the sweat breaking out on his forehead.
                - Ask a "slow" player to have a new thought every thirty seconds and a "fast" player a new thought every five seconds, as in the airport scene where the slow player drifts ("I like flying ...", "I like to get a window seat.", "If you're going to be cooped up ...", "You might as well have a good view.", "Of course, if it's cloudy ...") while the fast player panics ("Where's my ticket? I left it in my ... No! It's up in the bathroom. Did I pack my razor?", "Perhaps it's in the suitcase! But which suitcase?", "Did I pack the alarm-clock?", "No time to shave! Do I look respectable?", "Passport! Where is it?", "That's the old one. Ah! It's in my pocket!", "Shampoo! I'll get some from the bathroom.", "Did you phone the airline?", "Oh, God, I'll do it myself. The phone book!").
                - Ask groups to change ideas at the same pace, perhaps a slow picnic that gradually accelerates into a fast picnic, where the changes of pace need to be justified.
            - Variations: Pairs in which one player changes from slow to fast and the other from fast to slow, asking them to control the rate of change so they "pass" each other midway. Threesomes in which A maintains a neutral tempo, B moves from fast to slow, and C moves from slow to fast, leaving the stage just before exploding.
            - Coaching: Beginners get security by joining each other, so if one is fast they all tend to be fast, and simply asking for different speeds gives poor results until thoughts are rationed by the clock. It's always a pleasure seeing actors who do not take the same tempo.
        - Sound Scape
            - Players: one player offstage making sounds, plus the onstage players
            - Setup: a microphone, and a collected stock of noise-makers
            - Goal: trains sound-effect work that supports, extends, and comments on the action
            - Rules
                - A player offstage makes sound effects for the onstage players. The game dates back to one of the first classes at the Studio, when Johnstone clanked tin-cans while someone moved about wearing a mimed suit of armour, and it led to scenes with improvisers and jazz musicians. Use a microphone.
                - Typical sounds include doors creaking and slamming, taps or showers running, footsteps, voices, cars revving, beds creaking, animals barking, howling and whining, chalk screeching on blackboards, bones or muscles cracking as players stretch, metal vibrating ("doooinggg!"), things being inflated and deflating, fists smashing, yawns, sneezes and tuts, and emotional sounds like panting, gulping, sighing, weeping, and laughing.
                - The game can also involve dubbing the dialogue, although that would be unusual: Jasmine mimes opening a door (creak) and closing it (slam), walks to the shelf (footsteps), mimes removing a huge tin (scrape), mimes opening it (wrenching sounds plus the whining of a huge hungry animal), pours the contents on to the floor (slurping sounds), pats the huge thing making the slurping and panting noises (patting sounds) and says "Did you have fun today, darling?" and a huge voice on the mike says "Yes, I chased the postman!"
            - Variations: Sounds can be accepted physically (every joint can creak, and your digestive system can make strange noises), placed in the environment (the undertakers' sign is creaking in the wind), or used to establish mood, although music is better at that.
            - Coaching: Collect noise-makers.
        - You're Interesting
            - Players: pairs, with the coach interrupting and feeding suggestions
            - Setup: open space
            - Goal: a way of marking time on stage while seeming alive and interested, which reverses the beginner's habits of ignoring others and lowering expectations, and diminishes future-funk
            - Rules
                - Follow three rules: feel fit and awake, be interested in everything that the others say or do, and say nothing interesting, while avoiding being so uninteresting that you become interesting (by talking about nothing but cabbages, for example).
                - If a line creates no effect, interrupt: "She said, 'Good morning,' so be interested! Look surprised! Let it have some effect on you."
                - Restart the scene. They react to "Good morning!" with "So it is!", and when that is ignored, explain that whoever said "Good morning" is obliged by the rules to make the "So it is!" interesting as well, which produces the sheepish "Sorry I'm a bit late!"
                - If that line also creates no effect, suggest "I'm glad you managed to get here," or "Yes, we kept some cake for you," but delete remarks like "Did you have an accident?" as too interesting.
                - Develop the story by throwing in the occasional interesting idea: "Say, 'I've been promoted,'" or "Say, 'I've joined the police force.'"
                - Continue applying the rules to whatever emerges, as in the extended sample dialogue: "A policeman! That must be so interesting." "[Brightly] Really? Well, I suppose it must look glamorous from the outside, but it's all run of the mill. Parking tickets ... interrogations ..." "Interrogations? Goodness!" "You're interested? Well, that's encouraging. But it's mostly just chatting to citizens who don't quite know their place." "Oh! Not people like me, then? Hah! Hah!" "No, no, just social misfits. I look on it as further education." "Further education? Really! The teacher is always right, eh?" "What did you say?" "[Alarmed] Er ... what did you think I said?" "[Suave] I thought you said something very interesting. Drink up!" "Drink up? Er, are we going anywhere?" "We're having a party at Headquarters." "[Suppressed panic] But I couldn't er ..." "Of course you can. It's just round the corner." "Um ... er ... [Gulp]" Reject the closing line, "Drinks are on us. And you'll get a 'Help Your Local Police, Beat Yourself Up' T-shirt," as too interesting.
            - Coaching: When beginners try to think of clever things to say they pay scant attention to anyone else and look feeble and unsure to lower our expectations, and being interested reverses these tactics. Players who say dull things are likely to look bored, so remind them to be healthy and alert and to respond with interest to whatever is said. Once students obey the instructions they look wonderfully alive and fearless, and their interest makes us interested. Struggling to say dull things while being interested is more entertaining than the usual "I'm the wittiest" interaction.
        - Boring the Audience
            - Players: players of an already admired acting scene, plus the coach
            - Setup: any staged scene that has been rehearsed or performed
            - Goal: removes the pressure to "do your best" and allows something other than the social personality to operate
            - Rules
                - Take the players of an admired scene aside and whisper something to them.
                - Ask them to play the scene again.
                - The secret instruction is that they should bore the audience without being bored themselves.
                - The second presentation will almost certainly seem noticeably improved, and sometimes the actors seem wonderfully talented, revealing a truth and absorption never seen in them before.
                - Enjoy the onlookers' wrong guesses about what you whispered ("To be interesting? To be expressive? To be more relaxed? To pay attention to each other? To be truthful? To have a subtext? To have a purpose?") and the players' amazement, sometimes total disbelief, that their attempt to bore us made them more interesting.
            - Coaching: Most students were dreary when they did their best but became fascinating when content to be just average, hence Johnstone's old shouted instruction "Be more boring!" at the Studio. William Wyler stopped Olivier from emoting by reading the newspaper while making him do the same shot as many as fifty times, and Olivier was grateful, but telling him to "bore the camera" might have wasted less film.
        - Wallpaper Drama
            - Players: pairs
            - Setup: open space
            - Goal: a training game spun off from work on transitions, useful for improvised soap operas, that sustains interest while marking time on stage
            - Rules
                - Morph between positive and negative, always going through neutral, and always giving all three states the same value.
                - Work through the cycle using a concrete situation, for example the birthday gift scene. Positive: "Hey, great to see you." "Isn't it your birthday?" "You remembered!" "I bought this for you." "Oh, you shouldn't have!" Moving towards neutral: "You'll like it. It's an egg-whisk." "Ah well, I can always use another one. It's the thought that counts." "Yes, well, I was thinking of you." Moving towards negative: "It's my twenty-first birthday. And what do you bring me? A bloody egg-whisk." "If you don't want it, I'll have it back." "I want it!" "Show some gratitude then!" Moving towards neutral: "Yes, well, I'm sorry." "The thing is, I didn't have any money. Actually, I took the egg-whisk out of the kitchen drawer." "Stole it from your mum?" "I wanted to give you something." Moving towards positive: "We could make some egg-nog." "Great!" And so on.
                - The coach may need to prod the scene occasionally, for example "Tell him you're pregnant," and then, "Tell him it's not his."
            - Variations: More tricks for soap operas (not Johnstone's): enter a circle of light at one side of the stage, and/or touch the proscenium, to explain privately to the audience how you feel, go to the opposite side of the stage to supply narrative and/or explanations, and have an object that you touch when you want the audience to tell you the next line.
            - Coaching: The name comes from doing it well: you can burble on for ever and yet sustain some sort of interest. Left to themselves beginners move between negative and neutral and avoid being positive, so don't let this happen.
        - Straight Men
            - Players: pairs, a comic and a straight man
            - Setup: optionally a Velcroed letter "S" badge supplied by the scenographers
            - Goal: builds the neglected skill of playing the serious foil, and teaches strong contrast between performers
            - Rules
                - Establish the classic pairing: the straight man is a long-suffering "parental" figure and the comic is a pesky "child" who annoys them, as with Bert and Ernie on Sesame Street, or ventriloquists and their dummies, and note that in public improvisation there are very few straight men.
                - Have the scenographers stick a Velcroed "S" (for Straight man) on any improviser who is compulsively gagging, and tell the audience that this player is to be serious and will be dragged away by the gag-police if he or she makes any jokes.
                - Insist on strong contrast between the straight man and the comic, citing Jerry Lewis and Dean Martin, or Margaret Dupont and Groucho Marx.
                - Illustrate with Bernie Winters' account of Mike Winters commissioning a tailor to work through the night on a crazy Teddy boy outfit with drainpipe trousers, heavily padded shoulders and a velvet collar, designed to make Bernie look like a sack of potatoes moving across the stage: he stumbled on with the suit and big hat, ran up and down, and the audience howled before either had opened his mouth, his nervousness triggering the "ee" giggle which they roared at, and after that they could not go wrong, because the bulky suit made the perfect contrast to Mike's immaculate suit.
            - Coaching: Being a straight man is a skill worth acquiring, and should be taught at the same time as "being a passenger".

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 10 Procedures, gibberish and status #book:storytellers #gibberish #status
        - Gibberish Cards
            - Players: Any number working singly then in pairs
            - Setup: Gibberish sentences written along the narrow ends of playing cards to serve as scripts
            - Goal: Fluent gibberish with full vowel and consonant range, no prejudging of sounds, no 'Oggy Boggy Soggy Doggie Yoggy'
            - Rules
                - Hand out cards and say 'Use these cards as your script.' Reading means no one can be wrong, so students who suffered agonies of embarrassment become instantly proficient.
                - Watch your partner to discover whether you gave a compliment, an insult, or a proposal of marriage. Meaning is established by the recipient, so the emphasis falls on reacting rather than acting.
            - Variations: For over-fluent players (tobacco auctioneer pace, worshippers speaking in tongues) ask them to repeat some of the phrases their partner says, sometimes be at a loss for words, and adapt their gibberish to match the partner's so both seem to be speaking the same language. Later add national flavors: Italian, German, English, Canadian, Deep South gibberish.
            - Coaching: Strasberg urged making gibberish understood. Johnstone replaces this with watching the partner. Toddlers love speaking in tongues and make safe practice partners.
        - A Class in Gibberish
            - Players: Pairs rotating through class
            - Setup: One gibberish card per player initially, two later
            - Goal: Ingest a lifelong gibberish vocabulary with minimal stress
            - Rules
                - One improviser sits waiting, another enters, each holding one card: they meet, speak their sentence, and leave together.
                - Then allow two cards each. His example: [Entering] 'Mahkram Artep?' [Standing up] 'Ena hej!' [They're leaving together] 'Resarf nok!' [Nodding head] 'Reksew!'
                - Encourage a slight foreign accent.
                - When skilled, move to longer scenes in gibberish, then wean off cards by suggesting they begin adding gibberish sentences of their own.
            - Coaching: Ask why some interactions amuse and others don't. Students adopting stupid walks and comical gestures evoke minimal response, whereas quite normal players get laughs. Said to a dull improviser 'Eid leakim!' has no effect: a brilliant one looks surprised, interested, angry, shocked, embarrassed. We laugh from delight in seeing things happen, especially a drop in status. At least one player must be altered by the other, since altering yourself as you speak has little effect.
        - Alternating Gibberish and English
            - Players: Two or more
            - Setup: None beyond the switch command
            - Goal: Transfer the spatial and vocal confidence characteristic of good gibberish into normal improvisations
            - Rules
                - Version One: players switch from Gibberish to English and back on command, also runs with Party Endowments.
                - Version Two: A starts speaking gibberish while B speaks English, and any player may switch at any time, forcing the partner to switch too. His example: 'Nots ruh tyr rej?' / 'He's with a patient.' / 'Onito tenileh!' / [Switch] 'Tenileh?' / 'Yes, it's going to be twins.' / 'Adaran!' / [Switch] 'Renk luaf semaj!' / 'Oh dear! Should I call the nurse?'
                - Some changes from English to gibberish and back should occur mid-sentence.
            - Variations: Try with Party Endowments (p. 233)
            - Coaching: Mid-sentence switching is what pushes the confidence across into ordinary play.
        - Gibberish Shadows
            - Players: Four: two beginners play, two experienced shadows
            - Setup: Each beginner gets an experienced shadow staying close
            - Goal: Beginners play a full scene without being responsible for the sounds they make
            - Rules
                - Two beginners play a scene normally.
                - Each has an experienced shadow who stays close and feeds them gibberish sentences throughout.
        - Jokes in Gibberish
            - Players: Groups of four or five
            - Setup: Swap jokes in gibberish
            - Goal: Timing learned from listeners: when to hold back, when to release
            - Rules
                - Beginners start by translating existing jokes.
                - Then forget translation: 'Watch the onlookers for clues as to when to hold back, and when to release.'
                - The build-up to the climax can only be done by observing the listeners' reactions, correcting the common fault of continuing past the point where listeners expect the punchline.
            - Coaching: Introduce as 'advanced' or your students may get discouraged.
        - Reality Testing
            - Players: Solo students plus unwitting public
            - Setup: A dirty piece of paper with a fake address
            - Goal: Authentic gibberish generated by desperation not to be rumbled
            - Rules
                - Students dine out pretending to speak no English, which makes their gibberish very authentic because they were desperate not to be rumbled.
                - Or send them out clutching a dirty paper with a fake address, spouting gibberish to passers-by who always send them off in some definite direction.
            - Coaching: Anecdotal RADA assignment from Gower Street days, apologies owed to Dillon's bookshop.
        - Guess the Situation (Gibberish)
            - Players: Threes: one guesser, two scene players, playable en masse
            - Setup: Guesser sent out of earshot while the class agrees a situation
            - Goal: Keep actors in the present moment, remove lingering gibberish anxieties, show players how misleading they are
            - Rules
                - Agree a situation, e.g. guesser Roger is a brain surgeon and Sarah brings her son Tony to his clinic.
                - Guesser returns and is told he plays a scene in English while Sarah and Tony speak gibberish, treats them as if he understands everything, then afterwards reports who he was, who the others were, where the scene took place.
                - Scene players communicate purely through behaviour while the guesser improvises aloud and guesses: Sarah implores on her knees, Roger guesses executions and galleys, Tony points at his head, Sarah mimes chipping a hole in Tony's skull, Roger condemns adulterers, Sarah kisses Tony.
                - Stop the scene, forbid anyone to give the secret away, ask the guesser what he understood.
                - Replay with two other students profiting from the errors just seen: in the dog version (Olive is the dog who messed David's white carpet, Pat is David's sister) the new players whistle her in and press her down onto all fours until she barks and everyone cheers.
                - Divide the class into threes and play Guess the Situation en masse, encouraging fun situations such as the family returned from Mum's funeral where the guesser is Mum, i.e. Mum is a ghost.
            - Variations: Choose situations that will be fun
            - Coaching: Excellent for increasing beginner confidence. Even beginners don't mind failing at it because it is so entertaining. Blunder after blunder sweeps remaining qualms aside through sheer need to communicate.
        - Establishing Status Signals
            - Players: Any number, taught by lecture-demonstration
            - Setup: No props
            - Goal: Conscious control of dominance signals feeding every Status Game
            - Rules
                - Hold eye contact to dominate, break it and take a quick glance back when feeling submissive.
                - Hands near the head signal low status: touch your mouth while looking at someone and you feel hesitant and are perceived as lowering your status. A cigarette moved towards the mouth conceals a gesture of submission, which is why smoking is cool. Touching someone's face or head raises your status if accepted, hence doing it to children and the curious behaviour of barbers.
                - Other things equal, highest status uses the most space and is the most relaxed except when challenged. Folded arms, stiff symmetrical body reads as eager to be high status yet fails against someone lying on a sofa, legs lolling apart, fearless of attack.
                - We placate higher-status people by ruining posture and constricting the voice.
                - Try-it demonstrations: say Clint Eastwood's 'Make my day' while wagging your head and notice how ineffective it becomes, and drop instantly in status by standing toes together with heels out.
            - Coaching: Most of us are better at high or better at low, both defensive: high says 'Keep away, I bite', low says 'I'm not worth biting'. Bad drama schools give high roles to aristocratic students and subservient roles to low-status students: Hugh Crutwell at RADA did the opposite. Woody Allen raises status in order to demolish it, the gold watch inherited from the grandfather who sold it to him on his deathbed.
        - Circular Status
            - Players: Three or more standing in a circle
            - Setup: Circle formation
            - Goal: Practise playing high status to one person while playing low to another
            - Rules
                - Stand players in a circle and say 'The player to your left will dominate you, and the player on your right will submit to you', producing for example John above Ron above Terra above John.
                - Play scenes inside the loop.
            - Variations: Ghost Game dialogue (p. 181) cited as an instance of the same skill
            - Coaching: Such scenes seldom get anywhere but practising the double orientation is worthwhile.
        - Status Parties
            - Players: Whole class divided into Group A and Group B
            - Setup: Party space, no special props
            - Goal: Split the group into high-status and low-status players and demonstrate that applying one technique makes the others lock on automatically
            - Rules
                - Run a plain party: 'Mingle! Meet old friends. Ask where the food is. Exchange gossip. Find the hostess.' Stop after perhaps thirty seconds because anxiety makes players adopt each other's tempo and use of space.
                - Restart with contrasting assignments from his signal menus: blinking (As blink more often than usual, Bs hardly ever, opening and closing eyes slowly if they must, blinkers shortening the backs of their necks and becoming isolated, non-blinkers loud and confident, trying to dominate each other), speech (As use long grammatically complete sentences, Bs fragmented sentences and out of breath: 'Ah, Jeremy, I thought I might meet you here.' / '[Gasp] Mary . . . er . . . Well, I . . . yes, what a surprise . . .' then play the mirror version with Bs gasping through 'Rather a . . . er . . . [Gasp] good party'), heads (As keep heads absolutely still during each phrase and never touch their heads, Bs move heads normally and touch their own heads and faces), tempo (As slower and smoother than usual, delaying before replying including a physical delay, don't flinch, Bs making small unnecessary movements and responding instantly), voice and feet (As open each sentence with a long thoughtful 'errrrr' and rotate one foot slightly outward, Bs respond with an immediate tiny 'er' and turn one foot inward).
                - Have everyone try both poles.
            - Variations: Status Transitions continues directly from this game
            - Coaching: Few students enjoy both experiences equally because they have a preferred status, but require both. Non-blinkers tend to hold eye contact while blinkers keep looking away and glancing back as if power streaming from the Bs is too powerful to confront.
        - Two-Person Status Games
            - Players: Two plus watching class
            - Setup: Short scenes, furniture helps
            - Goal: Sustain each status configuration and learn gradual reversal
            - Rules
                - Both play low: '[Nervous laugh] I was so . . . er . . . grateful for your . . . er . . . invitation.' / 'Oh, please . . . I never thought . . . I mean . . . I'm so . . . so proud that you came.' / 'Er . . . no one invites me anywhere, actually.' Note that low-status players are hesitant to contradict people, like the editor in Evelyn Waugh's Scoop who said 'Up to a point, Lord Cooper' when he meant No.
                - One plays high, one low: 'So this is where you live, Cedric! Rather squalid!' / 'I'll clear a space on the bed for you, sir. I'm so sorry!' / 'Bit dark in here! If you could wash the windows, we might be able to see the view.' / 'That . . . er . . . that is the view. It's a cement wall.' / 'Well, I'd have that torn down for starters . . .'
                - Both play high: 'Shoes off, Paul. Can't have you trampling dog-shit everywhere.' / 'So this is where you live? It can't be pleasant sharing that toilet on the landing.' / 'It's just a pied-a-terre. I spend most of my time abroad, actually.' / 'Come hunting on the estate this weekend. We've a deer park full of animals all trained to stand sideways so you can get a good shot at them.'
                - Start one high one low and reverse gradually during the scene: tenant avoiding the landlord, visitor offering to buy a painting, smearing the painted clouds, 'My goodness! But these clouds are so real I could reach out and touch them! Oh!' / 'You've . . . You've smeared it!' / 'I, I'm so sorry.' / 'Idiot!'
            - Coaching: Theatresports status challenges tend to disappoint because players milk it: crawling onstage or pretending to pick noses for low, claiming to be millionaires or rock stars for high. Ideally status permeates all scenes, a good situation being one that makes status very clear and perhaps reverses it. Unless players are experts, explicit status challenges create inferior work.
        - Status Transitions
            - Players: Pairs
            - Setup: Chairs and a sofa
            - Goal: Justified, gradual reversal of physical status positions
            - Rules
                - Party form: replay status parties but each group gradually changes to the other group's behaviour, starters high ending low and vice versa.
                - Chair form: student sits on a chair, feet flat, toes touching, heels apart, one hand touching her mouth. Partner towers over her perhaps with one foot on an adjacent chair. Most students resist the dominant position, even leaning back, so tell them to point at their partners and yell something like 'Why did you do it?' or 'Don't you ever disobey me again': the body shifts forward into an aggressive pose and space pours over the submissive victim.
                - Play a scene ending when each has achieved the other's starting position, chairs interchangeable. Coach calls 'Keep altering! Don't get stuck! Janet, open your legs a little! Bill, touch your nose for a split second!'
                - Sofa form: one sprawls on the sofa in a high-status way, legs apart, the other kneels with head towards them and forehead touching the floor. Scenes end when they have swapped positions.
            - Coaching: Players must find justifications for the changes.
        - The Rejection Game
            - Players: Groups of four or five
            - Setup: A sociable scene with real furniture: couple who moved in upstairs invited down for coffee, or four friends coming into a lounge bar. Sofa, chairs and a low coffee table help because people feel safer on a sofa than at the edge of the group
            - Goal: Toughen improvisers and make them genuinely friendly, dissolving the secret caution that isolates actors and makes movies look unreal
            - Rules
                - Instruction to each: 'When the scene ends one of you has to be rejected by the other three, it must absolutely not be you!' This causes instant panic because the rejected person really loses, so frame it: 'No one will be forced to play this game. But if you're ejected, we can tell you why, and this will improve your ability to stay in a group,' or 'It's really a friendship game, since if you have a friend in the group, you're safe, and the two of you can eject someone else.'
                - Play, expecting minute after minute with nobody rejected: the moment anyone becomes vulnerable the others rescue them with eye contact and attention.
                - Coach demonstrations: tell a player feeling vulnerable to take his chair and move to the other side of the group to say something to another player, tying him to one person and isolating someone else, or turn someone into a ghost: 'Keep talking together, but when John says something pay not the slightest attention, so that he feels as if he doesn't exist.' Intervene if someone rescues the ghost with eye contact.
                - Valid endings: three exit leaving one onstage, one driven away, one clearly isolated and out of the group even though still physically there.
                - Strengthening techniques: talk to an individual, general remarks weaken your position. Don't control the group, taking charge and bossing isolates you. Never cross your legs away from the group. Don't fold arms or clutch the furniture or yourself. Don't pull feet under the chair. Open the body so your space flows into other people's. Find the most sympathetic person and try to link with them. And you aren't rejected until you accept rejection: stay calm and unperturbed, bide your time until you see a chance to link to somebody.
            - Coaching: Especially scary to pubescent people because rejection is fiercest then. After a few rounds anxiety is swept away by roars of laughter and the improvisation alters: players were only pretending to be friendly before, now they genuinely are.
        - Being the Same Status
            - Players: Two plus observing class
            - Setup: Scene space
            - Goal: Moment-by-moment mutual observation, probably the most important Status Game
            - Rules
                - Two players attempt to make the status gap as small as possible through a scene.
                - Coach interrupts occasionally asking the class 'Who is higher?' or 'Who is lower?'
                - Divided opinions mean success: congratulate the players and continue. If the class agrees Frank is higher than Tony, ask 'Which of them is wrong?', hoping to be told it is a silly question since either can restore the balance.
                - Once players discover mirroring solves it, place them in positions that don't allow mirroring, e.g. one sitting on the floor leaning back against the sofa the other is sitting on, positions you see at informal gatherings.
            - Variations: Anti-mirroring placements as above
            - Coaching: People are hardly ever equal in status except when forced into uniform and drilled, chorus lines and troops, so minimizing the gap is excellent training.
        - Fight for Your Number
            - Players: Three, or four, or three choosing between one and two only
            - Setup: Secret numbers, family casting: Dad, Mum and Mum's sister, or two teenagers and Gran
            - Goal: Model interactions between strangers and families, force status battles
            - Rules
                - Three students each secretly choose a number one to three: Ones most dominant, Twos less dominant, Threes least dominant.
                - Cast and play scenes in which each tries to achieve their secret number.
                - Ones should dominate everyone but prefer dominating a Two, battling other Ones. Twos need Threes to lord it over and Ones to look up to. Threes look up to everybody but long for a Two to relate to.
                - Expect sons elevated unwillingly to Number One by Mum, or three sisters all trying to out-submit each other.
            - Variations: Four-person group choosing one to three, or three-person group choosing either one or two, guaranteeing at least two players fighting for the same number
            - Coaching: Some Threes become so submissive they look as if they wandered in from another pecking order: tell them not to behave like Eights.
        - Self Up/Partner Down
            - Players: Two or more in conversation
            - Goal: Alternation strategies adding transitions, seeming more creative
            - Rules
                - Left alone students prefer working on their own status or on the partner's: alternate deliberately.
                - Self Up/Partner Down example: 'You're not handing in written homework are you? Why don't you use a computer. Look at my essay! You'd swear it was printed professionally. I shouldn't think your grubby little paragraph is going to impress anybody . . .'
                - Self Down/Partner Up example: 'You've got such a wonderful memory. I'll forget my own name one of these days. I'm boring you, aren't I? I don't have the gift of the gab like you!'
            - Coaching: Alternating adds transitions and makes you seem more creative.
        - Playing the Wrong Status
            - Players: Two
            - Setup: Relationship implying a large gap: teacher and child, mugger and victim, brain surgeon and patient
            - Goal: Comedy and insight from inverted interactive status
            - Rules
                - Choose the gap relationship, then play the opposite status.
                - Low-status mugger, high-status victim: 'Excuse me, miss. Er . . . Miss! Er . . .' / 'What are you muttering about?' / 'I'm a mugger! I . . . er . . . I mug people!' / 'I've read about this sort of thing . . .' / 'Give me your money! Er . . . hand it over!' / 'It's Ryan, isn't it? So this is how you show your gratitude for all those hours when I tried to teach you geography! Ungrateful boy!' / 'Auggghh!! My arm! It's broken!' / 'Just dislocated! Lie still, I'll fix it for you!'
                - Or pit a low-status hijacker against a high-status pilot: 'You want me to fly where?' / 'I'm sorry it . . . it's either Ohio or Iowa, I get them confused.' / 'You've no idea how to fire that gun, have you?' / 'Yes I do! And I'm prepared to use it!' / 'If you pull the trigger a flag will pop out. They sell them in the airport shop.'
            - Coaching: Peter Cook played a high-status beginner to Dudley Moore's low-status Welsh piano teacher: 'This note is A, and this is B and so on, up to G, and then we start again with A.' / 'Oh, I don't think that will do at all. We'll refer to this note on the far left as Number One . . .' / '[Gulp] Ah, well, perhaps you'd be so good as to press number twenty-two then . . .'
        - Passengers
            - Players: Three
            - Setup: Neutral third character added to any status scene
            - Goal: Complicate and stabilize status transactions
            - Rules
                - Take a two-person status premise, e.g. a high-status sister haranguing her low-status brother for not cleaning the apartment.
                - Add a passenger, here a woman he picked up in a bar, who stays neutral and tries to remain uninvolved.
            - Variations: King Game Passenger Version uses the master's lover, masseur, or language teacher
        - Low-status Trick Presentation
            - Players: Two alternating entrances, rest of class cheering, optional pianist
            - Setup: A flat either side of the acting area, pianist with loud circus music optional
            - Goal: Train presentation stripped of trick content: the trick is less important than the presentation
            - Rules
                - Tony enters from behind one flat, runs centre-stage, says 'Trick', leaves as Petra enters from the other side doing the same. Time it so there's no dead stage: overlap.
                - Round two: bow before and after you say 'Trick!' Class urged to cheer, applaud and shout 'Bravo!' throughout.
                - Run on with small steps demonstrating eagerness, and be so happy it's obvious you'd never dream of challenging their superiority. See them and bow, see them and say Trick, see them and bow again, and see them before leaving.
                - If the audience has no sensation of being seen, first play the Beep-Beep Game (p. 260), since being seen allows friendship signals and being pumped with inner delight at how wonderful the audience is.
                - With pianist: after the first bow raise a hand to stop the music. Pianist gives a drum roll while you prepare, rolling up sleeves, exercising fingers, taking deep breaths, conveying that you're about to attempt something very special and difficult, looking slightly worried about your chances of success.
                - Make them wait, absolutely, but not because you're high status: you're making them wait because the trick is so difficult and you want to do it well for them. Then say 'Trick', make a happy gesture of release, smile so we know the trick is over, pianist glissandoing back into circus music.
                - Wait until the split second after the drum-roll before deciding whether to do Johnstone's thumb trick or something spontaneous: stand on one leg, put your tongue out twisting an ear. Any trick passes as long as the build-up is well done.
                - Thumb trick: clasp your fist with the thumb inside, keep it still, hit it from underneath with your other fist letting the thumb shoot upright as if the blow created the effect. Perform it in front of the chest or to the side if costume jangles, never in front of the face where it confuses the image.
            - Variations: Give a simple real trick once the presentation is achieved
            - Coaching: Richardson Morgan mimed unsheathing a sword each entrance doing tricks like cutting his hand off, vanishing it into his sleeve, slicing a fly into eight pieces, knighting an audience member, swallowing the sword and extracting it from below: minutes passed with escalating ecstasy built on enthusiasm and understanding that the actual trick was of no importance. Correct presentation makes our cheering effortless, screwing it up forces us to shout Bravo. Now taught in clown schools with exactly the rules devised in the sixties.
        - Kinetic Dance Freeze Demonstration
            - Players: Whole class with two seated volunteers frozen
            - Setup: Chairs
            - Goal: Develop conscious awareness of the kinetic dance, the synchronized connectivities that restore agreed status
            - Rules
                - Background theory: touch your face for a moment and partners will almost certainly move to restore agreed status, rotating a foot inward, moving a thumb closer to fingers, or touching their own face, adaptations becoming obvious whenever someone enters or leaves a group.
                - Ask the class to freeze and maintain the direction their eyes look.
                - Pick two students sitting next to each other, keep them frozen while the rest walk to the opposite wall. The couple will look emotional: reluctant to make up after a quarrel, or very lonely, often one angry and one repentant, because they shared their space with people now gone.
                - Reverse the message by lifting one of them complete with chair and placing them on the opposite side: slight hostility flips to friendliness.
                - Say 'Okay, you can move', turn away thanking them, then ask 'What's happening now?': the frozen pair makes instant, effortless, unconscious adjustments until finding a correct relation to each other and to the group.
            - Variations: By this and similar tricks
            - Coaching: Connectivities are suppressed by grief, hostility and 'acting'. Absence of the dance tells coach and players something needs fixing. Onstage the relationship defaults to actor versus audience unless a character status relationship is achieved, at which point the dance kicks in and actors respond intuitively, no longer experiencing spectators as stern judges.
        - Party Endowments
            - Players: Four volunteers, preferably two men and two women
            - Setup: Party space with door, drinks, bookcase
            - Goal: Focus attention on the other players, create transitions, teach discretion
            - Rules
                - Each secretly endows the other three with qualities: 'There are three people on the stage with you. Decide that one is stupid, and that one is smelly, and that one is attractive.' Keep it secret. Treating someone as deaf doesn't make them deaf, and disagreements about people are ordinary: we can't even agree about flavours of ice-cream.
                - Cast one player as host, three guests entering at fifteen-second intervals so we can see what's happening, door locked so the host must answer it instead of shouting Come in.
                - It's a party because parties carry an obligation to be polite: no 'You smell like a fish!', find some more civilized way to solve the problem.
                - Forbid demonstrating attitudes: the host recoiling and gasping for air at the smelly guest gets 'Don't let him know that he's smelly! Be courteous!' Maximum permitted actions: open a window or say casually 'Came straight from work, did you?' Leave to answer the phone, or make introductions to escape. Guest contemptuous toward a host endowed idiot gets 'You're endowing your host with being an idiot, but you accepted the invitation. Can't you deal with stupid people without offending them?' Blatant admiration of the sexy guest gets 'Be afraid of being rejected!' or 'Don't be thought a slut!'
                - Concealment side-coaching: 'Conceal your feelings! Move your chair just half an inch!' A smelly person endowing you with attractiveness may be hard to shake off: find something interesting on the bookcase, go admire the view. Only be rude if someone is an out-and-out pest.
                - Purpose-giving side-coaching to convert chat into purposes: introduce people, offer drinks, 'Remember the present you left in the car!', 'Have a heart-attack!', 'Start a quarrel', 'Hold a seance!', 'Offer to help! Offer to grind the coffee! Remember something important that you need to tell them!', 'Find a way to get the attractive one to a bedroom!', 'Avoid the smelly one!', 'The funny one is getting funnier!'
                - For the funny endowment interrupt cautious laughter: ask players to point at the person they find amusing, then laugh next time that person speaks even at 'It's a nice day.' Restrain laughter demonstrated to the audience.
                - After the scene ask players to point to the sexy one and the smelly one and so on: vigorous laughing affirms it was just a game.
            - Variations: Once familiar replace stupidity with being amusing, giving sexy, smelly, funny. Alternate gibberish and English. Layer into Sandwiches. Move the party to a new locale such as a cinema queue or the park.
            - Coaching: Endowment menu offered: a reputation for hitting people at parties, easily shocked, tactless, nosy, rather delicate needing cosseting, physically fit energetic muscular, rich and desirable, makes snide remarks, unimportant and irritating, deeply religious, hypnotic personality, compulsive liar. Audience comprehension is unnecessary: 'If you're doing something that they can't fathom, you'll be a lot more interesting than if you held up a sign.' The kinetic dance locks in when players endow each other with qualities and vanishes instantly when they think I'll be the sexy one.
        - Endow Everything
            - Players: Three or four, use three for the shifting version
            - Setup: Repeat a Party Endowments scene with the same endowments
            - Goal: Contrasting attitudes to objects and topics generating transitions and aliveness
            - Rules
                - Replay the scene with the same person-endowments plus having an attitude to whatever else you relate to.
                - Dull route: similar attitude to everything, dirty sofa, holes in the carpet, depressing view, wallpaper needing replacement, stale biscuits.
                - Interesting route: choose contrasts: old but comfy sofa, tasteful wallpaper, coffee-table like the one you have at home, rather squalid area with magnificent view, creating a transition every time you switch attention.
                - Side-coach topics: 'Praise the movie! Change expression when ice-hockey is mentioned! Have an attitude to dogs!'
            - Variations: After practice shift attitudes to each person mid-scene: let the smelly one gradually become attractive, the boring one saintly. Move the party to a new locale: cinema queue, the park.
            - Coaching: Use three players, four is too complex.
        - Sandwiches
            - Players: Four standing in a circle
            - Setup: Party Endowments skills already rehearsed
            - Goal: Stack multiple simultaneous games into one scene
            - Rules
                - Standing in a circle announce: 'Point to the person on your left. This person is higher status than you. Now point to the person on your right: this person is lower status than you. The remaining person is the same status as you.'
                - Play a scene using the same endowments as before plus these status endowments, six qualities per player, three already practised.
                - Interesting combinations arise: endowing your unusually attractive partner with low status, a smelly player with high status.
                - Deeper layers: attitude to every topic brought up, moving from one emotional state to another, sustaining a body-image such as heavy sustained and indirect from Fast-Food Laban, pursuing a Stanislavskian objective.
            - Variations: Combine Fast-Food Stanislavsky with Fast-Food Laban (pp. 283-301), or with Fight for Your Number
            - Coaching: Such sandwiches sound insanely complicated yet we achieve them effortlessly in life.
        - The King Game
            - Players: One master, successive servants, one timekeeper
            - Setup: Master on a throne or comfortably arranged on a sofa with cushions, grapes and other comforts
            - Goal: Explore maximum-gap status transactions, compete for longest servant survival, produce exquisitely attentive play
            - Rules
                - Rule announced to the master: if anything irritates you no matter how minor, snap your fingers and your servant commits suicide, to be instantly replaced ad infinitum.
                - Appoint a timekeeper: 'We'll call fifteen seconds good! And twenty seconds excellent! Anyone like to try for twenty-five?' This gives masters permission to be severe.
                - Masters kill on impulse. Servants strive to make being onstage a pleasure.
                - Interrupt forgiving masters and point out the moments of irritation: 'You wanted to kill him after three seconds because his demeanour challenged you . . . Then you wanted to kill him because he cringed . . . because he asked you what you wanted . . . because he passed right in front of you on the way to the drinks cabinet . . . because he took a position behind you that made you feel uncomfortable. How will our servants learn if you're so forgiving?' Read the master's body: biting lip and frowning is unhappiness so kill, shirting about on the throne means snap, elbow shoved into your face means don't give her a chance, she's been pestering you from the moment she stepped onstage.
                - Named servant crimes: big steps and waving arms like a scullery-maid brought in while the regular servant is ill, standing over the master so uncomfortably that he crosses his leg away, the unsuccessful James Bond move, holding eye contact forcing the master to look away, asking if there is anything you want which forces a decision, since servants are paid to take trivial decisions away from masters.
                - Servant advice list: enter with a purpose. Never say 'Is there anything you need, ma'am?' Be physically and vocally discreet. Convey that the space belongs to the master and intrude only when necessary. Don't look servile or as if waiting to be punished. Don't out-stare, meaning out-status, the master. Don't leave awkward pauses. Giving a newspaper, serving coffee or breakfast may help but survival needs something of dramatic interest happening. Winning strategies: purposeful business like opening curtains or reporting an assassination plot, or discovering the jester role as entertainer or confidant, the clown in King Lear, John Brown tipping with Queen Victoria at Balmoral.
            - Variants: Die at the second finger-snap or third, saved until interest needs increasing: servants shocked by the first snap continue in exactly the same suicidal way. Pecking-order Version: once a servant survives indefinitely call in an under-servant killable by either servant or master, timing under-servants too. Degenerate Version where the master tells servants what to do and kills poor performance is pointless. Passenger Version: add the master's lover, masseur, or language teacher.
            - Coaching: Ask servants why they were killed: usually no idea, and masters often don't know either. Frame effort kindly: palace servants take months or years before serving the king at banquet, this takes only an hour or so. The real secret is to involve the master in an interesting situation, not just to serve: offering mimed drinks or foot-massages helps but drama sustains survival.
        - Master-Servant
            - Players: Two or more
            - Setup: Formal service setting, tea and cucumber sandwiches
            - Goal: Express parent-child material in disguised form so players work out personal traumas without realizing it, excellent with children
            - Rules
                - Create a platform by having the servant serve the master formally.
                - Break the routine of serving: 'Thank-you, Perkins.' / 'Might I have a word with you, sir?' / 'Fire away!' / 'I left Sammy's bowl on the record-player at speed thirty-three and a third, and when I came back you had him on forty-five.' / 'I was just trying to build up his muscle.' / 'Build up his muscle? The water was practically boiling.' / 'You exaggerate, Perkins!' / 'You just wait till I run your bath for you!'
                - Present spectators hoping personal material cracks the formality: 'Was that my daughter I heard in your room last evening, Perkins?' or 'Have you been reading my private diary, sir?' or 'Why didn't you tell me I was your son?'
                - Core principle: it's a mistake to assume master equals high status and servant low, confusing social with interactive status. The servant can beat the master while still looking like the servant: whatever status you play, always treat the space, the territory, as belonging to the master.
            - Variations: Blind Offer Version: the master snaps his or her fingers, gives no verbal orders, and the servant always knows what to do, the response always right, building a scene this way. Piggy-Back: masters use servants as horses, entering and leaving riding piggy-back: a good master and good servant play a scene ending in a gallop off to the park, a bad pair does the same thing, both pairs meet at the park and quarrel, one master challenges the other to a joust using boxing gloves on poles, perhaps injury or love, or quarrels without jousting and two further separate Master-Servant scenes before meeting again to fight the duel. Give the masters balloons to beat their servants with (p. 259).
            - Coaching: Piggy-back: take care not to injure anyone.
        - Slow-motion Commentary
            - Players: One mover, one commentator, optionally a colour-person
            - Setup: Stage space, sports-commentary frame
            - Goal: Variety, present-moment bodily attention, hindering players from searching ahead for good ideas
            - Rules
                - Put one player onstage moving in extreme slow-motion, another commentates on the spectacle. In all slow-motion work the body's centre of gravity must stay in motion no matter how minutely: even one finger moving less than an inch every five seconds needs connection to the body's centre, making movements integrated rather than robotic parts moving one at a time.
                - The commentator mixes modes: sometimes adding trivia, sometimes going mad with excitement, sometimes deploring imaginary infractions, sometimes falling silent as if nothing could be added, letting the event speak for itself.
                - Imaginary trivia cards for competitors: 'Ron Harris is wearing the red jock-strap that has become his most famous distinguishing mark,' or 'Plucky little Audrey Swinburne's father took time off from the local glue factory to supervise her training . . . her swimsuit was knitted by her eighty-two-year-old grandmother.'
                - Colour-person usually plays an expert or the athlete's trainer. Door-entry example: 'Oh . . . and I think something's happening. I can see a slit of light around the edge of the door . . . Difficult to be sure as the knob is entirely circular. What do you think, Kevin?' / 'I think it must be turning, Monty, otherwise the Judges would have counted this competitor out by now.'
                - Commentary can direct the player: 'I don't think he's set himself up properly for this move . . . He's about to give himself a most tremendous crack on the head in about three minutes' time . . .' possibly closing 'Rather a slow start at the International Entering A Room competition here at Chutney. And now it's time for these important messages . . .'
                - Commentator guidelines: allow the event to speak for itself. Get excited and build climaxes even though no climax exists. Explain finer points of the game, infractions and so on. Supply background information on characters or things peripheral to the event. His full Frank Quimby Barnsley Toad-Swallowing commentary demonstrates mode-switching with long pauses, judge disputes over freezing with terror, crowd fighting, trainer time-outs overruled, and a baulk predicted minutes early, all while Frank inclines his head one inch baring his teeth slightly.
            - Variations: Two or more players moving in slow-motion. Commentator supplies his own colour-person and so on using different voices. Wider Applications: lay commentary onto normal-speed scenes, e.g. two players trying to keep status as close as possible: 'She's flashing her top teeth at him, Tom.' / 'Yes, she's definitely lowering her status by that move. He'll have to respond to that if he wants to keep the balance. Yes, he's putting his hand to his face.' / 'Her blink-rate has increased, Tom, and she's a little breathless when she speaks. He'll have to compensate.' Such commentary can side-coach in ways too intrusive otherwise: 'that's the third idea he's killed. This scene is becoming weighed down by a relentless negativity. What do you think, Nigel?'
            - Coaching: Expect to shout 'Slow down! Do less! Move imperceptibly!' Whenever the commentator hints something is imminent players speed up unless restrained: the elegant performer maintains exactly the same pace, a refusal to accelerate that can be magical. Reject the New Zealand game-sheet description of commentators talking as quickly as possible: real-life commentators would be fired for such intrusion. Players must allow gaps so comments can be inserted, calming rattled beginners, but commentators must be experts so practise the skills in class rather than public. Benchmark: Ben Benison and Rick Morgan mimed a table-setting at the Mercury Theatre Notting Hill in 1969 lasting eight timed minutes with no music and no commentary, audience enthralled.
        - Verse
            - Players: Two or more, spectators may prompt rhymes
            - Setup: Spoken couplets
            - Goal: Force abandonment of control of the future, get sucked into adventures beyond conscious control
            - Rules
                - Say any line that comes to you and trust that either you or your partner finds a second line, or someone prompts you: spectators laugh when a rhyme is bad, applaud an apt one.
                - Accept whatever the mind dredges up. His chain: 'Well, here I am on holiday.' / 'I think I'll row across the bay!' Rowing may have been the last thing in your mind, but go with it. / 'Here's what I need, a little boat.' The next line eludes you while mentally cycling stoat, cantaloupe and grope, someone shouts Float: 'I think I'll see if it will float!' / 'Oh dear, it seems the boat is sinking, / I cannot swim' saved by someone calling 'And I've been drinking!' then 'A mermaid sees my horrid plight, / And keeps me in her bed all night': launched unintentionally into Little Mermaid.
                - Spectators see you allowing verse to control you and admire your courage launching onto a sea of couplets.
            - Variations: Popular challenges and forfeits in Micetro and Gorilla Theatre: best scene in verse, limerick based on an audience member's name, best epic poem lasting until the audience begs for mercy. Verse spoken or sung.
            - Coaching: We live in a verseless culture compared with Elizabethan England or Sei Shonagon's Japan: ask students to learn limericks so they at least know the form, then offer travesties before originals: 'Forgive me please, you corpse with bleeding wounds / That I am courteous to your murderers' beside Mark Antony's 'Oh pardon me thou bleeding piece of earth, / That I am meek and gentle with these butchers.' Or 'Heavenly powers, come and keep us safe!' beside Hamlet's 'Angels and Ministers of Grace, defend us!' Even the dullest student likely feels the increase of power, some get hooked.
        - The Klutz
            - Players: One solo, or improviser with audience volunteer
            - Setup: Stage space
            - Goal: Laughable repeated self-injury
            - Rules
                - Pretend to hurt yourself, recover gradually, then hurt yourself again.
                - Sound matters more than physicality: scream and say 'I can't move!' and audiences see cramp or a slipped disc although you've done nothing physical.
                - Pain must be exaggerated to demonstrate it isn't real or the laughter stops immediately, unless they hate you.
                - Follow W. C. Fields: hide the pain with embarrassment, and the more you do that the better they like it.
                - Distinguish fast-pain, a jab with a pin, from slow-pain, a crushed foot. Suggested injuries unlikely to cause serious harm: bite tongue, be stung by an insect, slap leg harder than intended, electric shock, poke self in eye, elbow slipping off table so the head your hand was supporting falls to the table making the bang with the other hand, catching one foot behind the heel of the other while walking then trying not to trip, hand stuck in jug or vase, hurting a tooth biting something, bumping into furniture.
            - Variations: Theatre Machine travelling version played with an audience volunteer: improviser ends crippled but eager to interact. Derek Flores park-bench scene: audience volunteer asked to use the 'bad things happen to everyone I make friends with' tilt, Flores injuring himself again and again while persuading her it was coincidence, giving the impression of a man so driven by carnal desire he'd walk through fire.
            - Coaching: Knock-about comedians compare bruises but comedy lies in what happens after the pain. Taller players suffer greater bodily strain, shorter ones can learn spectacular falls: study tumbling with a good trainer.
        - Being Concussed
            - Players: Two, e.g. servant and master, or balloon games
            - Setup: Falls and impacts
            - Goal: Confusion releasing laughter more than impact
            - Rules
                - A servant takes the master's hand and throws himself into a forward roll intending to look thrown by the master: unless he pretends to be disorientated it seems like a gymnastic trick.
                - Hit by a balloon and falling as if hit by an iron bar: look in the wrong direction as you scramble to your feet. The bafflement and double-takes release laughter more than the blow or the crash.
                - Case study: Ben Benison hit an audience volunteer, a gymnast, who jerked into the air, rotated horizontal and crashed onto concrete: horrifying, but had he clambered up staring in all directions it would have been hysterically funny.
            - Coaching: Childhood trampoline clowns: two were funny, the third copying them wasn't, and he was the one who never looked confused. When clowns fall or are biffed we may be laughing at their changed attitude to the space.
        - Staying with It (Not Advancing)
            - Players: One performer with coach, or pairs: performer plus trainer
            - Setup: A precisely defined action
            - Goal: Extemporize on whatever you're doing at the split second of command irrespective of plans, attending to the present
            - Rules
                - Coach says 'Make the activity more interesting.' Catch evasions: feeding a dog becomes shouting for a runaway dog, reading a newspaper becomes dialling to cancel a picnic, being negative so nothing happens. Insist on staying with the activity: find the news item difficult to read and take out a magnifying glass, gasp with horror and justify it, the friend murdered in the item, add disgusted or interested sounds, read the item aloud, mime showing it to the audience. Let extra ideas float in one ear and out the other.
                - Boiled egg elaborations: mime tapping the egg with your spoon and hear it tap back, attack it with a bread-knife, pick it up and bash it on the table, attack with bread-knife and succeed, levering the flap up very slowly and suspiciously and receiving a squirt in the eye as the egg seals itself, wrestling the lid against something trying to keep it closed from inside.
                - Select details from running scenes: 'Wait! What are you doing at this exact moment?' 'We're going to put a worm on the hook!' 'That's your intention, what are you actually doing?' 'Getting the worm out of the tin.' 'See how long you can keep us interested in that activity.' They shake the tin, poke a finger in and get bitten, stick on a Band-Aid, order the worm to leave the tin, tempt it with a voluptuous finger, hypnotize it and get hypnotized instead, threaten it with a revolver.
                - Define actions broadly: 'brushing hair' could include brushing other people's hair or a giant's, 'scratching my back' includes a chair-leg or rubbing against a tree but forbids scratching someone else's back, 'swimming' includes breast-stroke, waterfall, drowning, emerging in heaven in an ocean of champagne. Waiting for a friend at a bus stop permits phoning to find out what happened: phoning a taxi is allowed if the action was defined as waiting. Brushing teeth when a tooth drops out: brush the tooth.
                - Elegant selection: notice what is already happening: breathing, growing older, touching your nose. Charlie Rivel extended preparing to sit on a chair for minutes without getting his behind onto the seat.
                - Speed training in pairs: performer defines then elaborates an action, trainer gives encouragement and stops digressions, suggesting ideas when the performer dries, sharing the trouble. Breathing sequence: breathe more fully, add a contented 'ahhhhhh' on the exhale, mime opening a window exulting in fresh air, add resistance, pant and take little gasps, suffocate, push ribs in and out manually, crawl to a table, open a drawer, seize a knife, perform a tracheotomy on yourself.
                - Universal expanders: add dialogue or change emotion. Stroking a cat: explain to the audience how it soothes you, how sensual it is, or change to rage: 'This cat is so spoiled. Who strokes me? Who gets my meals? Who lets me sleep in a patch of sunlight all day!'
                - Waiting-for-instructions expansion: phoning someone, asking the audience for advice, praying to God, consulting encyclopedias, shouting 'What am I supposed to do?', looking through scraps of paper, trying activities at random, weeping in despair, slashing wrists and having God say 'That's right, Sidney!' while screaming 'But I'm not Sidney! I'm Philip! What are Philip's instructions?' Character out of Beckett or Kafka.
            - Coaching: Bodies are in the present, so this procedure hinders searching ahead. Held to one activity, silent-movie and cartoon ideas present themselves automatically. Command issued at the moment you drop an egg generates Keaton chains: grab, slip, dive just before it hits, knock it out the window accidentally, bird flies off with it, motorbike pursuit, truck slams into you, grab the egg hurtling through air. Make one small activity more interesting and you'll seem a natural comedian.
        - Advancing the Activity
            - Players: One performer plus trainer or coach
            - Setup: Follow-on drill from Staying with It
            - Goal: Expand small excursions into unanticipated new activities
            - Rules
                - Players staying with an action inevitably take excursions: stopping a bleeding finger might include running for a first-aid box, kept short or it reads as advancing.
                - Trainer watches, then: 'What are you doing now?' 'Trying to open the egg.' 'Weren't you sighing?' 'Was I?' 'Make "sighing" more interesting.' She has advanced from failing to open the egg to sighing.
                - The new action must also be made interesting: sighing leads to suicidal despair with tearful verbalization about a life being a series of fiascos including not being able to open an egg, planning a really interesting suicide such as battering herself to death with the egg.
                - Trainer shouts 'Advance!' as she glances at it: 'What are you doing?' 'Looking at the egg.' 'Make "looking at the egg" more interesting.'
            - Coaching: Advances chain indefinitely, each new activity demanding its own elaboration.

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 10 Procedures, balloons and audiences #book:storytellers #audience
        - Hitting with Balloons
            - Players: Pairs (soon framed as master and servant), or a trainer working with a student
            - Setup: Long 'airship' balloons that inflate to a couple of feet long and inches in diameter, ideally about a yard long and three or four inches across as the Theatre Machine's were, not the long thin balloons used for making balloon animals. Keep a large stock at the sides of the stage and backstage, the Theatre Machine had a hundred or more, sometimes stuck to the walls by static electricity, and sometimes destroyed most of them in a single performance.
            - Goal: Invigorating, safe slapstick. Balloons are brightly coloured, move strangely, and are 'safe' and 'dangerous' at the same time, exploding loudly but harmlessly, and the long ones are subliminally erotic. They are an excellent replacement for slap-sticks (two thin strips of slightly separated board that made a loud slap), which required padded victims and still risked hitting with the edge. Hitting with balloons gave Master-Servant scenes a new vitality.
            - Rules
                - You strike someone with a long balloon and he/she reacts as if struck by an iron bar, but recovers a lot sooner.
                - No padding is needed, and if the balloon bursts you can simply be handed another one.
                - Keep playing, treating every blow as an iron-bar blow with quick recovery, and restock balloons as they burst.
            - Coaching: A Need for Caution: some balloons contain a pinch of talcum powder to stop the rubber sticking to itself. Deposits of talc are usually contaminated with asbestos (one commercial sample of baby powder was found to be 38 per cent asbestos), so if you find talc in your mouth or on your lips after inflating a balloon, wash your mouth out. Consider buying a balloon pump. If a student has a fear of balloons, take the class outside where there are no walls to reflect the sound, and note that so much laughter is released by balloon-hitting that nervous students should be easy to desensitize.
        - Hitting with Entry
            - Players: Pairs, first striking their own forearms, then each other
            - Setup: Airship balloons
            - Goal: Teaches hitting through the object rather than at the surface. Human beings instinctively strike at the surface of other people as a way to assert status without doing serious damage, so entry has to be taught or the balloons will just be tapped against the partner.
            - Rules
                - 'Hit your forearms with the balloon. Try to burst it with the force of the blow!'
                - Notice that the intention to burst the balloon forces a firm grip (instead of just letting it waggle), and that it becomes obvious the balloon cannot hurt anyone however hard you strike.
                - 'Now strike your partner with the intention of smashing the balloon right through them.'
                - Carry this into Master-Servant scenes, where masters who seem 'mediocre' become demonic, and inspired, if they hit with entry.
            - Coaching: Entry means hitting through the object, as in those martial arts demonstrations where the head is accelerated to a point beyond a concrete block, trusting that it's the concrete that will shatter.
        - Restraint
            - Players: Master-servant pairs, with a trainer intervening physically and verbally
            - Setup: One balloon per master
            - Goal: Converts beginner hitting (beating carpets, suitable only for ending a Benny Hill sketch) into dramatically valuable blows, each with a separate build. If masters struggle not to hit their servants, the balloon becomes a gauge that exquisitely records the master's feelings.
            - Rules
                - 'Give each hit a separate build-up!'
                - When masters try to deliver a shower of follow-on blows, grab their arms if necessary: 'That's enough! One hit only. Now beg your servant's forgiveness!'
                - Play it so the balloon seems to be getting enraged while the master tries to calm it down: hold it behind the back as if to keep it out of the argument, let it vibrate and take little swipes at the air as it works its way round to the front again, raise it to strike and then force it down with both hands as if trying to press it under water.
                - Some masters beat themselves in their rage, or destroy their balloon, only to be instantly handed another.
                - The moment to hit is when the balloon can no longer contain its violence: a good hit is like a sneeze, something that comes after a 'build'.
                - If the hit is delayed too long and 'misses the wave', let the tension subside so you can start building to a fresh climax.
                - Once you've hit your servant (against your better judgment), apologize, make amends, and start building the balloon's rage again until it is impelled to lash out once more.
            - Coaching: Masters can easily make themselves enraged (they love negative emotions) but then their balloons seem quite 'dead'.
        - The Wheel of Tension
            - Players: Anyone for the clapping demonstration, then a master with a servant while the trainer controls the release
            - Setup: Balloons for the master-servant phase
            - Goal: Awareness of the inner rhythm we are normally unaware of, and training the moment of hitting to be unpredictable rather than metronomic.
            - Rules
                - Clap your hands, then wait (without counting) perhaps twenty or thirty seconds until there comes a moment when you know it's time to clap again, as if a huge wheel were revolving slowly at the edge of your consciousness and prompting one clap at each rotation.
                - Trainers interfere with this regularity, insisting that the moment of hitting should be unpredictable, and teach the skill by having the moment of release decided by the trainer.
                - Drive a master through a coached build such as: 'Not yet. Get angrier. Don't hit him. HIT NOW! Once! ONLY ONCE! Back, back. Help her up, dust her down, tidy her clothing. Now remember why you hit her and vibrate the balloon a bit. Calm the balloon down. Pour her a brandy, notice that half the brandy is missing! Get enraged but try not to show it. Ask where the brandy has gone. Realize that your servant has been stealing your brandy. Let the balloon go berserk. DON'T HIT HER! IT'S TOO LATE. Let the emotion subside a little. HIT HER AGAIN! NOW HIT HER AGAIN... Ask her to get you your tranquillizers. Make her swallow a handful...' and so on (see Spasms, p. 314).
        - Balloon Fights
            - Players: Whole group for the elimination fights, then master-servant pairs
            - Setup: Airship balloons, open space, and mats or carpet once real falling begins
            - Goal: Desensitizes balloon-shy performers, and trains balance, physical control, breath control, and the receiving of blows.
            - Rules
                - Set up 'sword-fights' with the balloons, en masse, until only one person is left standing, everyone stabbed by a balloon counting as 'dead'.
                - Try sword-fights in slow-motion, and listen for the 'tap' of a balloon hitting someone, which means it was speeding up to tap the surface. Side-coach: 'Slow-motion! Think of pressing the balloon into the body! Keep the centre of the body in motion, not just the arm that you intend to strike with, or you'll look like a railway signal.'
                - If you're slain, be smashed to the floor in slow-motion and bounce a few times, but don't speed up, there shouldn't be a sound as you hit the floor.
                - Place blows so they can be 'received': don't strike the knees from in front, strike from the rear so the legs can buckle, keep moving the balloon after contact with increasing pressure, thrust in slow-motion at the stomach so the servant grasps the balloon and bends over and can grip it until it bursts or makes a horrid wrenching noise as it's pulled away, strike at one shoulder so the servant can spin around, or hit straight down so the servant staggers about clutching her head or falls straight to the floor, but avoid the side of the head because a balloon must not explode beside someone's ear.
                - For servants who sillyly speed up to evade the slow-motion balloon, teach them to grimace with increasing horror as it moves inexorably towards their face, holding their heads still if necessary.
                - Get students to play scenes or 'fence' in slow-motion using slow dialogue: 'Taaaake thaaaaaaat yooouuuuu swiiinnnnne!' answered by 'Oooooohhh, noooooooo. Auuuuuggghhhhh!'
                - Once slow-motion hitting is mastered, ask students to speed up their interactions and have hit servants fall over, using mats or carpet, letting fit and healthy students do spectacular falls, but employing a really good tumbling instructor if you want them to stay fit and healthy, and remembering that the cries of pain and the disorientation matter more than the actual fall.
                - Most servants prefer to stand just beyond hitting range (as if the balloons really were iron bars), so tell the masters, 'Make the servants come to you! Don't go to them!' and tell the servants, 'Stand close. Try to be within three feet of the master. You're allowed to duck when you're swiped at, but you're not to avoid the blow by increasing the distance.'
            - Variations: Mass elimination sword-fights, slow-motion fights and scenes with slow dialogue, and full-speed fights with falls on mats.
            - Coaching: Moving and falling in slow-motion is excellent for balance and physical control, and the slow dialogue is excellent for breath control. There's something comical (and perhaps political) about the servant who tries to avoid being hit, and yet who always stands in the line of fire.
        - A Master-Servant Balloon-hitting Scene
            - Players: Two, a master (Sir) and a servant (Perkins)
            - Setup: A balloon for the master, room to strike and dive to the floor
            - Goal: Keeps the threat of violence alive so the scene stays charged, and holds both master and servant to the present moment.
            - Rules
                - Improvise on this model: Sir calls 'Perkins!', Perkins answers 'Sir?', and Sir demands 'Didn't I forbid you ever to be taller than me?'
                - Perkins argues back (all the servants going about on their hands and knees is stupid, the maids can't make the beds unless you go upstairs with them), the balloon becoming extremely agitated, ending on 'just because you're short!'
                - 'Short!' Sir strikes Perkins to the floor, is overcome with remorse ('Good God! What a brute I am'), helps Perkins up, accepts reassurance ('No harm done, sir'), confesses 'I've such a terrible temper', and when his balloon begins to vibrate snaps 'Are you contradicting me?'
                - On the answer the balloon becomes more alive, Sir sits suddenly on the floor, Perkins dives to the carpet, Sir stands and claims 'Just testing you, Perkins!', the balloon calming, and asks for the Increase Your Height book from the top shelf.
                - As the balloon trembles again and takes little swipes in the air while Sir appears to be restraining it forcibly ('I don't keep a dog and bark myself'), Perkins offers 'Perhaps you'd like to climb up with me, sir?' then produces a book 'like a flash'.
                - Sir discovers it's Ballroom Dancing for Midgets from the bottom shelf ('Don't lie to me!'), smashes Perkins down mad with rage, is horrified by his own violence, and asks 'Are you all right, old chap?', Perkins answering 'Oh yes, sir, just startled me a bit', and so on.
            - Coaching: It helps to roar or shriek or scream when hitting or being hit. Even if the master always manages to restrain the balloon, the threat keeps the scene alive and holds both master and servant to the present moment.
        - The Spontaneous Balloon
            - Players: Any performer wielding a balloon (typically the master)
            - Setup: An airship balloon tied with a 'gimmicked' knot: loop the neck as usual but insert the mouth only about a third of the way through the loop, so the pressure of the air and the friction of the rubber make a tight seal that you can still deflate at will. Inserted less than a millimetre or so into the loop, it becomes a spontaneous balloon that might discharge at any moment.
            - Goal: Comic deflation, giving the audience great pleasure as the master is reduced to flapping a little strip of rubber impotently in the air.
            - Rules
                - Inflate and tie the balloon with the gimmicked knot.
                - Arrange the knot so it sits at the end that hits the servant.
                - Strike: the balloon is quite likely to deflate on impact, whooshing away and leaving the master waving the limp strip of rubber.
            - Variations: Insert the mouth a third of the way into the loop for a reusable balloon you can deflate and save for another day, or less than a millimetre in for one that might discharge at any moment (in Johnstone's childhood house, balloons would suddenly whoosh about and his parents never understood why).
        - Beep-Beep
            - Players: One speaker addressing a group of from six to eight seated people
            - Setup: Seats for the group, nothing else
            - Goal: Replaces the alienating 'eye-slide' (freezing your gaze so it slides off a passing stranger, avoiding the status transaction of breaking eye contact) with 'recognition behaviour' (raising eyebrows, widening eyes, smiling, enlarged pupils), the way a rock star flashing recognition signals into a dark arena makes thousands feel 'seen'. It trains improvisers to relate comfortably and good-naturedly to audiences faster than anything else Johnstone dreamed up, fixing the habit of talking to side walls, to the gap between stalls and dress-circle, or to an imaginary audience floating a few feet overhead.
            - Rules
                - The speaker addresses the seated group, each member of which raises a hand and then begins to lower it slowly until the speaker makes genuine eye contact, at which point the hand returns to its first position and starts descending again.
                - If a hand descends low enough to touch the thigh, its owner says, 'Beep! Beep!' until genuine eye contact is made.
                - The speaker's task is to prevent or minimize these interruptions.
                - Open with, 'It's impossible to win this game, because the "audience" has such a great desire to say, "Beep! Beep!" that they'll speed up!', which gets everyone laughing, gives the spectators 'permission' to accelerate, and reassures the speaker of not being singled out as a 'loser'.
                - Turn to the group: 'If you feel "seen", please return your hands to their starting position and begin lowering them again.'
                - For nervous speakers who can't talk coherently at first, offer crutches: 'Just concentrate on the eye contacts!', or 'Count to a hundred out loud,' or 'Describe our physical appearance.'
                - After several speakers, raise the cheating issue: speakers complain that hands kept lowering despite eye contact, and the answer is that looking into their eyes isn't enough, they had no experience of 'being seen', you have to let them know that you 'saw' them (which is why Groucho Marx gave himself big, thick eyebrows and learned to waggle them up and down so quickly).
            - Coaching: Many speakers make themselves look 'dead', as if a formal photograph was being taken, but suppressing the beep-beeps makes them more expressive: they have to smile, or tilt their heads, or waggle their eyebrows, which makes them seem 'nicer' and friendlier and more alive. Groups who teach impro to managers of oil companies say the Beep-Beep Game is one of the quickest ways to turn corporate 'zombies' into human beings, and a few minutes' training can give permanent results.
        - Addressing an Audience
            - Players: Solo speaker
            - Setup: A theatre with stalls, dress-circle and upper-circle (Johnstone's example is the Royal Court in Sloane Square, London, where in his day the lights were dazzling so you only saw the first few rows)
            - Goal: Makes the whole audience experience themselves as 'being seen', so they warm to the speaker and feel in 'safe hands'.
            - Rules
                - Divide the front stalls into sections one, two, three and four, and the rear stalls into sections five, six and seven.
                - Slice the dress-circle into sections eight, nine and ten, and the upper-circle into sections eleven, twelve and thirteen.
                - Stand downstage and, as you speak to the audience, let your attention switch unpredictably from section to section, giving a recognition signal each time.
                - Run through sequences like One, Three, Eight, Thirteen, Nine, Four, Ten, Two, Eleven, Five, Twelve, Six and Seven so that no one can ever guess which part of the house you will address next.
            - Coaching: This scales the Beep-Beep recognition signals to a full house: the Elizabethans took pleasure in speaking directly to the audience, and performers unblocked by architecture (Ken Dodd, Dame Edna) prove the problem lies in the eye-slide, not the proscenium arch.

    - Impro for Storytellers 1999, ch 12 to 14 Character work #book:storytellers #character
        - Changing The Body Image
            - Players: Solo or whole group
            - Setup: Open training space, no equipment unless a trigger needs it (masks, makeup, card strips)
            - Goal: Generate character by altering the body image until the self transforms
            - Rules
                - Pick a physical trigger and sustain it while moving and speaking. Triggers from the text:
                - Mouth as small as possible while speaking loudly (becomes a tyrant). Top teeth exposed, biting bottom lip, head pushed forward with a giggle (submissive creature).
                - Both rows of teeth bared, breath hissing audibly, eyes wide, aware of peripheral space.
                - Michael Chekhov: place the mind in a body part or outside it, e.g. a silver ball in the chest radiating light (Siegfried hero), or a small hard black steely ball in one eye, then move while sustaining the sensation.
                - Viola Spolin: adjectives on one colour of paper strips, body parts on another. Pair arbitrarily: happy feet, silly bottom, proud chest, nasty nose. Try two at a time (crazy fingers with lustful lips, embarrassed knees with sensual mouth). Play families sharing angry chins and slow thighs while onlookers guess the qualities.
                - Wear a mask sustaining whatever sound it suggests. White face paint and meet the stranger in the mirror. Makeup, wigs, fake moustaches, false cheeks.
                - Turn inarticulate sounds into a voice that is not yours. Ape your pet. Imitate your parents. Go disguised into the real world and play scenes in public.
            - Variations: Whole families share the same quality, guessing games for the audience, public disguise outings
            - Coaching: All body-image work fixes attention on the present. Disguised play must leave people in a better state than you found them, and avoid discovery or arrest.
        - Machines
            - Players: 4 or more, ideally whole team
            - Setup: Empty stage
            - Goal: Group rhythm and desensitization for shy players. Johnstone rates it useless as spectacle
            - Rules
                - Player 1 stands and repeats one gesture and one sound in a mechanical loop.
                - Player 2 joins alongside with a different gesture and sound, synchronized into the whole.
                - Further players add on until a complete machine wheezes, hisses and chugs.
            - Variations: Build the machine for a "new employee" to operate, Chaplin-style ingestion included. Assemble an oracle machine that answers a question one word at a time
            - Coaching: Not recommended for public use. Building a machine merely as an effect is cowardly because there is no risk, and as a challenge both teams built similar machines, doubly tedious
        - People Machines
            - Players: Half dozen volunteers plus one subject
            - Setup: Class that already knows the Machine Game and knows each other well
            - Goal: Sharpen observation of real people, encourage mimicry patience, bond the group
            - Rules
                - Construct a "Keith" machine: each component performs one characteristic gesture and sound of the subject.
                - Components enter one at a time, never all together.
                - Move on to machines based on other teachers or celebrities. Study celebrities on videotape.
                - Only then invite students to volunteer themselves as subjects.
            - Variations: The finished machine spreads the subject across the stage like a cubist painting
            - Coaching: Never let players volunteer each other. One imitator gives offence, a whole group doing it does not. Almost nobody sustains imitation study alone, this game supplies the encouragement
        - Being Animals
            - Players: Entire group
            - Setup: Open space
            - Goal: Dissolve shyness and bodily-contact taboos, then build covert animal-spirit characters
            - Rules
                - Answer to "how much human?": be so like the animal we would lock you up. Wolves wolf their food, kittens romp, lizards stare and flick tongues. Contact taboos drop, snakes slide over each other, cats rub. Stage Two (covert animals): 3. Be human beings with the spirit of an animal, avoiding anything that would get a human classified as insane.
                - Run mixed parties (snakes with mice, dogs with cats) where everyone is on best behaviour and obliged to be courteous.
                - Class votes for the best snake, bear, etc. Then play scenes casting the winners: the winning bear is a teacher interviewing a slithery parent, or a chicken answers a "companionship" advert and arrives at Mr Wolf's home.
            - Variations: Feeds directly into Mouse Police
            - Coaching: Physical transformation games fix attention on the body, bodies only exist in the present, so partners rapport well
        - Mouse Police
            - Players: 2 or more police (humans), 1 or more concealed animals, supporting household cast
            - Setup: Domestic scene hiding covert animals (graduates of Being Animals Stage Two)
            - Goal: Accelerate the covert conversion, and as an entertainment the animal MUST be caught
            - Rules
                - Animal Police (who are not that animal) arrive on a report that such animals have moved into the area.
                - They question, hunt clues, notice suspicious behaviour (excessive sibilants, not blinking, mouse patties in the fridge, a hole instead of basement stairs).
                - Householders stay covert, denying everything while leaking traits.
                - Cover blows, the caught animal goes completely animal (hysterical wriggling under furniture, tail around piano legs), police pin and haul away.
                - Afterbeat: householders lament the lodger they hardly knew.
            - Variations: Any animal police pairing, any household
            - Coaching: Great pleasure lies in watching total commitment the instant the cover blows. Without a catch the audience gets no pay-off
        - Obsessions
            - Players: 2 or more (one obsessed player plus straight partners)
            - Setup: Ordinary realistic scene, classic version is a homecoming
            - Goal: Play any feeling as an obsession by basing it on a sustained sound and backward attention
            - Rules
                - Choose the obsession: Instant Death (default teaching version), horror of physical contact, insane craving for contact, or any feeling you can find a sound for.
                - Sustain the emotional sounds the WHOLE scene: gasps, pants, screams, or disgusted yuks, or erotic moans. The sounds jerk the body about, the body must never be still while making them. Intersperse dialogue between sounds.
                - Attention leaps constantly and especially to the space BEHIND: worst terrors or delights lurk just outside peripheral vision. Each leap finds fresh hallucination: razor rising through floorboards, poison gas in the chair arm, arsenic wallpaper, pigeon from the bacterial warfare lab, vase that moved six inches.
                - Believe death is seconds away, never plan ahead. Thinking instead of reading kills the game.
                - Partner resists as a normal person (boyfriend told she has never been like this and must calm her down).
                - Grade responses: a dog-phobic alarms at leash, kennel, drawing or toy dog, but far less than a drooling Rottweiler.
            - Variations: Psychotic version for audience delight (their secret feelings personified), normal version stays just this side of insanity and is more useful to actors. Cross-casting: teacher with touch horror meets student with contact lust, give the student a resistance (fear of expulsion, awe)
            - Coaching: The secret is the gasp. Tell players to make the sound only AFTER they fail at pure imagination, so they understand its value
        - Fast-Food Laban
            - Players: 4 or 5 per group scene, later 2 per scene
            - Setup: Pin up the wall chart: WEIGHT (heavy/light), SPACE (direct/indirect), TIME (sudden/sustained). One pick per column gives eight effort actions
            - Goal: Transform players through a sustained movement quality legible to an audience
            - Rules
                - The eight combinations: Heavy-Direct-Sudden punching, Heavy-Direct-Sustained pushing, Heavy-Indirect-Sudden slashing, Heavy-Indirect-Sustained wringing, Light-Indirect-Sustained stroking, Light-Indirect-Sudden flitting, Light-Direct-Sudden dabbing, Light-Direct-Sustained smoothing. (Chart lives in the chapter text, Johnstone's cut-down of Laban's twenty-four items.)
                - Four or five players agree on one action. "You are the Punch family, everything you do or say has to be punchy. Decide relationships quickly, show us the Punch family coming down to breakfast." Father rips the newspaper opening it, Mum slams dishes down.
                - Once skilled, play scenes in twos with OPPOSING qualities: punchy landlord with stroking tenant, dabbing doctor with wringing patient.
                - Replay with types switched: slashing teacher with smoothing pupil becomes slashing pupil with smoothing teacher.
                - Choose qualities privately, audience guesses. Interrupt, forbid even nodding, collect guesses, resume and repeat until guesses are right.
            - Variations: Teach together with Fast-Food Stanislavsky. Combine with status work and lists
            - Coaching: Qualities describe body use, not actual bodies: Fatty Arbuckle and Oliver Hardy were heavy men, light performers. Accuracy of guessing matters less than transformation. A wrong guess tells you what to eliminate. Naturally light players must practise heavy and vice versa, an improviser should assume any way of moving
        - Fast-Food Stanislavsky (Purpose Lists)
            - Players: Groups write, 2 to 4 play a scene
            - Setup: Tear paper into long strips, write a purpose atop each (father: to give people a good time, mother: to get sympathy from other people, son: to give people a bad time, daughter-in-law: to be thought intelligent). Divide class into husbands, wives, sons, daughters, each writes about six actions serving its purpose. Model lists live in the chapter text (Good Time, Bad Time, Thought Intelligent, Thought a Hero, Life and Soul of the Party, Thought Normal, To Seduce, Thought Beautiful, Thought Tidy, Not-to-Commit-a-Murder), and Appendix One holds a printed selection of Fast-Food Stanislavsky Lists
            - Goal: Give permission to create alien characters, stop forward thinking, make purpose produce action
            - Rules
                - Trap first: play a death-bed scene (Father dying, Mother nursing, Son arriving with Wife). It comes out solemn and hollow because players try to SHOW sadness.
                - Assign purposes. Knowing purpose alone still bores, the HOW matters.
                - Hand each player their list held like a script. Items are in no order: choose ANY item you can make truthful. Your own ideas are welcome, good ones get added to the list afterwards.
                - Truth rule: a purpose needs resistance and the best resistance is the truth. Do not stride in and tickle the dying man.
                - Coach shouts "Something else from the list!" every five to fifteen seconds to force transitions, beginners cling to item one.
                - Salt safe lists with bold items: hit, tickle, find a zit and panic, tie to bed.
                - Extend: groups write more lists (To be Thought a Saint, To Ruin Someone's Life, To be Stan Laurel, Mae West, Gorilla, Mad Scientist, WASP, John Wayne, To Relate Well to the Audience). Virginia Satir's disturbed-family roles adapt as lists: To be a Computer, To be a Confuser, To Accept Guilt, To Make Guilty.
            - Variations: Party warm-up: a list per guest, spare pile at the side so players swap lists and rejoin as someone new. Family scenes where ALL relatives play the same list, weirdness feels secure because shared. Timid players take Bad Time or Life-of-the-Party lists, anxiety drops because it was on the list
            - Coaching: Lists are another device against thinking ahead. They license rough-and-tumble, mock insults, tickling, self-abuse and confession. They work with status transactions and Fast-Food Laban. When players are truthful it looks like genius direction. Problems: scanners hunting the best item get six-item lists, laugh-getters choosing incongruous items get told to accept only what they can make truthful, one-item stayers have outsiders shout their list at them
        - Having A Purpose Is Not Enough (Shouted Tactics Drill)
            - Players: 1 player plus director, optional second player
            - Setup: Simple aim scene, e.g. character aims to get money
            - Goal: Prove that purpose without tactics is inert, and hand the player massive permission
            - Rules
                - Player states aim, plays politely toward it, stalls on bridging behaviour.
                - Director restarts the scene and shouts tactics continuously: Offer your labour! Steal something! Steal something else! Try blackmail! Offer violence! Beg! Weep! Flatter him! Offer to prostitute yourself!
                - Scene fills with incident and the player suddenly seems talented, because the director owns any transgression.
            - Variations: None needed
            - Coaching: Permission is the mechanism, the shouted instruction absorbs blame
        - To-Seduce Lists
            - Players: Separate men's and women's writing groups, then 1 champion per side plus scene partner
            - Setup: Huddles at opposite sides of the room each write a To-Seduce-a-Person-of-the-Opposite-Sex list. Model lists in chapter text (smile, be vulnerable, move sensually, candlelit dinner versus make her laugh, be athletic, be rich, lock door, offer money)
            - Goal: Strong comic situations from joky instructions
            - Rules
                - Each group chooses its own champion to play the list at a partner.
                - Establish STRONG resistance or you will need buckets of water: the woman must not be thought a whore, the man fears putting her off.
                - Remember the real-world case: the person you seduce may be programmed to give you a bad time.
            - Variations: Feed into Using Combined Lists (seducing one person while giving another a bad time)
            - Coaching: Expect explosions of laughter during writing. Even joky items like lock door and offer money create strong situations
        - Any Purpose For Any Scene
            - Players: 1 or more per scene
            - Setup: Any scene, improvised or scripted (running example: a student murders her professor, Danish fragment "Come in, Nielsen")
            - Goal: Break the belief that analysis of the text yields the purpose, replace cliché playing with character
            - Rules
                - Separate text from purpose: murdering the professor is the text, what happens. The purpose could be anything, even To Be Thought Clean and Tidy.
                - Take a random list and pursue its items INSIDE the required events. The tidy murderer dabs a blood spot from her dress, closes the corpse's eyes, polishes its spectacles.
                - Absurd-seeming pairings work: a Good-Time list on a murder means cheerful greeting through the door, coffee for the boss, endowing him with humour, sweet-in-mouth gun trick, cushion placed under his falling head.
                - Negative purposes work: write a Not-To-Commit-a-Murder list (enter reluctantly, show him the gun, give him a chance to escape). The actor speaks as if words are torn out, sweeps the desk then apologizes, weeps, hands back the gun, wrestles it back because the scenario forces the shot.
                - Written text: apply a list but respect the text, keep only usable items. Of the To-be-Thought-Beautiful list the first eight items fit nearly any text, others adapt (worry about flab via mirror business, exercise on a rowing machine mid-quarrel) and some must drop (name dropping, being interviewed). Demonstration: the Nielsen interview fragment replayed with random list pairs, then again with fresh pairs, each version transforms both actors.
            - Variations: Random draw makes the point fastest
            - Coaching: There is no best purpose and none could be found by analysing text. Actors have rolled on the floor with elation discovering this
        - Using Combined Lists
            - Players: 3, each playing TWO lists aimed at different partners
            - Setup: Proficient students. Sample assignment: Liz gives boyfriend Brian a bad time AND plays a To-Seduce list at Charlie. Brian gives Liz a good time AND plays To-be-Thought-Intelligent at Charlie. Charlie convinces Liz he is normal AND milks sympathy from Brian
            - Goal: Layered behaviour like real social life
            - Rules
                - Each player runs two simultaneous lists, addressing different behaviours to different partners in the same scene.
                - Advanced form: start on one list, then gradually dissolve into the second list across the scene.
            - Variations: Any list pairing that can coexist, not all pairs work
            - Coaching: We already do the dissolve effortlessly whenever meeting strangers and revising our opinion of them
        - Lists As Performance Games
            - Players: 2 or more, optionally an audience volunteer
            - Setup: Prepared lists, Johnstone notes lists are not really suited to public performance but offers two versions
            - Goal: Let an audience enjoy list-driven play
            - Rules
                - Version one: announce each instruction loudly in a detached way BEFORE obeying it. The audience enjoys seeing you controlled by an outside force, and announced items like invade his space or kill something create anticipation.
                - Version two: enlist audience volunteers to read the instructions out, asking them to steer toward some sort of story.
            - Variations: Combine both
            - Coaching: Anticipation is the engine, so announce clearly
        - Bell And Buzzer
            - Players: 1 volunteer controller plus 2 to 4 scene players cast as the volunteer's family
            - Setup: Volunteer holds a bell and a buzzer. Game reached the Loose Moose from Orlando, excellent addition to the Life Game
            - Goal: Shape the scene toward the volunteer's felt truth, strip out pointless blocking
            - Rules
                - Volunteer casts the players as his own family.
                - Players improvise. BELL rings when the playing is on the right track, BUZZER sounds when a guess is wrong.
                - On a buzzer, players revise their course and try again.
            - Variations: Abuse version: groups buzz arbitrary choices just to thwart actors for laughs (I'll get in the car, BZZZT, I won't get in the car). Preferred version reverses it to punish blocking: I won't get in the car, BZZZT, I'll get in the car, RING
            - Coaching: Prefer the anti-blocking use over the thwarting use
        - Scenes With Fingers
            - Players: 3 or more operators, each operating one hand of finger characters
            - Setup: Small theatre with good sight lines, empty floor as alien planet. Invented by Jim Curry via a Frisbee landing
            - Goal: Total absorption (operators are undefended instantly) and a spectacle unlike anything else
            - Rules
                - Walk middle and index fingers about the stage as astronaut explorers, voicing squeaky dialogue for them.
                - Creatures interact: scanner detects alien life form, a gloved-hand monster scrabbles in, phasers on kill, monster leaps on a finger, is shot, flops onto its back motionless.
                - Scale jumps allowed: a third operator walks a nude Barbie doll as a grieving giant whose "poor Glorp" was killed. Both teams creep on, each pair of hands a creature. A gigantic real hand thrusts through curtains and all creatures kneel as if God entered.
            - Variations: Potion turns a player into a giant, companions flee on fingertips in squeaky voices as the slow-motion giant pursues
            - Coaching: Nobody is ever self-conscious about their fingers' acting, so absorption is instant. Do not hide the hands. Finger characters are happy, perky, tiny voiced. Suits only smallish theatres
        - People As Objects
            - Players: Scene players plus any number of object players
            - Setup: Traditional mime game, no props
            - Goal: Generosity mood-setter and living scenery
            - Rules
                - Improvisers become whatever the scene needs: chairs, tables, telephones, a bench to sit on, polar-bear rug, shower.
                - Commit fully and hold: waxworks, statues, mirrors, sofas, footstools, slide-show projections, horses giving piggy-back rides. One player spent an ENTIRE scene as an armchair and caused a sensation.
            - Variations: Challenge phrasing: "We challenge you to the best use of the other team!"
            - Coaching: Too repetitive as a standalone challenge. Always available to spice up other games. No one worries whether their mime is good, offering yourself puts everyone in a good mood
        - Dennis's Puppet Show
            - Players: 2 audience volunteers as puppeteers, 1 backstage helper, 1 jaded-showman host
            - Setup: Puppet theatre of three folding sections with an oblong hole (improvise one over the back of a sofa). Puppeteers MUST be hidden, that is where the fun is. Backstage helper hands puppets, whispers advice, holds the microphone. Created by Dennis Cahill of Loose Moose
            - Goal: Audience delight and rapid de-inhibition of volunteers
            - Rules
                - Host invents a story for glove puppets operated live by the two volunteers.
                - Host plays a showman so jaded and disillusioned that inept, frightened puppets do not matter to the fiction.
                - Backstage feeds puppets and whispered advice throughout.
                - Volunteers typically shed inhibition fast, and spectators reward them eagerly.
            - Variations: Any venue with a concealment line
            - Coaching: Hide the operators so the audience imagines their difficulties
        - Changing The Object
            - Players: Circle of any size
            - Setup: Sit in a circle, one mimed object travels the circle (French mime exercise)
            - Goal: Relaxation, dropping intellectual control, spontaneity under observation
            - Rules
                - Receive the mimed object, use it as offered (lick the ice cream), then CHANGE it into something else before passing (envelope).
                - Keep a blank mind, let ideas float in one ear and out the other. Pre-planning fails: intend mouse, receive mouse.
                - Blank on receipt? HANDLE it: roll gum into a ball, pull pieces, stretch, rotate, weigh, stroke, observe disinterestedly. Stretching may become peeling off a surgical glove. Never force.
                - If it will not alter, pass it on "as is". Johnstone defines the non-changers as the only ones provably honest, reframing failure as success.
                - Bad mime? Announce what it became, humility reads well.
                - Openly chart the anxiety curve: dread peaks as the object approaches, collapses once it passes. Shared fear dissolves in discussion. Note that mime-trained students suffer MOST, tempted toward old material.
            - Variations: CHANGING REAL OBJECTS (Tom Osborn): walking stick becomes umbrella, harpoon or ear cleaner. Shoe pin becomes grenade. Chair hugged to the chest becomes bagpipes. Keep a heap of props stage side: bench becomes canoe, sofa becomes pool, boat up-ended becomes shrine. Audience donates props
            - Coaching: Handle things instead of deciding, they change of their own accord. Your imperfect mime alters objects anyway: the alarm clock shrinks as you wind it, the yo-yo resembles a bouncing ball. Detach ego from success and failure and ideas flood in: fishing rod to lasso, barbell to trapeze
        - Stealing
            - Players: 2 (authority figure plus thief), optional offstage responder as Nurse
            - Setup: Table piled high with junk, NOTHING breakable. Thief wears a huge coat with extra pockets sewn into the lining, or an over-large boiler suit. Optional man-mask or fright wig with false nose. Spin-off of the Making Faces Game, a staple for touring to audiences with no English
            - Goal: Visual lazzi, steal the set unseen
            - Rules
                - Authority runs a consultation (Doctor receiving patient Pat).
                - Doctor looks away on plausible pretexts: fetching the file, turning to consult a book, bending to search the desk.
                - Each look-away lets Pat palm items into the clothing: doll, hot-water bottle, tubing, kettle examined innocently then returned minus the tubing, pen and whistle palmed from under the Doctor's nose.
                - Anything visibly bulging MUST be protested and retrieved by the Doctor while Pat apologizes sincerely and steals yet more, picking the Doctor's pockets.
                - Escalate: boiler suit fattens into a fat suit, umbrella juts from a trouser leg, flag pokes from the collar, stolen goods start re-emerging and get shoved back.
                - Set-piece endings: the Doctor shakes Pat upside down while dozens of objects clatter down, or Pat crams the DOCTOR into the boiler suit (John Breem's ending).
            - Variations: Balloon pop as punctuation, Nurse called to search for the missing mutator
            - Coaching: Non-breakable props only. The pleasure is sincere apology combined with continued theft
        - The Knife Game
            - Players: 2 ideally (couple frame), 1 workable
            - Setup: Mimed knife and knife box. Works as symbol for the concealed self, for sex, or for becoming aliens in sci-fi scenes
            - Goal: Shocking, eerie skinning transformation
            - Rules
                - Handle the mimed knife as if very sharp, perhaps throw it at a wall, then grip it two-handed, blade upward, feigning mental stress.
                - Delay, ram the blade deep into the forehead, scream terrible agony, recover.
                - Seize the hilt and drag the blade down face and torso, kept at right angles to the flesh (careful at the groin), on down one leg, screaming or groaning. Lay the knife on an imaginary surface, breathe easier.
                - Press fingertips into the top of the slit and RIP the edges apart with birthy gasping, groaning, crying, howling. Such sounds release emotion in onlookers.
                - Ease the new distorted face out feature by grotesque feature. Use the freed arm to drag the other from its sleeve, then use that deformed arm to free its twin. Roll the skin down the body with excited, pleasurable sounds, peel it off like tights, hang it on a hook or fold it.
                - Adore the new body: stroke it, gloat in a mirror, leap about with enormous energy. Performance frames: babysitter admits boyfriend with "Did you bring the knives?", child wakes, parents return early, mix-up swapping skins back on. Motel honeymoon: heirloom chrome-steel knives, "Do me first, Peter," forehead stab, skin removed, newly skinned player becomes reptilian, hisses, throws the partner down and slits HIM open.
            - Variants: Room-mate discovered to be an alien. Lover revealed as a snake person mid-slough. Refuser gets skinned forcibly. Lizards smoke a cigarette afterward, room service arrives and gets skinned too.
            - Coaching: Seldom used, SAVE IT FOR SPECIAL OCCASIONS. Done well it is astounding violence, psychotic and intensely sexual. Push sounds, "More sounds! Be more reptilian!"
        - Hand On Knee
            - Players: 3 (offender in the middle, one victim each side)
            - Setup: Three plain chairs with touching edges, pretending to be on a train. Discovered by Roddy Maude-Roxby of the Theatre Machine
            - Goal: Moment-by-moment realism: the struggle to behave normally becomes memorable as well as funny
            - Rules
                - Middle player speaks to one neighbour while placing a hand on the OTHER neighbour's knee, with no awareness of doing it.
                - MANDATORY DRILL first: run the crossed action (talk left, touch right, talk right, touch left) fifteen to twenty times, faster each round. Without it the player touches whoever he addresses or touches nobody.
                - Brief the victims: start low-key, build to paroxysms of rage and embarrassment. The offender never notices, so he experiences himself as the tormented one.
                - Establish the train slowly FIRST: adverts, window, newspaper, mild shyness, let spectators wonder why this tableau is shown.
                - Deliver short remarks across the offending hand, then stare straight ahead so the problem evaporates before the victim decides what to do. Plonk the hand down FIRMLY. Let the victims simmer.
                - Victims play normal people suppressing scandal: inner questions (is he mad, or eccentric, did the others notice), a single gulp, skirt tugged half an inch, an "Excuse me?" answered by "Yes?" that collapses into "Oh, nothing." Do NOT cross legs away, pretend a wall stands beside you. Everyone jogs with the train's motion.
                - Coach escalates: squeeze her knee, make her jump. Squeeze his harder, force him to act. Mid-scene, order the offender NEUTRAL again and study three ruffled people in silence, sustainable for a long beat.
                - If a victim lifts the hand and parks it on the offender's own knee, rewind to the touch: a stranger would certainly notice. Escalate through grabbed wrists, purse beating, standing threats to throw him off the train, then a frozen fuming tableau. Endings NOT built in (options only): ticket collector arrives, genuine horrified realization of guilt, seat-swap rescue where the rescuer clasps HER knee as lights fade.
            - Variations: None recorded beyond ending choices
            - Coaching: Played purely for laughs it is filler and earns no sympathy. Attended moment by moment it lands. Once a year maximum
        - Spasms
            - Players: 2 minimum plus director with clap
            - Setup: Open stage, entrances from opposite sides. Seen as a front-curtain set-change cover at a pantomime, presumed ancient lazzi
            - Goal: Pay-off in the partner's escalating reactions, ending in shared contagion
            - Rules
                - Two strangers cross, double-take, recognize each other, greet effusively, swap information.
                - One player spasms at IRREGULAR intervals with a loud shriek and has NO awareness of doing it.
                - He continues baffled by her reaction, accuses HER of acting strangely. She demonstrates the spasm to explain, and catches it herself.
                - Both now spasm, growing angrier or happier, exiting one pursued by the other through gales of laughter. Technique: the sound must be ELATED, the spasm OPENS the body rather than contracting it, conveying a flash of manic happiness. Grotesque versions excite horror instead. Identical form every single time, beginners let theirs shrink as confidence drains. Contest option: line players up, hand over each head in turn, crowd chooses by applause. Timing drill: trainees may spasm ONLY when the director claps, and the director claps at the worst possible moments (mid-burglary whisper) and pointedly WITHHOLDS claps when both brace for one. Variant: the spasmer starts believing his partner's accusations. Almost never seen, must be forced, improvisers hate losing control
            - Coaching: Regularity kills it. Irregularity plus identical form is the whole trick
        - Paper-Flicking
            - Players: 2 (agent plus destitute applicant)
            - Setup: Desk, notepad, plentiful paper, reserve balls pre-screwed and hidden under the desk papers. Inspired by the Spasm Game
            - Goal: Comic gap between spoken approval and physical abuse
            - Rules
                - High-status agent interviews the desperate applicant, promising fame and fortune, oozing approval, writing down his details.
                - The INSTANT each page is written, rip it out, screw it into a tight little ball, hurl it violently at the applicant's face. Intervals unpredictable.
                - First hit draws rage, interrupt: You need this job! You can't pay the rent! Children to feed! Maybe it's a test!
                - Resume. Hits get ignored, then interactions bloom: Ow! I'm sorry? You threw paper at me! Impossible! The applicant unwraps a ball and gently pushes his phone number back across the desk, weeping, Please take it!
                - Agent alternates insane violence WHILE throwing with total approval BETWEEN throws, and believes himself kind and generous throughout.
                - Screw balls tight, throw so they STING, with a cry of violent rage. Audience wants many balls, so preload. Variant: suitor scene, a woman totally receptive while clubbing him at unpredictable intervals. Victim may attempt joining the joke, hurling a ball back, and gets ruled mentally unstable for an explosives factory.
            - Coaching: The victim must be genuinely desperate for the job. Unpredictability of the throw is everything
